      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0003" n="art0003">
        <p>@1</p>
        <p>Tel. No. 123. ESTABLISMED 60 YEARS (in Warrington). Tel. No. 123. E. Forshaw &amp; Son. Ltd., ENGINEERS, BOILERMAKERS, IRON .1 BRASS FOUNDERS,</p>
        <p>Mersey Boiler Works, Mersey Street, Warrington. Makers of IRON CASTINGS from the smallest size up to 20 tons weight. Clean Well-moulded and of the Highest Quality. To buy the best finished castings is to frequently save 5o per cent, in labour. BRASS CASTINGS (any weight); Gunmetal, Copper, Aluminium, Phosphor Bronze, Damaxlne, Acid Proof Metal and all other Alloys. CONSTRUCTIONAL IRON AND STEEL WORN, Corrugated Iron -Roots and Buildings, Farmers' Hay Sheds, Barns, Store Sheds, &amp;c., Iron and Steel Tanks, Cisterns, Steam Jacketted Pans, Boilers, &amp;c. Repairs by Experienced and Skilled Workmen to all kinds of Nlachinery and to Steam Boilers, Gas Engines, Traction Engines, Motor Cars CA Dynamos, Steam Engines, 011 Engines, Road Rollers, Motor Waggons, Electric Motors. ELECTRICAL PINGINLERING Speciality Work. Drawings and expert advice submitted free. Heavy Machining and Forging done for the trade. Turning up to 13 feet Canister. Placing up to 5 feet square. Slotting up to 18-inch stroke. Milling, Punching, She; rng and Bending, Press•ing, Dishing, Flanging and Rivetting by hydraulic pressure. Unlimited Tackle and Special Appliances for efficiently dealing with Repairs and fireakcbwn work, armimulated during am millennial* over 60 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0004" n="art0004">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD ON NATIONAL INSURANCE.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD ON NATIONAL INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>SPEECH AT DRUIDS' GATHERING.</p>
        <p>Presentations to Offlcers.</p>
        <p>' High officers of the Order of Druids and members of the Druids Hope Lodge, assembled in Atkinson's Cafe on Monday evening to enjoy a tea and social evening. This event took place in honour of the services of several members of the lodge and the occasion was recognised as a grand opportunity for handing handsome presents to them—P.D.G.M. Bro William Bibby for 35 years service as secretary, PG.G.M. Bro William Woods, long service as steward, and D.C. S Bro Allen and P.D.G.M. Bro John Bibby, officers of the board. Grand Master Bro A Billington (Crewe), presided and those present were :-13ro A H Crosfield, Councillor James Locker. Bro S N Green, Bros .1 R Copping, V. 0.11, J W Shaw, Grand Secretary, J Prentice, R Kirk. J Lavin, G B Tait, members of the Board, 0 T Rushton, D.G.M., T Derbyshire, D.D.G.M., R Perdue, D.T., E Allen, D.C.S., P.D.G.M.S. Mulkey, P.D.G.M. J Ray, P.P.G.M J. J Wilcock, P.P.0.51.0 Bebbington, P.D.G.M. S Packer, P.D.G.M. H Kent, P.D.G.M. J Jackson, P D.G.M. E Pointon, PD G.M. J Massey, P.D.G.M. John Bibby, P.D.G.M. J Woods, P.A. Robert Lees, secretary of the Widow and Orphans Fund, PA. It Cook, PAT. Hewitt, N.G.A. Wm Bibby, jun., PA. A Podmore, Lodge Treasurer, P.A. A Dickens and PAIL. Bibby, Doctors John Martin and F H Gandhi. aAn escellenetes was served, the catering of Atkinson's Cafes Ltd., giving the utmost satisfaction. In the early stages of the evening Mr. A. 11. Croslleld was initiated as an hon, member of the Order. After the toast of the King and Queen had been honoured, the CHAIRMAN said his pleasure at being there was enhanced by the fact that they had been enabled to initiate lir). Arthur Crosileld. Ile believed that in doing that they had initiated one of the right stamp and character. During tea he (the speaker) had the privilege of conversing with Mr. Croslield and he was of opinion that he had given considerable thought and attention to that which was most likely to conduce to the well-being of the particular district in which be way located. Ile trusted that his membership would be productive of much good. (Applause.) They were there primarily for the purpose of recogizing the services of onset their brethren (Bro. Bibb3) who bad worked in their midst for 85 years. In recognising such a lifetime of rervice he could quite understand the pleasure it gave them is offering to Bro. Bibby the good will which they cherished in their hearts towards him. (Applause. They were also present to recognise the services of Bro. Allen and Bro. Bibby, who were members of the Board of Management and Junior Auditor respectively. There was also Bro. Woods who was an old:and respected member of the society. In regard to all of them they had met to do honour to whom honour was due. (Applause.) Continuing the speaker said that the great topic which should occupy their consideration was the ' great scheme of the Government for the well being of the masses. They did not want to be too severe with the Chancellor of the Exchequer and in order to get the best out of the measure they ought to approach it with out any party strife or political rancour. (Hear. bear.) They should consider the proposals in the National Insurance Bill as being too sacred to offer any flippant opposition. (Hear, hear). They ought to remember the etate) in which Mr. I.loyd George found the country. More than ICO years ego the gre it bark of the Friendly Society movement was launched. But notwithstanding the fact that the bark had been sailing along time, Mr. George was face to face with the circumstance that outside Friendly Societies there were between eight and nine million people. The Chancellor set him- self to solve the problem as it presented itself to him and he had three classes of individuals to take into consideration the man who had been thrifty and had tried to make what provision he could; the man who cared nothing auto what his duty in life was or of his liability to all that" flesh was heir to" in the shape of ailments; and 3,the man who was heir to complaints and who was so dogged by misfortune that he had never been able to make any provision. In hia great proposals the Chancellor of the Exchequer had TAKEN INTO ACCOUNT THIC ILAN WHO HAD MADE</p>
        <p>PROV 'SION,</p>
        <p>the man who belonged ton friendly society. He bad also taken into account the man who did not care ansthing about his future and he had so safeguarded to the full the man of integrity end honour. The reason Mr. Lloyd George had done that was because he recognized it as an incumbent duty that man should provide for the future., He had safeguarded his proposals as far as he could So that they should suit all dances of society. He (the speaker) asked them to take those proposals as they found them, and graft into thorn and round about them in that true spirit of brotherhood which had counted for co much good in the history of cur great nation. In conclusion he appealed to them to do all they could to further the interests of the fraendly societies which they belonged to. (Applause). Bro. G. F. Ritswro's proposed the toast of "The Order of Druids," end coupled it with the name of Bro. Copping. Ile said he concurred with the remarks of the Chairmen with respect to the Insurance Bill. It was the duty of every member of a friendly society, not only of their society but of all the societies an the country, to take this question to heart sod study it for themselves. But while admitting that they believed in the principles of the Bill, there were certain alterations that should be made in the interests of all members of friendly societies. He felt that they were on the point of a greater success lathe Order than it had hitherto been blessed with. So long as:this great question was at stake he hoped that they would always remember the word "charge" and forget the word 'halt" in their endeavour to obtain those benetias which (be Order had intended to give them, although he believed that Mr. Lloyd George had not lacked in his good feelings towards the friendly societies when be introduced the State Insurance Bill. (Applause.) The toast was honoured with enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>roriTarTin—gion, replied. lie said they did not profEsa to be anything like the largest or wealthiest society in the United Kingdom, but they claimed to have done a proportionate amount of good to any other society in existence. (Hear, hear.) The preaent e as the most critical time that friendly societies had to pass through. They were proud of their achievements in the past, but they were now looking with arma fear and trepidation vs to what the</p>
        <p>future of their societies was going to be. (Hest., beer.) He wished to associate himself with the remarks of their Worthy Grand Master as to the intentions to the Chancellor of the Exchequer in bringing forward his great National Insurance Scheme. Mr. Lloyd George admitted when he intro- duced the Bill that it was not wit hoot its imperfections. He also recognised the friendly societies and went so far as to say that it would he a crime to do anything that would impair the usefulness of friendly societies. He fthe speaker) thought that the imperfections of the Bill had been intensified by the amendments passed since its introduction. Friendly societies were now in such a position that they had got to fight for their very existence. There were other interests at stake also and he believed that thews other interests had received more attention than their's. At the National Conference of Friendly Societies they were told that _ _ .</p>
        <p>;;ZEWI.O.is.:THAD BEEN MADE TO OTHER ASSOCIATIONS interested in the working of the Bill, to the prejudice of friendly societies. Those other associations had gone to the Chancellor of the Exchequer and said "These are our demands. If you grant the concessions we ask for we are prepared to assist you in the carrying out of ,sour great Act." And to a largo extent their demands had been conceded. But friendly societies had not been in that position •, they had not had the some definiteness about them. That: was cerionsly token into con- sideration at the Conference held last week, and it was decided that the time had arrived when they must fight for their very existence. The number of amend- ments they wanted to be carried had been boiled down to 12, and they were the irreducible minimum. He was not one of those who were goinu to take the 13111 es it was, as some wanted, and then have the imperfections remedied afterwards. If there were imperfections in the Bill relating to friendly societies it was their bounden duty to their members to have those imper- fections corrected before the Bill became law and not. trust to having them puteright afterwards. (Applause). From his observatioss at the National Conference he was convinced that the great societies bad no more! desire to wreck the Bill than they had as an Order of Druids. They were out to amend the Bill and if the Chancellor of the Exchequer would not make the Con- cessions no as to make it reatonably clear for them to carry on t eir societies in the best interesis of their members, then they would consider whethsr they would take part in the administration of the Act or not. The concessions which had beea made to other societies had keen made by the deter- mined stand they had taken. Friendly societies were simply asking for what was just and he hoped that when the Chancellor of the Exchequer came to consider their demands he would be prepared to meet them in a fair and honourable way but if he did not he hoped that all friendly societies would be unanimous as a body and unanimous on the course they would take after that. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>PRESENTATIONS.</head>
        <p>THE PRESENTATIONS.</p>
        <p>The presentations then took place. They were as follows :—Bro. W. Bibby, mahogany sideboard and handsome cabinet of cutlery ; Bro. W. Woods, purse of gold ; Bro. Allan, gold ringl; and Bro. John Bibby, gild medallion of the order. Bro. R. Cook made the presentations. Before doing so speeches eulogising the respective recipients were made by Bros J Massey and R Hewitt. Bro COOK said they were going to give Bro Bibby what he richly merited. He remembered the tirre when he succeeded to the secretaryship and he had held the office with credit to himself and to the lodge. (Applause.) He did not think the Medical Association would have been in such a prosperous position es it was now but for the efforts of Bro Bibby. (Applause.) With regard to Bro Woods there was no more painstaking or hard working roan in the Lodge or even in the whole Order. (Applause.) They all knew the merits of Bro. Allen and of Mr John Bibby he might say he was a worthy son of a worthy sire. (A Voice : A chip of the old block). (Applause.) In thanking the lodge for their handsome present, Bro. W. BIBBY said thirty-five years ago last month The was appointed secretary of that lodge. Out of the members who conferred that great honour upon him only 15 were left with them, nearly all the remainder having joined the Great Majority. During the whole of that time, on no single occasion had anything taken place that had Nosed the slightest discord between the Lodge and himself. The time appeared to him to be short because it had been so pleusant.lt would perhaps be not altogether out of place:if he gave them a few facts abut Druids Hope Lodge. In the year 1813 George Frederick Podmore, one of the most respected meals the town, be3ame very much dissatistiei with the manner his Druids' Lodge conducted their !matinees, it was more like a spree than anything else, lie could not get the Lodge to alter its ways, so he set to work and obtained some young men one of whom was with them day—Bro. William Barlow—although it wan 67 years ago) to form a new Ledge at which</p>
        <p>NO MEETING SHOULD EVER BE HELD IN ANY ÜBLIC •HOUSE</p>
        <p>or beer-bouse. This -v;s—the.-11-ra resolution passed which had been consented to by every member since. "That any member proposing this Lodge to be removed to a public-house, beet-house, or any other such place shill immediately be expelled the Lodge and not allowed to jdn again only as a new member." That did not mean that all members were teetotallers but simply that the business of the Lodge should be con- ducted outside a public-house or beer-house. Since 1641 the Lodge had only had three treasurers, the founder, his son and his grandson. The late Bro. Joseph Podmore held the treasurership for nearly 40 years. The Lodge had only had three secretaries, James Craik, Thomas Aspey and himself. It was only to ba expected that a Lodge founded by such men must prosper, so mu,;h so that some years liter they founded a scheme that when compared with the present laser- sure Bill put the Bill to shame as a most extravagant measure. The financial position of the Lodge to-day was something to be proud of. They were worth a sum equal to £6 54. per member in their Management Fund alone. As far as be knew that was a record for tteir Order. They had not accumulated that capital by being in any ways mean, no member ever appealed for assistance in vain. Testimonials had also been given to various' ofidaera the most recent being Bros. Barlow and Podmore. Four years after receiving this secretaryship and perhaps through holding it, he was appointed secretary of the Family Funeral Fund, also the Amalgamated Friendly Society Medical Association and both positions he still retained. Some years later he was appointed senior trustee of the district. That meeting being composed of representatives of every lodge in the district, together with the grand °Mears of the Order and members of the b. ard of management was most pleasing to biro, as be took it to be a reward for the beat portion of a life spent in the interests of the Order of Druids. Ile again thanked them for their splendid present and hoped that he would still be of service for some years to come. (Applause.) • Bro. Wooris remarked that he joined the Order in 1864 and had been a steward under three secretaries and three treasurers.</p>
        <p>Bro. ALLEN thanked them for their present and mid that sometirces when presentations were made there</p>
        <p>From Reynolds' Newgpaper.</p>
        <p>was the innuendo that they'should retire. He did.not think that applied to him, for he had recently been made a vice-grandmaster of the Order. (Applatme.) Nothing would please him more than to continue the good work in which he end they were engaged for the well-being of workingmen. Bro. JOHN 131noth said it hod kern bis ambition for some time to ohttin the sold medallion of the Order of Druids and be was gird he hod now got it. He intended to keep on working in the interests of Friendly Societies, for they were productive of much good to the people at large. (Applause). The toast of "The Honorary Member," was pro- posed by Bro. John Prentice of Leeds. He said it wee pleasing to see a gentleman of Mr. Crosflelds' stamp arsociating himself with the workers of Warrington. The only thing that he wan sorry about that night was that they could net get Mr. Crotliold an a member of Parliament pledged to support the amendments which I had been proposed by the National Friendly Society Conference. (Loughter and applause). They would have to look forward to the day when they should be able to rely upon his Parliamentary support. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>MR. CIROSFIRLD AND:IIII3.I.NSURANCE</p>
        <p>.. -- In replying to the toast, Mr A. H. CROSPIELD offered his heartfelt and sincere thanta for the extraordinary kindoese they had shown him in inviting him to join the Order, and Rho for tbe reception they had given him. It was not the first friendly society he had had the honour to belong to. For many yearn—be wee afraid . . .</p>
        <p>to say bow many— the work of friendl admiration for wh, organiEations we I</p>
        <p>ae had talren a eery deep interest in ly societies, and he had an unfeigned it were amongst the most splendid Lad in old England. He felt that the</p>
        <p>Photo Birtles. BRO. WM. BIBBY, P.D.G.M.</p>
        <p>wise forethought and all that those societies taught was a great thing for the country. They taught the young man forethought and discipline; they gave him a little insight into business methods, and they even gave him some little chance of practising oratory— (laughter)—and certainly they had had evidence that night of that remarkable faculty possessed by members of that ancient Order, and it made him feel quite a trepidation in addressing them for the first time. He could not say with that truthfulness which always distinguished politicians, that he was altogether unaccustomed to sneaking, but he was making a maiden speech before an Order of Druids' meeting. Ito felt particularly happy in being eleotei to that Order on such an interesting evening. They had been honoured by the presence of many distinguished officers and he admired with all his heart the diligence with which the officers of the great friendly societies carried out their work. He was also glad to be there at a very striking time in the history of friendly societies. It was much too late for him to go into any detail and what was more he was not going to skate on any thin ion —(laughter)—but he hoped he should be in order in saying he happened to be</p>
        <p>PERZSALT.N.'-dCtICAINTED WITII MR. I.T.OYD•GRORGR. (Hear, hear.) He knew the real democratic sympathy which he posumelvd and he knew the courage he had in facing great difficulties. Speaking only for himself, he (Mr. email,.ld) never looked upon the present great Insurance Bill as a measure of finality. He did not think it would be possible in these complevldays for any man—even if an archangel came down to help them—to introduce a measure which would be of finality. He also recognized that there were diplomatic interchangea of opinion going on at tha present time—and most rightly an. Of course, it WR3 for those societies—in which be was as ksenly interested as any mmn in England—to argue their case and make the bast terms that were possible for their own welfare and for the welfafe of the country. As opinions on the Bill hid been expressed, he would nay that to his mind it would be nothing leas than</p>
        <p>A NATIONAL DISASTER TO Ol'^ ^OMMENITY if amen ure, whirh in no many different wa3s was, going to be a far-two:ring blessing to the whole na•dara were not after due discussion, due care, and dun labour placed upon ths Statute Basks. He was very glad to hear whilst earning dews from London, that whilst the friendly societies were determined to thrash out every question in which they were Interested, with a view to the benelltting of their Order, they were not doing it is any carping anirit with the intention of wrecking the Bill. (Hear, bear). He wes else glad to he there becauie old fellow townsmen were being honoured and els) because it was fine to think that friendly societies recognised the services of those eh" bid woe good and honoured positions by the I valuable work they had rendered among those societies I They had bad a short speech from Bro. Woods end they I knew full well the good work he win doing. (Hear, "ear). He was quite stare that the welcome they gave , Bro. Allen was one that he was not going to forget— they gave Bro. Allen tha good opinion of thos3 whose opinion it was worth having. (Applause). And then there was his old friend Bro. Bibby. He did not know how long they had not known one another. He had known of his value' work amongst friendly societies and of the admirable work be had done in other isfluential positions he had filled. He (the speaker) was going to call Boa Bibby an old man frill of honour and of years. He saw the honour but did not Fee the years. (Laughter and applause.) He heard that he wee a grondfather. He thought that moat be delightful, though he was not yet one bimsel (Laughter.; It must he especially delightful 10 be</p>
        <p>grandfather when he did tot look it. (More laughter.) As to Mr John Bibby lie was following in his father's footsteps. Ile also believed that he witn following in the footateps of his grandfather, and of his great grandfather. fie was glad of that, for it did not alvrays happen that the son and the grandson carried out the traditions of the father, and the grandfather. lie was glad they had combined a little bit of art with their gathering; for in these difficult days with Romany grave problems confronting them,not the least encourag- ing sign in old England wits the spread of an intelligent appreciation of music. (Hear, hear.) Professor Huxley had said that the moat enduring form of refine- ment was literature. He was not there to dispute that, but even literature had a stonily rival in music. And the splendid development we had in England and happily not least in our own town—(applause) —of cctirse he was very proud of the success the Perfectio Band had the other day—(applause)—he honestly thought was one of the many signs to give them reason to face the future with hope and (encouragement. And he believed that largely with the assistance of societies like theirs the progress of our old nation would still continue. (Applause). Councillor I.oe gen said he had been an honorary member of that Order for some ten or eleven years , And nothing had given him greeter pleesura than to see Mr. Crosdeld initiated that evening. He was sure that when he left that room he would think of the beautiful , words read to him. They were words which were really fine for a body of workinguirn, or, if you will, they were good enough for the King of England, The ceremony impressed him very much and he knew such words were something to make them better men than before. If a man only left that room and lived op to what he was asked to, then be would live a life which NOBS a pleasure to himself and of admiration on the part of those around him. "THE DRUIDS' HOPE LODGE."</p>
        <p>Bro. H. J. MAINLICY proposed the toad of the " Druids' Hope Lodge "sad did that lodge contained some of the best poesible members that a lodge could have. Ile hoped that evening would act as an incentive to the young members to lo as the motto told them "To leave the ohl world better than they found it." Bro. A. POD,MoRE replied to the toast and said that would remain as a memorable occasion not only for the fact of the presentations that had taken place but , because that they had with them so many prominent officers. The Druids' Hope Lodge had that yeer attained its 670 ;rear and he could say that they were without doubt the richt at lodge in Warting-mn. This was due to the good work of all the officers who had worked assiduously for it. progress. A vote of thanks was passed to the chairman and artistes on the proposition of Bro. T. Derbyshire. The musical items gave much delight to the gather- ing. The artistes were :—Messrs. Booth, James Ray, L. Auklend and J. Draper. Mr. George Lomas proved on efficient accompanist.</p>
