      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>TOWN -BEAUTIFUL.</head>
        <p>THE TOWN -BEAUTIFUL.</p>
        <p>A WARKING TO INVESTORS.</p>
        <p>Lecture at the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>The history of town planning and lessons to he drawn from it were the central ideas of the lint of a series of illustrated lectures entitled " Town Planning in Practice," delivered by Mr. Henry it. Aldridge, on luesday evening in the Parr Hall. The Mayor (Coun- cillor Dr. Joseph) presided over a Sparta but repre- sentative attendance, which mainly consisted of those interested in the movement, i.e., members of the Town Conned, ceighbonring Urban and Rural Councils, land- ownere, builders &amp;,. Supporting the Mayor on the platform were Aldermen Bennett, Councillor Birtles and Conncillor Bellian. The MAYOR said the lecturer had been for some con- siderable time prominent in the Town Planning move- ment. He had also organized several excursions tn the Continent with the object of imparting knowledge to those who accompanied him. He had in a great measure been instrumental in instituting the Housing and Town Planning Act. (Applause). The subject of the lectures was one which should concern everybody— landowners, householders and builders. It affected the health, convenience and beauty of the district in %filch they lived. The Act was intended by nleans of schemes to develop land in the vicinity of towns in such a way as to secure proper genitory conditions. amenity and convenience in the laying out of land. It was concerned not only with the cure of, but the pre- vention of alums, and under any scheme that was brought forward the first interest should be the health of the people. (Applause) In former times there had been no control over schemes which were brought out for building purposes and the laying cut of towns. Local authorities had bad on control over such schemes which were left entirely to private interests and private interests proved much stronger than the health, enjoy- ment and pleasrnt life of the people. (Hear, hear.) The Act was intended to do away with that and local mithorities had now the power with regard to the laying out of towns. He believed that if it had bean in operation fifty years ago... _</p>
        <p>WARRINOToN- S'OI:LD-- HAVE BERN SAVED A GREAT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>DEAL OF MONEY,</head>
        <p>DEAL OF MONEY,</p>
        <p>(Applause.) During the last twenty-two years the improvement of the streets in Warrington, not to mention the provision of plessure and recreation grounds, bowling greens, and open spaces, bad cost over a quarter of a million pounds. We were suffering from the errors of the past and we had bad left us a disastrous legacy in the matter of health and expendi- ture owing to the want of wisdom on the part of our forefathers. We ought not to perpetuate those disasters but get as much information as we could and act accordingly. Mr. ALDRIDar., who lathe secretary of the National Housing and Town Planning Committee, then began his most interesting and lucid lecture. Ile said the object of the lectures was to place before them several definite points. Ile would give a brief summary of the past history of the town planning movement and completely explode the idea that it was a modern fad. He would give them a description of modern town planning achievements in other countries and also in our own. He would also summarise the modern arguments from the points of public health and municipal economy. The fact that in 20 years street improvements had cost Warrington a quarter of a million pounds, was only one of many instances in the United Kingdom. Then finally he hoped to outline the actual steps to be taken in order to administer the Housing and Town PI inning Act. He bad no hesitation whatever in strongly urging us to use the powers of the new Act for the reason that if disorder had to give place to order, if a hind of chaotic madness had to give place to comroonsense and thought- ful foresight, then surely the citizens of a town were right in endeavouring to exercise their power in the planning of a town In which thousands and tens of thousands yet unborn would have to spend their lives and earn their daily bread. (Applause.) The ancient character of town planning would be made quite clear when he told them that the first slide which would be thrown on the screen would show a piece of tows planning 5,000 years old—the planning of a little village to accommodate the workmen engaged in the coostruction of a pyramid in ancient Egypt. But, roughly speaking, town planning began 500 years before Christ, in a period of Greek greatness. Athens was then a kind of squalid collection of poor houses in mean streets and the Athenian going to dinner would go along a main street in which refuse Was thrown and at night Is wonld walk through the mud by the aid of a torchlight. With the coming of the sense of greatness the Greeks began to torn their attention to the building of their cities and from that time we had the beginning of probably some of the finest examples of statuary and architecture. At the same time we had the beginning of Greek culture and philosophy. As it was now, in those ancient cities it was MOOT DIEFICULT TO RE-MODEL THE ANCIENr TOWNS, so they needed to go to the outposts to see the floe examples of town planning. Having described the planning of Lancashire as an inartistic higgledy- piggledy model, the Lecturer said that when the great London fire almost swept the city away we had a chance to town plan, but we lost it. And he almost wished that we could have a very big fire in some of our cities—providing there were no danger to , life—and then we should have a chance.</p>
        <p>EDINBURGH WAS ONE OF THE MOST BEAUTIFUL CITIES</p>
        <p>in the world; but it wan ;;;;Tecuidentally so. It was the result of planning in 176 d and the great Princes- street belonged to that time. Edinburgh more than any other city except London had succeeded in keeping at the centre of the city a cultured and rich class of men. The spacious squares sod crescents at the centre !of Edinburgh had beautiful houses and there WAS PO ' reason for rich and cultured people to go to the out- skirts because the houses were rather inferior to those inside. By those rich men remaining in Edieburgh the rates were greatly assisted. He dared say thst in Warrington there was a complaint that the richer men lived outside of the town; but if 100 ! years ago we had bud a Town Council determined to use every beauty spot in the Borough and had it properly planned we might have had our rich citizens living in the centre of Warrington.. There was no reason why rich men should not live in the centre for in our towns in England we must not have any F.ast or West end. Tlse tendency of Germany to use blood and iron methods was not good enough for us and we nut; ! have no gulf between rich and poor. The men of wealth and culture nowt be willing to help their poorer brethren Rliai if they ware not willing they haid not got true culture. The lecturer graphically pictured solo* , of the towns on the continent where town planning had advanced and in advocating town planning in England he said it would be well for the landowners and builders to take notice of a word of warping. The workmen of this c inntry really had the desire for something better than they now hail and it was only a msttor of time when the workmen would wish to have a garden attached a better house. He would not be satisfied with the</p>
        <p>BRICK BOX WITH 'MB SL tTB</p>
        <p>(Laughter and applause.) Those who built boutes for investment should realise that if they built houses which the men of the future did not want because they desired something better then their houses would lose their yeloe. In conclusion the Lecturer said those who</p>
        <p>HOUSE FURNISHING.</p>
        <p>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS.</p>
        <p>BELFAST LOYALTY.</p>
        <p>The magnificent contribution of the Nationalists of Belfast to the Irish Parlia- mentary Party Fund is worthy of their record as practical supporters of Home Rule. In the cheque for £550 forwarded from the National Club there is a reminder that, in what has too long been regarded in England as the impregnable stronghold of Carsonites ' and disloyal "loyalists," there is a strong and steady organisation of workingmen in the Northern capital whose adherence to the demand for self-government is as firm and unwavering as that evinced in the most exclusively National part of the whole country. Before the "hot air" of the Craigavon demonstratim has had time to cool, the public of Great Britain—and it is for them that this message from Belfast has the greater significance—are informed that in the most prosperous part of Ulster the cause of Ireland and the Irish party finds its most generous supporters. As a political fact this contribution is of more moment than all the braggadocio of talk of Provisional Governments and marches upon Cork. It signifies the adhesion of one third of the population of Belfast to the National ideal and denotes a spirit that can only be strengthened by "the foolish threats of English Tory lawyers coming over to create strife in Ulster." Out of their fight in the local arena against oppression and bigotry the Nationalists of Belfast have emerged as the most helpful champions of the National cause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>CARSON'S CLAIMS.</head>
        <p>CARSON'S CLAIMS.</p>
        <p>Two debates in Manchester, one in the County Forum and the other in the Man- chester Athenum, where Mr. J. W. Dulanty stated the case for Irish self-government against two well paid Tory barristers, show with a remarkable vividness that Ulster is to be played for all it is worth,or rather we may say for far more than it will be worth to the Unionists. It was on both occasions abundantly clear that the preposterous claim 1 that Sir Edward Carson and Messrs. Campbell and Moore have authority to speak for the people of Ulster will have to be met with the crushing testimony of fact. Again and again, 1 friends of Home Rule will find it imperatively necessary to reiterate that this authority is derived only from the North-east corner; that the Parliamentary representation of Ulster is equally divided between Nationalists and Unionists, that in Belfast itself a third of the population is passionately attached to Home Rule and the lead of Mr. John Redmond. The arithmetic of politics is eminently simple and is not to be overborne by the swaggering threats as to the action to be taken by the Ulster masses. LORD PIRRIE'S BOMBSHELL.</p>
        <p>Lord Pirrie, as we showed last week in this column, has helped in a valuable measure to dissipate the myth exploited on Unionist platforms. He now goes further and shews that the gentlemen who glibly denounce boy- cotting are themselves past masters at the game. Lord Pirrie as a spokesman for Belfast and Ulster is rather more impressive than Sir Edward Carson or Mr. Campbell or a retired militia captain. He has achieved fame and fortune in the province. His name is associated with an industrial enterprise i which s one of the staple industries of Belfast's prosperity, and is known all over the world. If it were true that an Irish Parliament would jeopardise the wealth and liberties of the population he assuredly, of all men in Ireland, would not be among its opponents. His courageous declaration has maddened the strike-mongers—first, because it has so prominently identified him with the national demands, and next because it was destructive to the contention that all the "wealth and intelligence" of Belfast are at the service of Unionism. With one excep- tion, he pointed out, none of the persons who participated in the demonstration at Craig- avon, of which so much has been made, could claim any solid interest in the industrial or commercial welfare of Belfast. Further, he indicated that in the city there is a growth of the feeling he entertains so strongly—that Catholics and Protestants could in national affairs work together in harmony as they do in local councils. But, of course, Lord Perrie's greatest offending, in the opinion of the Unionists, is his complete exposure of their claim that they are the accredited custodians of the industrial and commercial welfare of Ulster. A LIE THAT LIVES.</p>
        <p>The Unionists are so persistent in their allegations about Home Rule meaning separation that they fix on the slightest pre- text for this outrageous theory. And a</p>
        <p>favourite device of theirs is to tell English audiences that Parnell in a speech in America had declared that he would never be satisfied until Ireland had "broken the last link with England." Over and over again has this passage been denounced as a calumny by the Nationalist leaders, and Parnell himself with circumstances of the greatest publicity—namely before the Parnell Commission trial—denied on oath ever using such words. Still the lie lives on and therefore it may be well to recall to English and Irish minds the debate on the second reading of the Home Rule Bill on May 25th, 1886. On that occasion Sir George (then Mr.) Trevelyan quoted the "last link" passage, Parnell at once interrupted and said (we quote from Hansard).</p>
        <p>"This is not the first time that this- calumny has been uttered against me in this House, and as I contradicted the statement the first time it was ever made some two or three years ago in this House, I think I am entitled to ask the authority for his quotation, the date of the speech, and the place where I am Illleged to have made it."</p>
        <p>Mr. Trevelyan thereupon withdrew the entire statement and Parnell offered to send him next day copies of all the speeches be had made in America and assured him that "na such words, nor any one of the words quoted" would be found in them. Great indeed must be the poverty of argument on the Unionist side when they are compelled to trade on such baseless fictions and false- hoods as the one we call attention to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>ODDFELLOWSHIr. Olive Branch Lodge, Warrington District (MU.)</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWSHIr. Olive Branch Lodge, Warrington District (MU.)</p>
        <p>-- Os Saturday evening last a social and distribution of bowling prizes took place at the above Lodge. There was also a presentation of a framed Past Officer's certificate of merit to P.O. William A. Storey, who has recently completed his term of office in the Lodge. Invitations had been sent oat to the district officers, who kindly accepted the same, and they received • very hearty welcome from the Brethren present The meet- ing was presided over by the Noble Grand, Bro. George 1 Brown and amongst those present were :—Prov. G.M. Peter Lester,Deputy G.M. Herbert Whitelegge,P.P.G.M. F Webster (District Secretary), N.G. George Brown, Lodge G M. Albert Butterworth, Minute Secretary John Brown, P.G. A. Cotterill (treasorer), P.G. %%Wiens Hrughton, P.P.G.hf. Alban Boyle (secretary), and the• visiting brethren included P.P.G.NI. Thomas P. Brad- shaw, P.P.0.111. Lot Brooks, P.P.G.M. Isiorbory, P.P.G.kI. !William Holbrook, P.G. Walter Dennett end' others. At the conclusion of the ordinary business of the Lodge, Deputy G.M. WHITELY:O6K having bees requested to present the Certificate of Merit to P.G. Storey, in the course of his remarks said that the recipient of that presentation ought to feel it a great honour to receive arch recognition from the Brethren of the Lodge. It showed that he bad carried out his , duties satisfactorily. A certificate of that kind could not be bought ; it bad to be earned by arduous work,. and he hoped that younger marchers would imitate the example net and strive to earn similar recognition by , the Lodge. He had great pleasure in • king P.O. Storey's acceptance of the certificate, and hoped he I would hang it in a prominent place In his home, and I that it would remind him of the work he had done on 1 behalf of the Olive Branch Lodge, and no spur him on to further efforts in the good work they were all engaged in doing both for the Lodge nod District. (Applause). P.G. STOREY in reply said that he wale glad to know that be had been successful in doing his duty towards his lodge and also that he had given satisfaction to the brethren. He bad gained a lot of experience in the work of the Lodge and hoped to make practical use of it. It had been a very interesting period during his term of office and he considered its groat honour to he presented with a certificate of merit and thanked the officers and brethren very heartily for their kindness towards him. (Applanae.) The DISTRICT GRAND MASTER (Bro. Lester) then presented the prizes won at the bowling main which wan played a short time ago at Halton Cantle and in doing no said he was pleased to perform the duty and hoped that the holding of a bowling main in 000DGC:i00 eith the Lodge would tend to draw young men to the Lodge and so increase their membership. The young members especially could do a lot of good in this respect by trying to get their companions to join and by so doing would greatly assist in making the Oliva Branch Lodge prosperous. (Applause.) The winners of the prizes were P.P.0.N1„ Forbes, P.G. Hy Walker, P.G. Juo Boyle, sen.,P.G. A Cotterill, Bro. W Lilly, P.O. A E Butterworth, Bro. Jan Boyle. Jun., Bro. Faulkner, Bro. Geo Savage and Bro. A Bentley. The social part of the programme, during which some capital songs were given in god style, eas well sustained by the following brethren and friends : P.P.G.hf. Lot Brooks, P.G. Walter Dennett, P.G. A Cotterill, P.G. T Speed, PG Tom Throssell and Messrs Gough and S Oddy. Mr. Alfred Grounds proved a very efficient pianist. After a vote of thanks to the donors of the prizes and others who had given their assistance a very pleasant evening was brought to a close by the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>Late Mr. F. G. Taylors Bequest to the</head>
