      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Great Singer'e Farewell.—Madatue Albani, the great Soprano, who bade her adieu to the London public at tho Albert !fall last Saturday, was born in Canada in 1852, of French parentage, and made her first appearance in public when eight years old. She subse- quently studied in France and Italy, and made her debut in opera in 1870, soon gaining a great reputation both in opera and oratorio. Some eight years later she married Mr. Ernest ('ye, director of Italian Opera, Covent Garden. Over ten thonsani people were present at the Albert Hall tart week, when Madame Albani made her final appearance in public in London, she having previously received from the Queen a letter of good wishes on her retirement. That was her adieu to the metropolis, but Madame Albani will yet give several farewell concerts in the provinces, at Manchester, Liverpool, Birmingham, and possibly other pieces. Madame Alban' is undertaking the training of several pupils, and it is possibly on account of that demand upon her time that she is retiring from singing in public, but, speaking of the future of the c3ncert stage, Madame Albani does not seem to think that modern compositions, or the set of the vocalist equal the highest standards of the past. Autumn Weddings.—The Coronation year of 1011, has been noted for the breaking off of several society engagements, but many have puma to a happy ending, one of the most important being the alliance this week betweea the tw3 ducal houses of Northumberland and Richmond and Gordon. Both Lord Percy and Lady Helen Gordon-Lennox have had an enormous number of valueble wedding gifts bestovired epee them. The bride is tall, fair and slender, and is generally considered one of the prettiest and most popular girls in society. Another event of interest to a great number of distin- guished people, will be the wedding of Lord Leconfleld and Miss Violet Rawson, which will also take place in London, at St. Margerets, Westminster, on November Bth. The autumn will further see two weddings in the Grosvenor family, those of Lady Helen thosvenor, and of her half-brother Lord Henry Grosvenor. The former who is a girl of 23,1 s engaged to Sir Hill Child, and Lord Henry, who has been a widower for many years, is to marry Mrs. Edward Greavcs. The late Doke of Westminster, who was twice married, was the father of fifteen children, Lord Henry being the third son, and Lady Helen the youngest daughter. •She is aunt to the present Duke, although some years his junior in age. On Petticoats.—The strike of work-girls at a Notting- ham factory on account of their being put to the making of pinafores, inateed of petticoats, has happily been settled. The girls paraded the streets with placaros " We will make underskirts, we will not sew pinafores." The old-fashioned "petticoat" has become an underskirt, but it may be mentioned that it was originally the name applied to a man's garment. Now- a-days the nerne end the article, are alike discarded by both sexes. The law requires woman to wear a dis- tinctive style of dress from that of man, but no law can compel a woman to wear pay particular garment, and with the coming of the hobble skirt, underskirts have gone out. A good many trades and industries have suffered from the vogue for attenuated fashions end although changes sometimes come quickly, it does not seem likely that the underskirt-makers will be working over-time yet awhile. The Predictions of a Severe Winter.—We usually expect two or three brief spells of fine autumn weather associated with the festival. of St. Lobes, Oct. 18, All Saints, and St. Martin the latter falling on November It. There is an old belief that tie weather at Martinmas determines what sort of winter it is going to be, bat the prophets have already decided to their own eatisfaction, that it Is going to be a very severe one. The predictions of a cold winter are usually associated with the state of the berries, the movements of certain animals or will birds, but this year more importance is attached to the behaviour of the bees. They are coating the walls of their hives, with an extra lining of wax, and this proceeding, we are told, is invariably the precursion of a rigorous winter. It cannot be raid that there inns such thing as prescience of this nature, but it is probable that it comes true, if at all mostly by accident, and there is no statistical confirmation of the popular belief that hot summers are followed by cold winters. The most ominous factor in all these predictions is that a severe winter is overdue, for we do have such a visitation once Inn circle of twelve or thirteen years. and we have not had an old- fashioned winter since 1895.</p>
        <p>Ancient and Modern Modes Combined.—This autumn's fashions are reminiscent of the thirties of the last cen- tury, a period that to most people is associated with severity of a trim and crudity of an uninteresting description, but our designers and adapters have omitted the severities, and crudities, and adapted those features only which are in consonance with modern n.orien. This judicious mingling of ancient with modern styles has resulted in a harmonious whole,which cannot fail to please the most difficult taste. The fichu and flounce, refit° and ruche, the sash and the bow all belonged to that period when the headgear was decidedly pretty, although so much cannot be said of the millinery of to-day. The materials of the thirties were gorgeous Indeed—no are those of this season—but whereas the former stood alone, BO stiff were they, ours rthich are even licher, are soft and supple. For, too, is used as never before, with the most fragile fabrics for afternoon and evening wear is combined successfully ssith the riches or simplest materials. The 'opts for lace takes us back to the last century,nlthough its use was not an universal then as now, when it adorns day and evening frocks entdoor wraps, muffs, stoles, and footgear, with the</p>
        <p>most obliging Impartiality. Entire dresses are made of it for evening wear,and when arranged over coloured nuts, the effect of a rich brocade is produced. Th e threequarter lace tunic is a popular afternoon toilette, and an insertion of lace to match often finishes the hem. Coarse lace trims muffs, velvet coats, and stoles. Never was the lace scarf more popular. A novelty in this connection is of Valenciennes lace in black and white, sewn in alternate TORS. Coloured dress shoes are veiled in black and white lace, and a rosette of the same with a bead in the centre takes the place of a buckle. Theatre bags are not only made of lace, to match the frocks worn with them, but are lined with the same. .</p>
        <p>—lsie—w—ft;bbons.—The narrow black velvet ribbon band which in the early part of last century was the sole ornament permitted the young girl of that period, is again much worn, but with a difference. It is now threaded through jewelled sides, and finished in front with n dainty jewelled bouquet of svarkling Il)wers composed often of valuable gems. the new vuturun ribbons are distinguished by their width, softness and lightness, and by the beauty of their designs. The huge bows worn during the summer are still preferred as trimmings for hats by. these women who found them suitable to their puilettlar style, bele° the great width. _</p>
        <p>Handsome Trimmings.—Elaborate knots of cord or braid, as trimming for coats and skirls, admit of a display of combinations of colour, of which many examples will be Been this winter. Black and white will be prominent in these braids, and elaborate designs are shown in which the colours aro interwoven, and those will be nisi on two- coloured c ate and dresses,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>RYLANDS' RECREATION CLUB'S "SMOKER."</head>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECREATION CLUB'S "SMOKER."</p>
        <p>President's Tribute to the Club.</p>
        <p>As a wind-up to a most successful season the bowling section of the Itylands' Recreation Club held its annual smoking concert and prize distribution in tbe pavilion on Saturday evening. There was a large gathering and the affair proved eminently successful and enjoyable. Mr. W. Peter Rylands (president) occupied the chair, and was supported by Messrs. T Leslie, T Reddish, H Maddock, J Newton, J Jeffreys, J Critchley (chairman of the Bonding Green Committee) and George Monks (secretary). . . . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. RYLANDS, beforeaubmitting the loyal toast, con- gratulated the club upon completing nearly five years' history. lie felt that it was a difficult undertaking at the commencement when the men were subscribing and making great sacrifices without an immediate return, but he was proud to think that club minnow established on a thoroughly sound basis and that they could look forward to the future with more than confidence. (Applause ) The most important branch, however, was the bowling greens which needed a great deal of atten- tion before reaching anything like perfection. Men who were fond of any particular sport or game were naturally disposed to seek the most perfect conditions but he hoped that they would not criticise too closely their own greens but rally round them and give their patronage. Flo wanted them to . . . - .</p>
        <p>be proud to bowl for Rylands Bros. (Cheery.) Their's woos club that would compare favourably with any club in the whole of Britain. (Applause.) It was also the more praiseworthy :when it was taken into account that it was the men themselves who hal the management in hand and not the firm, whose only part wan to give it their sympstbetio support. It was a magnificent example to the wwole kingdom, as a step, they might describe in the direction of Social Reform and as of greater practicaL value than many of the nostrums of the time. (hour. hear.) Things like that gave an interest in life, some where to go and something to enjoy. (Applause.) Mr. Ryland' then distributsli the prizes as follow:</p>
        <p>811 IBM COM PETTTION.—W inners, Church-street No 1 (Wiredraweni) : W Rouglaley (captain), clock ; .1 Langley, cruet ; II Widdop, umbrella ; E Haskell, trinket net ; II Broadhurst, kettle; L Bale, clock ; C Horton, umbrella ; E Hovey, lea net ; H Jerakes, band bag. Runners-up (Ropery No. I) : W Drinbobbin (ceptnio), E Gleave, T Platt, E Soott, J Thomason, J Molyneus, J Ilsokey, .1 Curti*. Each of the runners up received a watlh.</p>
        <p>BOW LING MAIN.—G Monks, James ',Temkin, R Haigh, J Thomason, A Littlewood, I. Bate, S Sheldon, It Allen, S Broadhurst, TV Cotten), Cl Shaw, H Langley, T Humphreys, J Critchley, J Wells, J Hankey. Mr. MADDOCK, in proposing the toast of " The President," aaid that Mr. Rylands had come there at great Inconvenience to himself. The credit of the club, however, was not altogether upon their owe shoulders for had Mr. Itylande not come forward with his generosity to their ail they could not have made such progress. (Applause.) Mr. 11. 'lmo secoaded and the vote was heartily passed. During the evening no excellent program me was gone through. Movies, T Humphreys, .1 Willett, J Holbrook and Davies all contributed a rugs in first- class style. Mr T Leslie gave • reading from Mark Twain while Messrs W W Wnittle and F Kershaw were responsible for the cornio side of the programme. Mr. E. A. Trots was an alcomplished accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WARRINGTON CLUB.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON CLUB.</p>
        <p>A mixed foursome competition was played on the links of the above club on Satnrdny, for prizes given by Lieutenant-Colonel Lyon. Mr. and Mrs. L. A. Voisey won the Colonel's prize, and the second, given by the Captain, Mr. 11. Fsirclougb, wee carried off by Mrs. Hepherd and Mr. John Fairclough. The sweepstake weas divithd tquelly between Mr. and Mrs. J. E. Birtles, Mr. and Miss Flepherd, and Mr. and Miss Fairhurst. The following are the returns:— Mr and Mrs Voisey lOl 141 861 Mr.) Fairclongh and Mrs Hepherd lO2 13 89 Mr and Mm J E Birtles lOO 10 90 Mr E 0 Fairhurst and Miss Esirhurst 110 20 90 Mr.) Hepherd and Miss Ilepherd ...... 10 t 11 90 Mr T B Handley and Miss Richardson 94 31 901 Mr and Mrs.) Fairhurst Dr Peacooke and Miss 14 Picton Mr end Mrs If A White lO6 14 92 Mr Cll Tinsley and Mrs Davies Mr 111 Brady and Mrs Newcombe lO3 10 93 Mr H Kirkconnell and Miss Lofthouse 104 10 91 Rev T Swarbreck and Miss Platen Mr F H Preedy and Miss Bainbridge ll3 15i 97i Mr E A Connor and Miss D Ha 11... Mr E Fairclough &amp; M iss A G Fairclough 121 201 100 i MrErie Fairclongh &amp; Miss NFairclqugh 117 16i 100 i Mr If.) Hall and Mrs Handley Mr J Bainbridge and Miss Dolan l2l 19 102 Mr E 0 Davies and Miss Greenall llO 8 102 Mr F G Ainsworth and Miss Pigot 120 16i 103)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>HOCKEY</head>
        <p>HOCKEY</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD CLUB. The First Eleven were not at their best last Satur- day, and only made a draw of 5 5 with Manchester Y.M.C. 4. All credit is however due to the visitors, for they certianly played well from start to finish. They were first to score, and then Banks eqnslired for Latchford, but the Y.M.C.A. inside left gained the lead by a smart follow up, pushing the ball into the net before the goslkeeper could get rid of it. Travis, however, equalized before half-time. Each side scored three times in the second half, Latchford through Travis (2) and Parker (I). The defence on both sides was shaky. The visitors centre-half and left wing were good. The Second Eleven visited Northern Cricket Ground to play Northern" B "end gave a capital account of themselves, winning by 7-2. Northern have strong , teams this season and possess e beautiful ground. More- over their players are good sportsmen and a visit to Northern is indeed a pleasure. The game was con- tested at a fast rate throughout and Latchford won through the prompt shooting of the forwards in front of goal. Despite the seven goals against him the Northern goalkeeper defended well and in the home attacking party the left wing was moat prominent. W. H. Minarets played one of his best games and shot 2 goals for Latchford, the other goals being obtained by W. Fletcher (I) 11. Fletcher (I.) The matches for this afternosn with Liverpool University are cancelled owing to that club failing to secure a ground so far this season. It has therefore been arranged for the First Eleven (whites) to play the Second Eleven (clarets). Bully off 2.45 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CROSFIELD'S V. WIGAN.</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S V. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>This match was played on the ground of the former and ended in a win for Wigan by 2 goals to I. The visitors played a splendid combination game, aided by the terrific clearing drives by their fell backs. Sandy, of Croefleld's, showed excnitional speed, but was rather selfish with the ball, which is noticeable with several other players. Harrison and Paget's combination and hand-work were excellently displayed, and if practised by their clubmates will undoubtedly improve their play.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LADIES V. BOWDON LADIES. Played at Bowdon on Wednesday. Score : Mies D Richardson Mrs Hepberd Mrs Newcombe 0 Miss Neeham Mrs E A White 0 Mrs Abbott 1 Mrs Births I) Miss Duggan Miss Lottbouse</p>
        <p>SCHOOL PLAYGROUNDS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>LANCASHIRE SUGGESTION TO THE BOARD</head>
        <p>A LANCASHIRE SUGGESTION TO THE BOARD</p>
        <p>OF EDUCATION. The Board of Education having appointed a Depart- mental Committee to report on playground accommoda- tion and the cost of extending playgrounds in some cases being prohibitive, the Lancashire Elementary Education Sub-committee have forwarded the following suggestion to the Board :—That the existing tchoolle should not be refused recognition, or have their recog- nised accommodation reduced because the playground accommodation is not equal to the area required in the erection of a new echool ; and that county local educa- tion sulthoritiea should be empowered to provide play- ing fields and gymnasia, and to organise games and I other physical exercises.</p>
      </div>
