      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M.P. LABOUR AND THE MUNICIPAL</p>
        <p>ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The coming Municipal elections will give the local Labour parties in the country an opportunity of testing their voting strength, and of measuring the progress they have made. Last year the Labour Party were quite unusually successful in the Municipal elections, winning a considerable number of 'seats in Many important towns. This year, so far as we can gather the information, there seems to be the average number of Lanaur candidates, though perhaps not so many new candidates as there used to be a few years ago. That is a natural outcome of success. The Labour Party has now something to hold, something to lose, as well as something to gain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>BIRDS IN THE HAND.</head>
        <p>BIRDS IN THE HAND.</p>
        <p>Each year labour candidates retire for re-election, and the Party seems to think, and quite rightly, that it is better to concen- trate their efforts in keeping what they have than to risk losing that by grasping for too much. This accounts for the undoubted fact that the Labour Party does not now put forward as many candidates for municipal elections as it did in its earlier years. But a large number of seats are being attacked,and the outlook is distinctly promising.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>POWER FOR GOOD.</head>
        <p>A POWER FOR GOOD.</p>
        <p>Few people who have watched the activity' of labour in local politics will deny that its efforts have helped to raise the standard of municipal government, and to rouse a greater public interest in civic questions. The pro- 1 blems of local government are of vital interest to all classes, but to none so much as the working classes. Professor Jones, of Glas- gow, has been attacking the Labour Party in the " Hibbert Journal," on the ground that it is a class party, and that by seeking to promote its own interests it is corrupting political life. It is singular that a !man of the ability and knowledge, and especially the sympathy of Professor Henry Jones, should take such an erroneous view of the work and aim of the Labour Party. His premises are perfectly sound, and every thinking man will accept them. He has already laid down these premises and built upon them in the chapters of his book, entitled The Working Faith of a Social Reformer." He maintains, and rightly, that it is immoral and anti-social for any party or section to seek to promote its own special interests which are not the interests of the community generally. But the mistake he makes is in regarding the labour movement as falling within that condemnation. NOT A CLASS MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>The Labour movement is not a class move- ment, and in promoting the welfare of the , labouring classes, a party is not advancing sectional interests only, but is doing good which will benefit the State as a whole. Any instance of legislation passed by the support of the Labour Party will serve to illustrate this point. Take Workmen's CoMpensation, Education or even the instance which might appear to give some support to Professor Jones' contention, namely the Trades Dis- putes Act. The Education Acts, Old Age Pensions, and indeed a good deal of our legislation is specially enacted for the work- ing classes, and, therefore, it is all immoral and corrupting according to Professor Jones. But this is the point that this critic fails to see. The working class, as Frederick Harrison long ago said, is not a class—it is a nation. Everything which benefits that class benefits the whole community. Education, public health, insurance bills, pensions for the aged, all raise the standard of working class intelligence and life, and therefore raise the real wealth of the nation. If the Labour Party were concerned altogether in seeking to improve the condition of the work- ing classes—if it paid no heed to other ques- tions of national politics (which is not true) it would still not be justly open to the charge that Professor Jones makes against it. The condition of the working classes is the problem of present day politics. On the improvement of that condition the very exist- ence of the State is at stake. If we had a democratic party which was representative of all classes, that party would have to take up the work which the Labour movement has made specially its own. It is not class work : it is social work: and the party which is doing that work is not a class party but a social party, and it is not corrupting but elevating public life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WORKING CLASS WORK.</head>
        <p>WORKING CLASS WORK.</p>
        <p>The work of the Labour Party in municipal government, which is wholly directed at using municipal powers to benefit the working classes, would be called immoral, corrupting, and anti-social work by Professor Jones. But it is work which is purifying local government, which is brightening the lives of the poor, and which is raising the whole tone of public life. The education of the masses is surely not a class matter. It is the working class, generally speaking, which uses the national education system, but it is the whole nation which benefits from it. So it is with public health administration. Everything which lowers the death-rate, which reduces sickness, which enables an individual to become a better workman and a better citizen is not 'class benefit—it is a benefit to all classes. TWO UNDENIABLE FACTS.</p>
        <p>It is the purpose of local government to raise the condition of the people, and as the people are mainly working people it follows that municipal government is mainly a working class question. That is the case; and therefore the Labour Party is quite right in asserting the claims of working men and women to an adequate share in the manage- ment of local affairs. Iwo things in this connection are undeniable. The first is that it is for the public good that all classes should be taking an interest in, and an active part in the work of local government. Democracy means not only government by the consent of the governed, but govern- ment by the active co-operation of the governed. The second undeniable thing is that the working class have plenty of men and women among them who are quite com- petent to fill representative positions on our local bodies. A third contention might be advanced which would not perhaps carry such universal assent, but which, I think, is equally undeniable. That contention is that as municipal questions are mainly working class questions, intelligent working men and women are more likely to understand these questions, owing to their personal experiences than people in other ranks of society. It might be urged on the other hand that work- ing men have not the business experieme which other classes have acquired, but experience has shown that this is not the case in actual practice, for working men Councillors have shown as much ability for public work as the men who have been supposed to possess a mon ()rely of busines ability.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>GROWING RESPONSIBILITY.</head>
        <p>GROWING RESPONSIBILITY.</p>
        <p>The importance of municipal work to the working classes is growing every year. They are especially the sufferers by any neglect to carry out municipal powers. If the Educa- tion Acts be badly administered it is the workingman's children who suffer; if housing be bad, it is his children who die; it the Old Age Pensions Act or the Poor Law be .harshly administered it .is his class who . .</p>
        <p>are the viCtims. The case for working mm and women on our public bodies cannot be questioned, and the Labour Party, in spite of Professor Jones' criticism,, is doing a praise- worthy work for its own class in particular, and for the country generally by bringing its energy and idealism into civic affairs. WOMEN AS COUNCILLORS.</p>
        <p>It is very much to be regretted that women are not coming forward in larger numbers as municipal candidates. It is difficult I know for women to qualify under the law as it now stands. Married women are not eligible as municipal candidates, and tha‘ disqualifies the great bulk of the most suitable women. But in spite of that, women are not taking advantage of the limited facilities they have to the extent they ought to do. This to some extent is due to the opposition of men, who do not seem very anxious to welcome women's co-operation in local government. Putting party aside, one may appeal for more serious interest by the citizens in local affairs. A good part of the work of Parlia- ment is rendered useless or ineffective because local opinion lags behind legislative concessions. British municipal institutions are pure, and on the whole well managed, but they are capable of improvement in' that respect. It is not so much that what we do is badly done, as that so much which might be done is left undone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>"FANCY RELIGIONS."</p>
        <p>In the bygones there was an oil sergeant-major who, on mustering his men for Sunday mo mug church parade, was wont to divide them into three sections, thus : "Church of Englanders to the right, Roman Catholics to the left, fancy religions in the rear." I tbougheof this as I read of the New Thought Cult, a fresh religion for " Reconstruction of Civilisa- tion" lately imported from America. It may be very briefly and roughly described as a sort of Christian Science, "only more so," one of the peculiarities of its teaching being that members are immortal until they desire death! A LOCAL POSITIVIST.</p>
        <p>I was impelled to this reference by what I read in my Examiner" concerning the death and funeral of Mr. Hubert Congreve. The talented and lamented engineer was one of the comparatively few English followers of Auguste Comte, who himself curiously enough, while temporarily deranged in mind owing te illness, once attempted suicide by throwing himself into the Seine, but was rescued none the worse. The truly great philosopher's work on what he called the Universal Religion, and what its professors in these parts and readers of this paper n sr know as the Religion of Humanity, was i-sued 57 years ago under the title, "A course of positive policy." It is worthy cf special note that the motto of the cult is " Vivre pour autrui" ("live for others"). Although adherents are not very numeronr. there are and always have been among them persons of the highest intellectual eminence. Have Warrington admirers of George ERA noticed that her teaching is generally Positivist in tone? THE OPPOSITE EXTREME.</p>
        <p>It is remarkable that in his theological views the immediate predecessor of the late Mr. Congreve in the office of chin f engineer of the Manchester Ship Canal Cotnpany was at the Antipodes aeit were. He was one of the popularly so-called " Plymotrh Brethren." It should be observed in passing. that, just as the " Friends " mildly protest against the nickname " Quakers," no these " Brethren," while willing and even eager to be understood (the term, that is) as embracing sisters, strongly object to the persistence of the original parochial title now that their system has extensive ramifications. I once heard a distinguished and accomplished member of this sill struggling sect deliver a simple and earnest address based upon what he held to be the Gospel according to Jesus Christ, and, to be quite plain and frank, some of his hearers felt at the close as if they had been consigned t the quarter described in the first book of Dante's Divine Comedy! Of course, the speaker had no such intention. One cannot supp3se that he had for a moment contemplated such a possibility, but that was the effect upon sir.dry and divers among those who had assembled to bear him and other hot gospellers, the occasion being a tent mission in an industrial district unaccustomed to such vigorous and unvarnished lay testimony. THE SWEDENBORGIANS.</p>
        <p>Let me anticipate another disclaimer by explain- ing that I use merely fur convenience as a hesdline this common man-in-the-street designation of the New Jerusalem Church. I fear me that the narrow-minded " non corn " aforesaid would have unceremoniously I classed even this venerable foundation with his "fancy religions." Its present exponent so far as this town is concerned is attracting select and interested audi- ences to his Wednesday night lectures at the Masonic , Hall. Whatever the general reader may think of the religious teaching of the famous Swedish philosopher and mystic in question, it must be allowed that Emmanuel Swedenborg is entitled to rank among the greatest, figures in history. Of transparent sincerity and character absolutely irreproachable, as well as of rare intellectual ability aid extraordinary scholarly attainments, he was indeed a marvel. When we come to consider his claim to have paserd alive through all the experiences which accompany death, the Rev W. 11. Claxton and those of us who appreciate the genius of the great Swede but do not interpret " the writings" as he does, must part c mpany. For sometime I have been a diligent reader of " Morn- ing I.ight," a most interesting organ of the New Church, and I gather therefrom that although Swedenborg met the epii it of Louie MV. and Ge'rge IL in the heavenly kingdoms, he found the Psalmist David and the Apostle Paul in the infernal regions ! I see from the advertisement on the first page of this issue that the ex-President of the New Church Conference invites relevant questions. May I respectfully ask how he explains the apparent injustice of the unequal system of future rewards and punishment thus reveaved by the seer his Master, or rather how be reconciles the incidents re'erred to with the stupendous claims put forth by and in behalf of the undoubtedly sincere, but possibly in certain important respects mistaken, Swedenborg ? CREMATION.. _</p>
        <p>However, I have not yet quite done with the sad I case of the late Mr. Hubert Congreve, whose remains were cremated at Manchester last week. Rather over a quarter of a century ago, the Cremation Society was founded by the late Sir Henry Thompson "to promote cremation as a method of disposing of the dead in a sanitary, reverent, and innocuous manner without danger to the living." To be sure, one might g) very much further back still, and show how this method of disposal of the dead has been common to civilised nations as well as barbarians. In our own country at present, however, Christ'an sentiment is evidently adverse thereto, notwiihstanding the not infrequent examples which some of the most intellectual and cultured and refined women as well as men of our time have canaed to toe set at one or other of thedosen or ovirtcrematoria already available in this land. For myself, with the late Kate Field, whose body was cremated at San Francisco, where I happen to have two or three personal friends, I believe Cremation is not only the healthiest and the cleanest, but the most Poet:cal way of disposing of the dead ; whoever prefers loathsome worms to ashes possesses a strange imagination." MORE ABOUT EATING.</p>
        <p>From the topic to which the recent banquet here gave rise to that of food faddism is a simple and easy transition. How often should one eat ? One well- known doctor declaim it to be a matter of tempera- ment, habit and experiment. Let me summarise his conclusions : Because one man keeps fit and lives to • ripe old age by eating five rnealsa day, it does sot follow that another will be similarly benefited. A great deal depends upon habit. If you have been in the habit of eating only twice&amp; day—in the morning and at right- sod have kept well on that habit, then adhere to it. Similarly, if you have been used to four meals • day, the stomach will haTe grown accustomed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>to nourishment four times per diem, and certainly will become upset if you try to exist on two meals.. great thing let° find out what form of diet suits too, and then stick to it —whatever other poople do or advise. TO BREAKFAST OR NOT? Locally, this would seem for the moment to be the question; and our good friend the Rev. A. M. Mitchell who has in his time played many interesting parts, is entitled to the credit f ita resurrection. As to this, however, some of us will venture to withstand him to the face, because We think he is to be blamed—that is, if he is to be underst)od as undertaking a no-breakfast crusade; otherwise, of course, we should not be so impertinent as to interfere. I for one anticipate that in this, an in every cause he has espoused, Mr. Mitebell will turn out a whole-hogger, therefore, I take the opportunity to interpose • word in behalf of the British breakfast. A. Pr. Spriggs, writing in the "Hospital " a while ago, put it, the solid breakfast, an ancient British institution, is taken after the night's reek when the body generally is of good tone. Ido not doubt that the digestive organs , are in better condition at this time, for fo_ds, which later in the day are ill-borne in dyspeptic subject, such an bac ,n, can often be relished at breakfast without penalty. The desire to rest He! after a good meal is also frequently absent at this hour, though present after later meals. In the morn- ing the digestive system is more like that of a child, who seldom desires to rest after any meal. A meat breakfast can, therefore, be recommended, provided that time be allowed to eat it at leisure. It is won • the rev. gentleman's move, so' it please him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>LATE SIR WILLIAM CROSSLEY.</head>
        <p>THE LATE SIR WILLIAM CROSSLEY.</p>
        <p>An Impressive Funeral.</p>
        <p>Deeply impressive scenes were witnessed at Bowdon on Monday when the funeral of the late Sir William John Crcsaley took place in the Parish Churchyard. All the people of the district had come out to show their sympathy at the loss of an honoured friend and , neighbour. Blinds were lowered in every house—in the homes of the poor as well as in the mansions of the rich -and the roadways leading from Clerffield, Sir William Crossley's house, were thronged with silent spectators. Near the church were lines of people behind an anemic which was Lrmed by the hundreds of work people in the employment of Crossley Bra., Limited, Openshaw, Manchester, or 'associated with the °panelist Lads' Club, of which Sir William was the founder and conrtant helper. (halide the church a large prtion of the grouna was covered with timers. The choicest of the wreaths, with two or three exceptions, were laid on the stem of the chancel. Those exceptions were the large clusters of white lilies and other beautiful flowers which were borne on the coffin.</p>
        <p>The principal mourners were Lady Crossley (widow). Sir Kenneth (son) and Lady Kenneth Crossley, Mr. Eric Crossley (son) and Mrs. Eric Crossley, Mr. Brian Crossley (son), Mrs. McLaren (sister), Mr. Hastings Crostley (brother), Mr. Ales McLaren (nephew), Mr. Alan, Mr. Erskine, and Mr. Marshall Crossley (nephews), Miss Anderson (eausin), Mr. George and Mr. Harry Irwin (cousins), Mr. and Mrs. Hastings Irwin (eou-ins), Mr. Frank Crossley, Mr. William Crossley, and Mr. Sydney Crossley, Mr. Alfred Simpson, Mr. Alderman James Fades, Mr. and Mrs. J. N. Field, Mr. and Mrs. Frank Merriman, sod Mr. Norman Field.</p>
        <p>There were many deputations from clrporations and societies to which Sir William Crossley had rendet ed service.</p>
        <p>The service in the chareh was conducted by the Rev. Hewlett Johnson, vicar of Si. Margaret's Church ('it which Sir William Crossley and his household were accustomed to worship when at Bowdon). The Vicar of the church, Archdeacon Msitland Wood, was pres,nt, but took no part in the service. The Bishop of Lincoln (Dr. Hicks) read the Lmismi. The choir attended and chanted the Ptillm (xc ) and led in the singing of the hymns "0, hapy band of rilgrima," 'The sower went forth sowing," and " Praise to the Holiest in the height." The organist was Mr. J. P. Field (of St. Margaret's). . _</p>
        <p>The committa service at the graveside was real by the Rev. 11. Johnson. Th-n the choir and the great coogregiti in sang the hymn Fight the good Best with all thy might, Christ is thy strength, nod Christ thy right. The Benediction was pronounced by the Bishop of Lincoln.</p>
        <p>LETTER FROM MR. J. R. REIM- FLETCHER, MP. The following is the copy of a letter received by Mr. W. E. Thompson, chairman of the Altriacham Division Liberal Association : Ciok's Hill, Mondeeley-on-Sea, Norfolk, October 19th. Dear Sir,—l hasten to convey my sincere sympathy in the great loss that you have sustained in the death of Sir Willism Crossley. During the late contest I found him no kind, listh during the nomination, at the cJunting of the votes, and afterwards. The loss of this well-doer is irreparable. I am lying ill in bed down here, or would have come op North. You can be %muted of the con,lolenoe of all pirties end creeds ins the heavy blow that has befallen you. Yours very faithfully, J. KEBTY-FLETCHER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>TO SAVE £l3OOOOOO.</head>
        <p>TO SAVE £l3OOOOOO.</p>
        <p>A Grand New Crusade Against Coughs,</p>
        <p>Colds and Bronchitis.</p>
        <p>There is a right way and a wrong way of treating coughs and colds, and until the discovery of the wonderful breatheable I'eps the more popular way was the wrong. This explains the prevalence and recurrence of duvet and cheat troubles, and the alarm- ing screed of consumption which directlyor indirectly coats the nation £13,000,000 a year. One in seven die from this" white plague. " It is admitted that neglected and wronglydreated coughs and colds form one of the chief ceases of long disease, because the germs of consumption, which find no hold in healthy lungs, a, settle down in tissue weakened by cold and the strain of coughing. Not the least imprtant phase of the crusade against consumption must be the education of the people into a slier, more natural, and therefore more effective method of treating coughs, colds, and bronchitis. This is the success'ul aim of Peps. The cures pro- duced by the Peps treatment are bot`i striking and unmistakable. •</p>
        <p>Just as the whole sustaining pwer of an ox Can be concentrated within a sm3ll jar, so the health-giving properties of a pine forest are now brought into the home through the agency of a boa of these wonderful little Peps, which enable victims of chest troubies to stay at home and there enjoy many of the benefita of the ideal open-air treatment. This beneficent result is achieved by the natural way in which the lungs are bathed internally with the volatile medicine stored up in Peps. When a tablet is placed in the month and allowed to slowly dissolve, the pine essences and associated medicines escape in the form of fumes which are at once taken with the air we breathe right down the bronchi and into the lungs ! How different this rational and direct Pepe treatment from the old plan of swallowing peppery cough mixtures which, being taken in the stomach, cannot reach the lung. Nature permits only air to piss into the bronchial tubes and lungs, and the Peps medicine is inhaled in the form of air.. . . .</p>
        <p>As the l'eps fumes pais through he breathing pasasges to the lunge they bathe the delicate lining :membrane with a comforting vapour atilt immediately !soothes away any soreness, and tills the cheat and lunge with a strengthening and disease-resisting medicine. The injury wrought by coughing, wheezing, and sneezing is repaired and the germ. that spread coughs, colds, sore throat and influenza— the fore- runners of consumption—positively cannot live where the Peps fumes penetrate. To the chronic bronchial sufferer, who fears the chilly autumn nights and dreads the arrival of winter. Peps come as a boon and a blessicg. They instantly ease that tightnese scenes the chest and by clearing the bronchial tubes of the hard, obstructive phlegm, get rid of that choking feeling and that exhaustive rough which racks the chest night and morning. Th, purity and effectiveness of Peps are well known. For warding off coughs, colds, bronchitis, asthma, influenza, sore throat, whooping cough, pleurisy, pneumonia, and keeping at bay even consumption itself, there is nothing no reliable., Peps, which can be obtained from all chemists, or direct frnm the Pepe Co., Carlton Bill, Leeds, at one-and-three-hallpence or two-and-nine • box. No family can do without Pepe.</p>
