        <p>Collocillsr DICKENSON sail they did not have the seven acres of land given to them ; they pill dearly for theni.llo considered that the Oen which the General l'urposes Corn buttes, selected was the right and prep ir one, lie did at think this coruruitt.e could improve upon it. Councillor PENIBERToN said the Sites anl Buildings Committee were practically unanimous in selecting the same site as the Town Council, and Alderman Bennett was the only one against it. Mr. KINGDON said that when they were sated to pass a resolution they ought to be provided with a sufficient descrip; ice of the land to show what they were passing. (Gear, hear.) Alderman BOLTON (replying to the discussion), said they decided to have a school in a particular locality and they asked the Council for a site. The Sites and Building Committee thought they hal bettor take what they could get. They went to the General Purposes , Committee and it v as decided that this particular spot was the only one upon which they could build the school. Let them pars the resolution and get the schools built. The minutes were then agreed to.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Cawley, of 28, Lloyd-street, Warrington, says:—" My kidney trouble began with pains in the back, and later I noticod that the kidney excretions were disordered and painful, containing gravel, and there was a frequent desire to relieve the bladder.</p>
