      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>NEW SCHOOL IN LOVELY-LANE. The Feeding of Children.</head>
        <p>THE NEW SCHOOL IN LOVELY-LANE. The Feeding of Children.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Education Committee was held at tbe Town Hall on Monday evening, Councillor H Roberts presiding. There were present : —Mr A J Jolley (deputy-chairman), Aldermen Bennett, Bolton, Councillors Belllan, Bill.les, Dickenson, Henshall, Jackson-Holmes, Jenkins, Pemberton. Wilson, Revs James Davies, Canon Stevenson, Father N Wilson, Messrs B T Fairclough, J Chrimes, II Kingdon, E Plinston, 0 Walker, Mrs C Jackson-Holmes, Mr J 51 Murray (director), and Mr It B Roberts (as:it:tent director). Mr. M URRkY read a letter fro -a the Fairlhld schools stating that Mr. Edward Isherwood, of Bewsey-road, had Leen appointed foundation manag• r in place of the late Mr. F. 0. Taylor. He also read a letter etatiog that the resignation of the Rev. T. Vickers as foundation manager &amp;c., of the Ladies' School of Indus- try had been accepted; and that Rev. J. H. Buckmasto had succeeded him. THE FEEDING OF POOR CHILDREN. -- •</p>
        <p>• -- The DIReCTOR read a letter from Sirs. G. F. Robson, (hon. secretary of the Woman's Labour Lesgue),enclos- hag a petition signed by 375 persons respecting the feeding of necessitous school children. The petition was as follows :—" We, the undersigned, protest against the decision arrived at re the providing of free meals for necessitous school children, and demand that all children in whose homes the income is less than 2s 3d er head per week when the rent is deducted, shall be provided with free meals." The CHAIRMAN did not think it was desirable to open up anything which might become controversial in regard to the subject matter of the petition. He would like the Chairman of the School Attendance Committee as early as possible to give a statement of the facts of the case in regard to the notion of the School Attend- ance Committee and of this committee as it would throw considerable light on the situation and remove a misconception that etisted. That should be done as early as possible, especially in view of a statement made recently at a public meeting which was not founded on facts and knowledge. Otherwise it might appear that this committee was not us sensitive to the claims of the very poor in legard to the feeding of their children as it unquestionably was and always had been. The difficulties which had existed had not been of the committee's making. The committee would have made provision if it had been allowed to do so in the only way in which it was possible to deal with the matter. The numbers were comparatively far too small for the Board of Race' ion to recognize as forming a sufficient number of constituents to justify the putting into operation of the somewhat cast-iron regulation which they imposed on them. He moved that the petition be referred to the School Attendance Com- mittee. Alderman BOLTON seconded and the motion was agreed to.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINATIONS. At a meeting of the School Management Committee a summary of the results of the examinations in science, art, commercial end other subjects held dy the Board of Education, the City and Guilds of London Inatitute, the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes and the Royal Society of Arts,so far as they affect students who attended the School of Art, the Technical Institute the Commercial Institute and the Branch Technical schools daring the session 1910-11, eras submitted and noted. The Director was instructed to convey to the Head Teachers of the various Evening Schools the Committee's satisfaction at the good results obtained at these examinations and their appreciation of the work of the Evening Schools staffs last session. The Rev. Canon Stevenson said there had been an all-round advance during the past year. In regard to the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes there was a gratifying increase in the number examined and passed, while with regard to the com- mercial institute they noticed a very great improve- ment. They had done better, perhaps, than any town with one exception in this part of the kingdom. (Hear, hear.) Each of the certificates meant at least four subjects gained by the student. in regard to the summary of branch technical schools there was only an apparent falling off in numbers, because the new sub- jects in the domestic group °curse included four subjects. Ile should like to oiler sincere congratula- tions to the principals of the Technical School and the Art Schools, and the staff of the evening schools for assisting to produce such results (Hear, heor.) The Rev. Father Wit-sox seconded. Alderman BENNE:III said that with regard to the /IR inlal in; be felt regret that the successes of the girls were not in proper proportion to those of the boys, and that so few had learnt to swim. They were about to enlarge the bathe, sod he hoped in a little while every opportunity would be given to both boys and girls to learn such a useful art as swimming. (Hear, hear). The CHA 111 MAN said they were all in accord with the Chairman of the Committee in his appreciation of the results of the examinations and his kind reference to the staffs of the school, THE LOVELY-LANE SCHOOL SITE. At a meeting of the Sites and Buildings Committee held on October 6th, a report having been submitted as to the 'withdrawal, by the Chairman of the Sites and • Buildings Committee at the meeting of the Education Committee, on the 18th September, 1911,0 f resolutions dealing with the position of the site of the public elementary school to be erected in Lovely-lane, together with a copy of a resolution passed by the General Purposes Committee of the Town Council allotting, for that purpose, about two acres of the land adjoining the railway, it was resolved that applica- tion be made te the Local Governmeet Board for their sanction to the appropriation by this committee of about two acres of land on the northern extremity of the site pureheaed by the Town Council in Lovely. lane,for the purpose of the erection thereon of a public elementary school. At the same meeting authority was given for tenders to be invited for the enlargement of the Bolton Council school conditionally upon this draft specification and quantities submitted by the architects being approved by the Surveyor. Alderman BOLTON, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, said the committee were doing their best to provide more schools. They would soon have three or! four new ones going at once. (Hear, hear). Mr. FAIRCLOVG II seconded. Alderman BeNwrrr said that at the General Purposes Committee they debated the matter and a good case was made not for endeavouring to lay out the Given acres on what he considered to be artistic and utilitarian lines. If a proper plan had been prepared, with a perspective view, he felt sure that the decision come to would have been different. Ile hoped that sometime, probably in a thousand years from now— (laughter)—they would begin to realise that if they had seven acres of land it would be worth while to make the best use of them and lay them out in the best manner. (Hear, bear). The Rev. J. DAVIES remarked that Alderman Bennett was assuming that the matter had been finished with He had no right to do so. The CitAIRMAS said the position was that certain suggested sites were submitted to the General Purposes Committee, and after consideration they decided upon this particular site, and it was referred hack to the Education Committee for their approval or othere ise. The Rev. J. DAvilo : llst we have not the plan before an. Continuing. he said he hail changed his opinion since baked been on the spot. Ile thought the top end site would be the best. Councillor Manta said that where the General</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ANCIENT ORDER OF FORESTERS.</head>
        <p>ANCIENT ORDER OF FORESTERS.</p>
        <p>Warrington District.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above society was held on Saturday, at the Horse and Jockey Inn, Alartinscroft, P.D.C.R. Bro George May bury, District Chief Ranger presiding, and PtC.R. Bro 0 W It Runes, Sub- District Chief Hanger occupied the vice-chair. There were also present P.D.C.R.'s lire K Thornley, District Secretary eel Bro P Peacock, District Treasure-, Bros II Maybury, J Chimes, T Hollins, W Orrett, .1 Walsh, District Truttee P.O R.'s Bros S B Healey, W Healey, H Emmerson, Aki Baines, E Clayton, W James, Taylor, H Perdue, T Lightfoot, and E ilollins as Delegates from the Caere forming the district. The district meeting was opened according to the ritual laid down for use in the order. There were returned 1,172 old members, and 35 new members while six members bad died during the quarter. The funeral fund shows a balance of £3,639 70. . . .</p>
        <p>After the Court returns had heen chocked the dele- gates proceeded with the business relating io the interests of the District, the following Brethren were unanimously elected as the Officers of the District for the coaxing year. Bro. G. W. R. Baines was elected District Cnief Ranger and Bro. Tnomas Roden sub- district Chief Ranger. Bro. James Bancroft was elected Relieving Oflioer and Bro. it. Emmerson3 District Examiner.</p>
        <p>During the afterooon the National Insurance Bill came up for discussion and if. was agreed that the Dis- trict fall in with the suggestion that a Cauferenoe of the kindred Societies in town be held to discuss and adopt propositions for amendments thereon, and that the suggested amendments to the Bill be forwarded to the local representatives in the House of Commons. A hearty vote of thinks was accorded to the o.llcers for their services during the past year. The meet bng was brought to a close by the D.C.R. Bro. Baines, after which the thficers and Dalegates adj rurned to the dining-room where a sumptuous repast awaited therm Justice was done to the good things provided by the hostess, Miss Massey, and she was accorded a hearty vote of thanks.</p>
        <p>• On returning to lisivn, the dist: ict offiaers and dale. gates proceedid to Court Dope No. TM, held at the Masons Arms, Mill-street. A hearty reception was aecoided taa them, and the district officers were rrrptested to occupy their respective positions in the Court. Upon taking their places, they v too given a hearty greeting, and congratulatory speeches were given by P.D.C.R. Bro 0 klaybury, T Rollins, H Maybury, J Walsh and the District Secretary.--The District Chief Ranger responded, and hoped that unity and good-fellowship would prevail during his you of office, and he would endeavour to do all in his power to promote the success of Forestry in the District. The meeting terminated with a vote of thanks to the District Officers and Delegates for their attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>HIGHER EDUCATION IN LANCASHIRE.</head>
        <p>HIGHER EDUCATION IN LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>Increase of £10,600 In Expenditure.</p>
        <p>Sir Henry Ilibbert, chairman of the Lancashire County Education Committee, at the County Offices, Preston, on Monday, mentioned that, whereas the expenditure last yet, on higher education in non- county boroughs, urban districts and rural districts was .C 17,108 this year it was estimated to cost £58,066, or a difference of about £10,600. The committee had approved of estimates involving an estimated expendi- ture of £6,113 for endowed grammar schools financed by the county, and of the payment of .C 7350 to univorsitiee, colleges, S:c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>GHENT EXIITBITION, 1013.</head>
        <p>GHENT EXIITBITION, 1013.</p>
        <p>Considerable interest has been AT(Mei throughout the Continent of Europe by the announcement that an exhibition on an elaborate and probably unprecedented scale is to be held in the ancient Flemish city of Ghent. Belgium, as a nation, is exerting every effort to make the event worthy of a country which possesses unique historical associationa and a commerce which has grown enormously in volume in the last half century. This exhibition will be a peaceful battle-ground of the great trading nations of the world. Already Germany, ever alert to seine, the plating opportunity to her advantage, end France with characteristic enterprise, have applied for extensive space. Great Britain will doubtlest, al a nation, realise the obligation due to her traders of making a worthy display of her still unrivalled manu- factures and arts. Ghent's notable venture is under the direct patronage of his Majesty the King of the Belgians, which means a great deal there, much more than might be supposed. Full particulars end illu- strated booklet containing plan of exhibition, etc., may be obtained by calling or writing to the special London Office, 1, Suithampton-row, W.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>sir Be sure you get the</head>
        <p>sir Be sure you get the</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>REFORMS IN LOCAL RATING.</p>
        <p>Conservatives Support Taxes ou</p>
        <p>Land an Beer.</p>
        <p>Interest in the work of the Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society continues to ha well maintained and there was a large attendance a% the weekly meeting held in Caira-streot Church Parlour on Tuesday evening. The subject for debate win a 1111 U prdvide for a readjustment of local rat ng, and it vies somewhat surprising to find both sides of the House in agreement on the main principles. The CHANCELLOR OF THE EXCHEqUER (Mr. J. Jarman) in introducing the measure (which was published in last week's "Examiner,") points I out that the first provi• sion was a uniform ay atem of rating for the whole of London. It had long been felt that the present method was v3ry unfair' resulting as it did, in tremendously high rates and low assessable values in East and South London and high values and low rates in the West End. It was high time that the whole of London was treated as one city. (Appl.use). It was also felt that throughout the Kingdom the overseers of the poor, who at present bad the rating in their own hands should cease to act in that capacity, and that those who were more directly in touch with the will and wishes of the ratepayers—the memhers of the various Councils —should take over the work. It was not right tout municipalities should subsidise th 3 rates at tha expense of municipal enterprises. Toe consumer of water, gas and electricity and the user of trenaways, ought eat to charged more than the actual cont.Corporationi should he not be out to make profits., but to study the good of the ratepayers generally, and It eats therefore proposed that in future all profits should, after all charges for interest on c 'pita' outlay,depreciation of plant, upkeep,siages &amp;c., , had been met, be used for the purpo33 of a reduction in the charges for the various services. Having referred to the provision for the direct payment of rates by the tenant, tho Chancellor passed on to the clausss relliting to the separate valuation of land, buildings and fittings In order to ascertain the site valus of lend, they wJuld employ one of three tests :—(1) the ground rent pay- able; 12) the owner's own valution ; (3) in the event of the owner declining to value, the rating authorities, should make the valuation. They considered it wee time that the ground lendlord whoop to now had done' nothing for the value which he had taken out of a town, should pay his share of the n uoicipal up-keep Industry would be greatly relieved by the provision that the rating would ouly be on half the value of fittings and machinery, wherees land sal build- logs would be sipsrately rated on their full annual I value. In 1817 lend bore 63 per cent, of rates and I other property 31 per cent. la 1891 the proportion was : land 15.1 per cent. ; property 8169 per cant. It was proposed that a special rate to be called " the Poor and Police Hate" should be levied for the pur- poses of the relief of the poor, workhouses, infirmaries, hOSpitalS, asylums, police expenses and the feedlng of necessitous children. Three-fourths of this rate was to be charged on public-hauses, clubs and si.nilar establishments in proportion to their sales of intoxicants. In Warrington the present poor rate (exclusive of education) produced £IO,OOJ, and under the new scheme" the trade" would have to contribute .C 31,000 equal to 7w lid, per head of the population. Under the old excise end licence duties the charge was 17s. per head, an that iostesd of being burdened " the trade" would be relieved to the extent of nearly £20,000,000 in taxa:Lin. That could hardly be called robbery and spoliation. (Laughter.) It was estimated that 90 per cent, of the poverty and crime of the country was due directly or indirectly to the drinking habits of the people, and it was not therefore too much to ask that 75 per cent, of the cost of dealing with those evils should he paid by those who were directly responsible for the habits referred to. The present assessments of public-houses would stand, because the Government did not intend to interfere with the value of the houses but only to take a proportion on the sales. The brewing industry,4, was well to remember, gave far less employment in proportion to turnover than any other trade and the money spout in it waa wasted, as it did not produce anything in return. On the into of any land within a rating authority's area, it was proposed that the authority should be entitled to increment on the same at the rate of 20 per cent of the increased price obtained over the amount that the land had been rated at. "The authority would also have pourer to purchase any land required for municipal purposes at 25 years purchase or less of the annual ratable value. At the present time when muni- cipalities required land,they often had to pays fictitious price. The Chancellor concluded by asking the House to consider the Bill on its merits, promising that any amendments which did not interfere with vital principles would receive every consideration from the Cabinet, lie asked members of the Conservative party who were interested in town planning, better housing of the people, and the lessening of crime to support the measere, hems 3 those things could not be accomplished while the existing land laws were in force. Ile appealod for the support of the Irish on the ground that they had experienced for many years the difficulty of dealing with the laud question in their own country, having had to buy out absentee landlords and pay a tremendous piece for the privilege. In asking for the co-operation of the Socialists and Independents' the Chancellor said that possibly the Bill was not what tiny wanted, but it was a step in the right direction. (Applause.) Mr. MARK HOWARTH (President of the Board of Trade) formally seconded the motion. Mr. E. A. WHITE (C, St. Albans). in proposing the usual Opposition amendment that the Bill be real six months hence, complimented the Chancellor of the Exchequer on having made a dry subject interesting. The Government in that lipase, be continued, appeared, to be much sounder Conservatives than the Govern- ment at Westminster. (Laughter.) There were many points in the Bill which were quite in accordance with Conservative principles although it would be better re-drafted and certain grave faults rectified. Then it IVIS possible that the Bill might pass unanimously. If it was intended that Parish Councils should assess the valued is their own parishes, it was open to grave objection, as such bodies were apt to be too much under the influence of a few individuals. It was quite possible that in some parishes one or two large property owners might get on the Council and bring about anequitible rating as between their own property and that of smaller owners who could not make their inflaence felt to the same extent. The present system of as,.esaucent was preferable. The clause dealing with municipal undertakings was sound in principle, bat there was room for considerable difference of opinion as to what amount should be net aside for depreciation, fr.c. A reserve fund should be created, or at any rate there should be a safeguard against the rates basing to make up any di flciency. The principle of direct rating of tenants was one of the soundest Conserva- tive principles he had ever heard advocated bye Radical Government. (Laughter). Ile was in sympathy with the attempt to rate ground rents, with the proviso that it should only apply to values created from the date of the passing of the 13,11. If the taxation hid to apply to all existing ground rents, it would be grossly unfair. There seemed to hen popular id.a that all ground rent receivers were wealthy men and big landlords who ought to be " got at," but quite a large number of small people had invested their savings in ground rents. Considerably more common sense had been shown by the Government in dealing with the valusiion of land, buildings and fittings than by Mr. Lloyd George, who was still struggling with the question. He had only made 300,000 valuations out 01 11,000,000 in about two</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Pills as Mr. Cawley had.</head>
        <p>le Pills as Mr. Cawley had.</p>
        <p>sears, and then they were a matter of mete gums work Ile had no objection who Let et to licensed promises and clubs being rated on the Lash a of liquor ealea pr tvided that the ill-adviked arid bray), charges imposed by Mr. Lloyd George were abolished. At the same time he objected to any inter-mixing of so-called temperance with a purely (local question. Drinking was quite as often caused by poverty as poverty wane used by drink. Ile Wes lii spapathy with the increment chore, pro- vided that the et nerd duty whieh Mr. Lloyd-George wuti attempting to levy di I not apply. He had rolwa)a thought that the local authority was the proper! authority to rem ice any increment that there might be on the sale of land. He had no objection to the acquisition of land by the municipality at 5 years' purchaae,if the average assessment for a certain number of years was to be the basis. Mr. C. Er- BARKER (L., Dundee) said Mr. 7 bite, in his moderate and in many respects wise speech, had act.nowledgerl and accepted on behalf of the party he represouted, the principle upon which the Bill was based —n irue'y, that any increase in value which came about, net through the exertions of the landoweer but as the product of the united efforts of the community, should to n gre at extent be returned to the municipality. The difficulty was that inn change of the law of that kin I they could hardly avoid being unjust to some individuate. He believed the Chancellor wou'd be willing to consider any amendment on the qtystion of land valuation which would have the effect of corrying out more gralually what both sides of the House agreed should be done. He was glad to hear that the Con- servatives had always held the principle of the 8.11. It was quite news to him—(laughter)—but if it had I been at the back of their minds until now and had at I 14.4 come to the front, he thought they would soon make progress on the land question. The acceptance of the printiple that values should be the property of those who created them made it unneceesary for him to go into details. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. C. D. PARKINSON (C., Preston) said the prevent law with reg ird to rating was that the assessment was to he based on what a place would reasonably let for, and he thought that basis should be retained. No fairer system could be devis3d. In his view the only true principle of taxation Lir imperial and municipal pur- poses was that there should be only one tax, t 3 be based on a man's iacoine. Then they would get an absolute levelling up. It should not be a question of futurity with regard to load values, but of a man's income from year to year. That was perhaps more of a Radical idea than a Conservative one. (Laughter). At the same time, he thought the man earning LI a week and the man wi.h £50,000 a year should contribute to the upkeep of the State end municipality on a graduated basis, but let there be one tax only. (Applause)' Mr. H. New (President of the Local Government Board) rejoiced to find that the Tory party were the pioneers and sponsors of the taxation of laud values. (Laughter.) Ile was not in agreement with the sjstem of one tax for all purposes, to be based on income, because men might have a vast quantity of land, place n a exceedingly low value upon it an undeveloped -land, hold it up and avoid paying the extra impost. He did not subscribe to the view that 90 per cent of poverty Was due to strong drink. If one considered the economic aspect, it was a much deeper question than that. Poverty and uoattractive environment were often the cause of drunkenness.He was glad that the clubs were not to 133 exempt from the taxation on solos of liquor, as they offered a very attractive bait in opposition to the public houses and ought to bear their share of the burdens of the country. He did not agree that the licensing trade WB3 unduly taxed under the Lloyd-George Budget, for it must be borne in mind glint the money spent in liquor was unproductive, the only apparent result accruing from it being poverty, crime and national degradation. lie regretted that the land purchase clauses of the present measure were not in force when the site of the Lovely-lane School was purchased, as the ratepayers would probably not have hod to pay such an enormous sum for it.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. MCDERM I D (C., Paisley) pointed out that the new 1111 proposed to make the relief of the poor, maintenance of workhouses, hospitals etc. dependent upon the capacity of the community to con- sume intoxicating liquors. (Laughter.) It would relieve a certain section of their obligations, for some people, from conscientious mo Cares, rigidly refused to enter licensed premises. There ought to be a clause compelling all householders or occupiers to call at the nearest public house in order that they should not, escape their share of taxation. (Laughter.) The Bill appeared to be a cleverly-designed hit at" the trade after all. (Laughter.) Mr. J. alcDoNabo (Secretary of State for India) said the fact that no damaging criticisms had been levelled at the broad principles of the Bill was indicative of the changs of view that was coming ove: the Tory party. Some of the criticisms of the member for St. Alban's commanded his sympathy. There was a real danger of I local influence prevailing to an undue extent if pro- ' polities were valued by parish cottacils. Ile was also inclined to the view that municipalities which were empowered by Parliament to tandert he trading concarna shou'd als ha all iwad, subject to certain limitations, to make a reasonable profit. It was an inducement to careful trading. The ground rent classes should only apply to I attire purchases. It seemed unfair that a man who had invested £2OO or £3OO —probably his life's savings—in ground resits, which were considered a secure investment, on the bails that they were not subject to taxation, should suddenly find a tax clapped on. With regard to the liquor traffic, its effects on the people were so harmful that he WAS in favour of taxing It until it was almost driven out of tho country. Mr. HAWTHORNS (C. B. Belfast) said there were many questions of more pressing importance than the ming of land and property. He did not think the Bill would, in the aggregate, make much difference to the amount of taxation payable by the individual. Mr. P. O'BRIEN (L., St. Pancras) pointed out that large concerns were prepared to value their buildings and plant for the purpose of their own insurance and it should be equally easy to arrive at a valuation for the levying of taxation. The tax on the landowner would not be excessive, but would tend to equalize matters between the landlord deriving capital from the land and the man who had sunk his capital sod brains in industry. As to the liquor trade, he was satisfied with the states most made by the Bishop of Liverpool the previous day, that all people agreed that the greater part of the misery, pauperism, crime and insanity in this country was due to intoxicating drink. The debate was adjourned until nest Tuesday evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>POEMS OF LUKE HAMILTON TALBOT.</head>
        <p>THE POEMS OF LUKE HAMILTON TALBOT.</p>
        <p>It is to Alderman Bennett that one's thanks are doe I for having rescued from oblivion so many of the poetic 'fancies of Luke Hamilton Talbot, late Chief . Constable of Warrington. Mr. Talbot spent eleven years in Warrington as head of the police here, and during that time his many-eidedness became known to a large number of acquaintances, while a few friends were admitted to the intimacy of his thoughts and aspirations. Little was known, pub!icly, of the late " Chief's " ventures into the realm of poetic creation, and many of the co -me which have just been publish, d thr regh tbe interest aud 'Mamma love of Alderman Bennett, e-e tho light for the fist time. there are a hundre I or so of them. and they give a drep insight into the s'range imaginings, the idealistic hopes, and the morbid fears of the was who died so tragically in far-off Egypt some four and a half Tears ago. Altlerman Bennett supplies a lengthy biographical sketch of Luke Talbot as a preface to the book, and surveys his li:erary labours with a more than kindly eye. The absence of criticism is what he has to say of a fellow-dreamer's fancies impela u, to remark that he appreciates the work of Mr. Talbot too highly. It is surely a ois-use of inperlttivea to say " Iris genius had a great and ever-growing range, and the g smut of his lyre is as wide as the world and as high as he eternal stars." fly such perfervid testim 'ay Alderman Bennett stimu- hites one's cr:tical faculty, and it is semesary to slier the view-point of this brief notice of the hook tori al as kindly as memory directs with the poetic effusions of a man whote outlcek was darkened by failing health and clii had ap, at wady, a distinct inclivat ion to the pessimistic. Much that Mr. Talbot wrote was too evidently the echo of a great, r " lyre " than his own, but it is only fair to ray tlrot doubtl,sa he laboured under no self-deceptions in the matter. Yet, there is no scarcity of fine thought, vivid imogina- firm, an I inotr:cal skill in may other of these poems. Some of then, p SiPBS inherent beauty, and few are really devoid of may appeal to the heart or iniuti They are worthy their rovers, autt &amp;serve to be erected as some memorial to the unfamiliar but chai act, ristic side of the wan who filled a prominent place ia the public life If the town, and who will always be pleasantly remembered, alike by friends and acquaintanc, s. One or two quotations from the book will give a better idea, than anything we can sly, of the quality and variety of Mr. Tat bot's poems. The first in a so. tof proem entitled "My Songs," and stiggesta that Mr. Talbot penned it with the idea of some time publishing his work.</p>
        <p>" MY SONGS." (First two verses.) Poor children of a obililess heart, At last from borne you make a start ; You carry with sou bud deport, My dreams, sad hove, and fears, and wrongs; I've dressed you in no robal of Art, But in the homespun that belongs To lowly folk, my little songs.</p>
        <p>The world may give you scorns and sneers, Conceived in sorrow, b nu to tears. Treating you like your types and peers, Puor childroo of its cluvi cities, Woose bitter cry ii in its eam From day tv day, while no man pitim : Such is the world, my little ditties.</p>
        <p>Nnt infregaeatly Mr. Talbot translated into verso the scenes and redectioas frJIII a lif:thluo speat ill close touch with the "under-world." " in Bany lon " he paints a sal, yet lurid picture :</p>
        <p>'IN BABYLON.' and sixth verses). Si. the fele of a WOlll3 yonrer, waere, spite of the desperate eyes, The light of her onildhxod lingers, like gold in the twilight sties ; A ihwer of the wayside blown from its home in the . .</p>
        <p>winds and dews To its doom in the pitiless deeps of the sweltering City's stews ; And the life Got meant an I bahioned for the glory of fruitful days Is a danger that walks in the darkness, a curse in noontide ways.</p>
        <p>She how she maree amongst them; they gither their garments is : Her sieters,whe never airflow! the terrible need to sin ! And the arimg, self-sanotillal lin Ivo s.rute with kw;</p>
        <p>shimo itagnown, And his righteoui right hand ready to smite her with</p>
        <p>foremost stone. Oh, pitiless has t of woman ! Ah, recresnt hesrt of</p>
        <p>mut ! le this how the work go-s on thst Carist in him lave begsn</p>
        <p>One of the most picturesque proluctioos, " A Cow- boy's song in Englwid " is strongly suggestive of Kipling, wham the author doubtless took as his model. Mr. Talhot's acquaintanoe with the Wild West, and some of the richest fruits of his imagination, are however, embodied in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>"TIIE COWBOY'S SONG IN ENGLAND."</head>
        <p>"TIIE COWBOY'S SONG IN ENGLAND."</p>
        <p>There's a dretry Wry a'sove me, and the bltst ir like • blade • . .</p>
        <p>, All the sombre street is s Alen with the rains ; 'Mid the aeur and solemn hurry of Lll3 work--a-day</p>
        <p>—, parade, I am yearning, lam turning to the dreams that never fade— I am pining for the West and for the plains !</p>
        <p>R6FRAIN. And its oh ! to hear ths neigh Of the Bronchos in the grey, When the calling of the cattle wakes the wonder of the day, . . . . .</p>
        <p>Oh ! to gallop in the mist, With a lariat on my wrist, Handing bitch the shAggy rovers to the lines, . . .</p>
        <p>Hustling up the lowing throng, To the cracking of the thong, As the wind is wakieg early in the pines.</p>
        <p>Oh ! the town is trim and narrow, and there really isn't room Fern little quiet practice with a " gun And the carts upon the cobbles, "Cutter-clatter " in the gloom, Make me madder, aye, and sadder than the old, familiar boom Of the Bullocks' beating Bleeder on the run. REFRAIS.</p>
        <p>So, it's oh ! to set my sails For the iinforgotten trails Where I used to round the cattle up and pen them in the rails ; „ . .</p>
        <p>Ob.! to swing the singing rope From the Broach° on the " lope," And to brand the kicsing "User that it entwine' ; Oh ! to race and win the lead, In the track of the stampede, When the wind is like a trumpet in the pines. tern thinking—where the blood is fall of centuries of cold, Where a frolic or a fight is seldom seen, 'Mid the cool sal quiet sinners so protected and controlled— Of the funning where the" gunning " was as ready as the gold, But the revelry and devilry were</p>
        <p>RIIFItA IN. Oh ! the banjos and guitars In the shouting, shacty-b trr, Ancl,the magic of the music hushing down the sudden jars ! Add its oh ! to drop asleap Where the patient planets heap Solemn vigil on the prairie as the wanderer reclines, With a blanket on the ground, Saddle-pillowod in the sound Of a drowsy wind a-dreaming in the pines.</p>
        <p>We see the po,t at his best in the simple suggestiv. " Lost Bohemia," and again in the more imaginative The Land of Manacia" (to-morrow). Lost BOHEMIA. We lived in our loved Bohemia Where it was always day; Hope and beelth were our earthly wealth, Work was its one sweet pay' • Lands or gold we hod none to hold, Clear we had little to chow; But we lived in our old Bohemia Twenty years ago. What did we care for gear or gold? lied we not youth and song, Laughter and ploy, and every day Happy as it was long? Friend nso friend to the journey's end Let him go high or low; Love wu the Ns of Bohemia Twenty years ago. Boys and girls in Bohemia Dreamed—and we did not doubt Name and fame and the World's acclaim Bosse day weuld end us o; How Of where we had little are, This was enough to know', None co ild fail in Bohemia Toetty years net,.</p>
        <p>Whore is our oil ii hema? Whera are its boys so bravo ? Where are the curie of ita goldms girl*?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <p>Friend, we are old and grave; Skies are gray, we have bad our day; Dreams—they were dumps we know; But we lived in our lost Bohemia Twenty years age.</p>
        <p>A touching tribute to the late hr. Henry Taylor (police court missionary) who died suddenly, August 26th, 190tt is paid in "In Idemoiism," the first and last verses of which are IN MEMORIAM.</p>
        <p>Peace !—it is well with him—yea—pessiog well, Called hence in baste as God s beloved guest, To sit at noond ty in the Goose of Reet While we toil even to the vesper hell. Soldier of Christ—the record nerds no glees ; Galled from the fighting line by swift commmd, To be requited by the King's tight band, " For Valour," with the glory of the Cross.</p>
        <p>" Poignantly significant," to Alderman Bennett toys, is the lest selection in the book : A CASE OF FELO DE BE. One eay before God's Mercy Seat A Soul stood suddanly—unbidden, And laid before the All-Father's feet. The sorrow that in Time was hidden.</p>
        <p>On Earth below men railed above Duet that had spurned the boon of breath ; Wit', lips that wronged their Writ of Love They damned him by the deed of Death. But Clod, in Ills Eternal City, Wroth to behold the ehameful sight, Bade the great Angel of His Pity Clothe the unbioden soul in white. When they who wrought the thing He loathed, With brutal rite and small decree, St and naked spirits to be clothed What colour will their garments be ?</p>
        <p>" The rooms of Luke Hamilton Talbot," salecte I and arranged by Arthur Bennett; Sui, rise l'ubli hing Co ,1911, price 31. tit As frontispiece there is au excellent autographed portrait of the late Mr. Talbot. The book is clearly printed and neatly bound in grten cloth. • AMBULANCE WORK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>" A National Asset.' THE CROSTIELD SHIELD COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>" A National Asset.' THE CROSTIELD SHIELD COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The Credit,ld Shield presented a few years ago for competition among the members of No. 4 division of the St. John Ambulance Brigade, has undoubtedly been a means of stimulating intermit in ambulance work. This was again amply proved on Haturday by the fact that 9 teams from all puts of the division attended Messrs. Cr.:m(l9lth to compete for the shiel 1 and other prikea offered by them. District Wet Superintendent Garnett (Burnley) judged the stretcher work and Ur Pooley the viva voce. the awards were as follow : Horwlch 113 points ; Crompton 157 pow s ; Nelson 155; Warrington, 116 ; Northwich, 142 ; Hindiey, 125; Crewe 108 • Burnley, 103 and Lancaster 83. Iforwich were also the a inners of the Shield last year. • . .</p>
        <p>The shield and prizes were di AriOuteck by Mr. Haalam,a director of Mews. Crush. II t S mu. Prior to doing., he delivered abort address in the course of which he said he obtained his medallion in Ma, and went through. special nursing and military course in Australia, at the time of the Spanish-American war, and volunteered fur service in the Philippines to assist the natives who were suffering no much from ths luck of medical attention. (Applause.) Peace was declared before he could reach the scene of action, so he did not have experience of ambulauob work on the battlefield, but he had sees in mines and elsewhere the harm that could be done by kind-horned, but untrained, people endeavouring to assist the injured and making the injury icflaitely worse than it was to begin with. Oa the other hand an ambulance min out of practice was little better than an ignorant person and the principal aim of the Brigad 3 and of those compaati-</p>
        <p>Bona was to keep men in perfect training. Ambulance men should be ready to take their places if war did break out in this ceuntry, ant so be in a posi- tion to alleviate the awf id sufferings that war entailed. He was proud ta think that of the amoulance men belonging to his firm eleven belonged to the Naval Sick Berth Reserve and eleven to the Military Home Hospitals Reserve. lie was glad to hear that the Government were doing mote to enoourage them to take up reserve work ant to become thoroughly efficient in it. He had been struck by the admirable manner in which they had all miquitted themselves that afternoon and the military-like precision with which they went through all their movements. (Applauee). Mr. Haslam then presented the shield to the Harwich team, at the sane time handing each man a handsome , clock. Crompton as the second team were awarded barometers and Nelson, third, meat carvers. In proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Minim, Cstenet TRIMBLE (deputy-commissioner of No. 4 district), said that tha shield and prises offered by his firm and the excellent facilities they gave each year for the holding of the competition had done much to sustain interest in ambulance work in this district. The improvement that had taken place in discipline and appearance was most marked, and he expressed his jay at the smart soldier-like parade he had witnessed that day. Mr.Usslam was right in saying that not many of their men belonged to the reserves, but in justice to the men it should be borne in mind that on j Ailing the Brigade they practically said they were willing t 3 turn out whenever the Governmmt required them to do so. It should not he forgotten that during the South African war, 2,003 of their man went to serve their country at the front. (Applause.) Th 3 increased (sanitise whioh the Government were now giving for training in the reserves would result in more joining, and if the Government U3Ol onmmon sense they would have a fine elucient reserve in a very short time The Brigade was rendering a service to the country, at no expense, the magnitude of which was not appreciated If the R A.M.C. were called out of the country, the Brigade is three days could fill every place in the military hospitals. (Applause). A body with the existing orgianisation to d 3 that should be recognized by the Government and should receive local support wherever a branch of the Brigade existed. They bad only done middling in the formation of voluntary aid detachmenta in No. 4 district. Had the brigade been consulted in the first instanne they could have carried these detachments into effect, but the Red Cross organizations were in some pl ices occupying the same ground as they, sapping the support the Brigade ought to he receiving, so that sea matter of self-defence the Brigade should, wherever posusible, be active In the Formation of voluntary aid detachments. (Applause). District Chief Supetintoodent GARNETT seconded the motion which was heartily carried, asd three cheers were accorded to Mr. Hallam. On the motion of Captain WARING, chief superintendent of the Warrington Corps, seconded by Superintendent RO4l (Croslisld Division) the judges were thanked for their services, ANNUAL MEETING Or THE WARRINGTON</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>"cdEiP;:</head>
        <p>"cdEiP;:</p>
        <p>The beadquartere divffirin of the Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade held its annual meeting at the Polies Station wader the presidency of Chi.f Superiatendent Waring. There were also present: —Dr C Law, Dr .1 S Manson (hon siimeon), Mr 8 Baldry (superintendent), Mr II T Williams (first officer), and Mr A ti Spann (bon secretary). Meson. Ilankinson and Milford were re-elected auditor., and were heartily thanked for their past servioer. Messrs. Haw' on and Glenton were elected on the Icommittee. It was proposed by Mr. IiANKINiON, and seconded by Mr. B. OtteaN, that the hon. secretary, Mr. A. I. Spann, be nominated for the appointment of second officer, in place of Mr. P. Taylor resigned. Privates Smith, Lee and Ilankineon were nominated for promotion to corporal. The lion. Secretary's annual report stated that the work of the division for the session 1910-11 had been of s strenuous nstureand it woe pleasing to re yard that it had been a susectsful year. Represent otivee d,r national public duty had been ,cot both to the London t.loronation ceremony and also to the Carnarvon Investiture, and the report from th 3 Deputy Commis- sioner spoke very highly of the smart and military appearance of th 3 Me 3 and on the creditable manner they performed their ambulance duties. Toe annual inspection Was conducted last Msy, by Mr. W . S. Woodcock, D.S.S., and an excellent report cun from headquarters. In the c mime of his remarks after the inspection, Mr. Woodcock strongly urged the member. to join the S.J.A.B. Companies whio3 are being formed to carry out duty on the field in the case of invasion. It was pleasing to report that 38 members hat expressed their willingness to join this voluntary detachment. Thsre were still &amp;Soot 20 short to make up a full comp ony, sod urgently appealed to those who had not vet joined to become attached to show the public of Warrington that althou its the Brigade gener- ally had been sighted by tits War Ofll3o, they Meat to be to the fore and were not lacking in tholr duty to sufferiog humanity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
