      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>eff--et atia.ll,lN"?E', 6 A 111LEFIC</head>
        <p>eff--et atia.ll,lN"?E', 6 A 111LEFIC</p>
        <p>NOTES BY " OYALIST."</p>
        <p>S 3 far, Warrington have not done badly, and with a beater record than Oldham, the members most admit that the team has more than realised expectations. It still suffers born an outstanding weakness at centre- threequarter, but in other departments the players have approached same of the highest standards of play in the history of the dub. If the main defect could bs remedied, then it is conceivable that the Wirepnllers would once again make their name a power in the land.</p>
        <p>To-day, Warrington have their first taste of cup-tie football, and they will not be without strong supporters in the first round for the Lancashire Cup at Widnes. Since I saw the Chemicals crushed by the Australians I have not had quite the same respect for their prowess, and notwithstanding the result of last year's engagements between the rival teams, I think Warringtnn have a fair chance of carrying cif the honours to-They. Warrington ought to have the pull forward and half lyick, and this should be enough to</p>
        <p>_ turn the scales. That the match will he a Cable • royal there is no doubt whatever, and the team that tries hardest and lasts longest will win.</p>
        <p>The Wirepullers are Er aking another experiment at centre and one that ia fully justified under the cir- cumstances. Taylor returns to the line, and the other position has been given to Woodburn, who has physique and pace to qualify him for centre. Whether he has the nEcessary knoa ledge of the centre game remains to be proved. The young Ulverston wing! has so far served for want of opportunities on the Warrington team, but in the middle he should have plenty of scope for displaying the best football he has in him. Here's success to the new centre! May he prove just the man wasted!</p>
        <p>The Warringtqn team will be : Tilley, full back ; Waywell, Woodburn, Ta3lr, and Brathhaw, three- quarters; Brooks and Creevey, half-backs ; Johnston, Thomas, Shogars, Ch,sters, Arnold and Brocklehurst. forwards.</p>
        <p>Widnes have selected their strongest available tnm, the only absentee being If. Taylor. who has not yet recovered from his injury three weeks ago. The team will be : —Jones; Reed, Lally, TAWS, Swinton ; Nom, o.G.tra ; Aspey (0), Aspsy Johns)°, Lloyd, Drearthy and Rigby. Reserve, 0. Evans.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday's math was a credit to the Warrington men, Ig• they had a comptratively weak team in the field against the Cup-holders - who were generally fancied—and yet won handsomely. It is a long time since Broughton Rangers won at Wilderspool and like most clubs they find points hard to get here. Warrington started off with a rare rattle, and as the result of C eevey's genius for attack they were very soon eight punts to the good, the half back and Waywell ea-rh scoring very fine tries. Later, Bradshaw, gg a third in a fashion which was emphasis of bin greatly improved form, and Warrington tuned round with a score of 13 points to two points in their favour.</p>
        <p>The Rangers altered their tactics somewhat in the •second half and made the game a forward one. The Broughton six put in some hefty work, which substantiated their claim to be the best pack in the Union, and twice the home defence fell, but they kept a five points margin to the end. Creevey was suffering from a bad knock in this half, and originated few attacking movements, but in the burly burly near the home line none did better than Thomas, Johnston, Arnold and Brooks. The " skipper put more vim into his play than f ir some time past, but he has not found his scoring form yi t. The Rangers are more of a capable than a great side, and their combination is nothing (co boast of. The two wings. Scott and Bouch, are worth making openings for, but the centre play is indifferent. There as a great dearth of class centres in the N.U. to-day.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows: For. Against. Per- P. W. Lft G. T. P. G. T. P. c'nege Wipe I 7 0 0... 24 28 132... 3 7 27 10000 Halifax 7 5 0 2.. 9 19 75... 6 7 33 03.71 Hull K. It— 6 5 1 0... 21 14 81... 5 3 19 81•33 Hudderslrld 7 5 1 1... 20 31 142... 8 12 52 78.57 Widnes 'Wakefield Y 6 4 1 1... 15 24 102... 6 5 27 7500 Broughton 6 4 2 0... 11 8 46... 8 9 43 66.60 St. Helens 6 4 2 0... 11 21 85... 7 8 38 6616 Hunelet 7 4 2 1... 18 20 96... 12 7 45 61.28 Warrington 8 5 3 0... 9 14 60... 12 17 75 6250 Keighley Rochdale H 7 4 3 0... 13 18 83... 9 20 78 57.14 Oldham 8 4 3 1... 5527 111... 19 25 113 5725 Dewsbury 6 3 3 0... 812 52.. 1117 73 5000 York 6 3 3 0... 13 8 50... 14 16 76 5360 Salford 7 3 3 1... 7 18 68... 14 15 73 1000 Leeds 7 3 4 0... 9 19 75... 15 21 93 42.85 Burrow 7 2 4 1... 5 14 53... 11 13 61 3571 Runcorn Hull 9 3 6 0... 18 30 126... 16 18 66 3323 Batley 'Leigh 7 2 5 0... 12 14 66... 13 16 74 28'57 Swinton 7 2 5 0... 6 9 39... 12 12 60 2827 'Coventry Bromley Ebbw Vale 7 0 6 1... 7 7 35... 23 34 148 714 Bradford</p>
        <p>Papakura was a disappointment oa Saturday. After his fine display at fall-back for the "A" team I expectel he would do himself justice for the seniors, but the centre position was obviously unsuited to him. The pace of the game, too, showed that he regnires plenty of training before he can expect a place in first- class company. Two things everybody admires about the Maori are his good temper and enthusiasm for the game. In addition he is a brainy player and when he gets that spare ten lbs. or so removed he should justify bin reputation. lam glad to see that he is down once more to play for the " A" team.</p>
        <p>The "A" team at home against Prestwich will be: Papakura, fall-back; Clare, Unaworth, Vernon and Povey, three-quarters; Daintith and Jordan, half-backs; Cooper, Newton, Tranter, Critchley. Hamm and Naylor forwards. The new men are all local juniMS, who are having a trial.</p>
        <p>The Australians are still winning their matches and potting their method, severely criticised. On :Saturday they won at St. Helena and on Wednesday they defeated the chosen of England on the Associa- tion ground at Fulham, London. It is rather inter- eresting to read what the London par ors have to say of the ma.ch. The " Morning Leader" and "Daily Chronicle "both publish beg reports and make some pungent criticisms.</p>
        <p>" Cornithian " in the " Daily Chronicle " says in the course of an interesting account of the match : " As played yesterday, this Northern Union game is a mixtu•e of all kinds of wrestling with boxing—an occasionally very iretty bit of football being thrown in to excuse the rest. As a spectacle I think the Northern Union game can be an improvement upon the Rugby Union game, but I think it will be neces- sary to confine it to players who are out for the game, and not t 3 men who will get so much more money by winning."</p>
        <p>" Astral "in the '' Morning !Paler "gives nearly a column to the game, and remarks that it was a revelation to adherents of the old game. "It is a game for giants and not for weaklings . . . A Soccer professional's game is a mere holiday com- pared with that d his Noithern Union confrere. As for the ordinary Ilugby Union team I do not think they would last half-as-hour at the pace which is set." Alluding to the vigours of the name, the same writer says : Tripping and fisting off were common yester- day. and one of the Colonial backs, after tnrowing his man with a terrisc thud, proceeded to wind him with a short arm jolt, but was not ordered off. As a game in itself there is nothing wrong with the Northern 'Union game, but some of the regrettable incidents at Fulham suggest that • preliminary course of the 20ft ring under the Queensberry rules might be advisable."</p>
        <p>" The Dispatch" relates some very foul incidents, but says the fouling was started by the English players. "The Daily News" makes no refereace to rough play.</p>
        <p>Next Wednesday, on the ground of the Tiverton club at Gocdieon Park, the Austtalians will meet a elide representing the Ncrthern League, and the fol-</p>
      </div>
