      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Warrington Beat the Cup-Holders.</head>
        <p>Warrington Beat the Cup-Holders.</p>
        <p>a The visit of the Cup-holders, Broughton Range,s, was a great attraction at IVilderapool on Saturday, and fully 7,000 spectators assembled. Interest was increased also by the first appearance for the senior team of Papakura, the Maori beer, who took the place of Taylor at centre.The absence of Woodburn necessitated the appearance of Waywell for the first time this season. Brorighton were at full strength. Beautiful weather prevailed, and a fast and exciting game was witnessed. Teams and officials : Warlington—Tilley, back : Weywell, LTneworth, Papakura and Bradrhaw, threequarters ; Creevey and Broaks, half-backs ; Johnston, Thomas, Sbugars, Cheaters, Arnold and Brocklehurst, forwards. Broughton Rangers—Davidson, back ; Bosch, Wild, I Harris and Scott, threequarters ; Barlaw and Jones, half-backs ; J L Clampitt, R Clanopitt, Winskill, Hirst, Beetham and Gorry, forwards. Referee, Mr Wood, Huddersfield ; touch-judges Mess a H Ogden, Oldham, and A Lee, Leigh. Warrington won the toss and started the game full of confidence and dash. The Broughton full-back was soon busily employed, and a 11 te return by Tilley established the game at the Broughton end. Here the home forwards showed their metal and secured I possession of the hall. Brooks broke away and passed to Creevey, who drew the defence on to Wayw all before cutting in in delightful fashion and scoring near the posts amidst tremendous cheering. Thomas added a goal, and the locals were in high glee. The Rangers made a spasmodic burst, whilst a rather dangerous pass by Pepakura gave Tilley • warm handful. A good breakaway by Brooks was well seconded by Arnold and Shugars, and the burly Welshman ploughed his way through a ruck of players until half-way was reached. Creevey supplemented matters when he kicked judiciously, and then came a pretty effort by Bradshaw, who lacked land to put on the requisite finishing touch. However, hot scrimmages were the order at the Broughton end, and though Unsworth tried to get through he found the tackling tea accurate to allow of any liberties. Creevey got possession again, and with an artistic cross-kick put Waywell in possession. The henna° favoured the IVarringtonian, who exhibited a fine turn of speed, and notched • second try at the corner. Thomas this time missed the goal. The "Wirepallers" again showed splendid form on resuming and Arnold and Thomas ware ever conspicuous in 83.110 robust work by the home vanguard. Wheeling several packs the Broughton forwards negatived an advantage gained by Bro .kn, and eventually the ball woo worked across to Bauch, who had a clear field until he reached Tilley. The latter caused him to swerve, and then Waywell came up with a rattle and downed the speedy. Ranger a yard from the line. The Wirepulters, ' however, were penalised, and though the angle was an extreme one, Davidson landed a goal. The le iders immediately went to the pavilion end and clover play by Brooks and Creavey again placed the Broughton men on the defence. As the game neared the interval so did Warrington force matters effectively, and although Davidson and Scott held the opposition at bay with commendable off ode, the visitors were unable to cheek B:adshaw who, securing the ball at half-way speedily made tracks along the touch line, and following his own kick rased round the posts for Thomas to goal again. Just before the interval Creevey was hurt. and proved of little use afterwards. Half time : Warangtan 13 points ; Broughtcn 2 points. TurnMg round, the v'siting forwards rushed the packs to mach purpose that the game was quickly transferred to the Warrington end, where Jones and Barlow almost dodged through in quick 1111000133i00. Maintaining the attack, the visitors were not long before they crossed, and the try was the outcome of a smart handling movement in which Wild, Jones and Scott shone, the scorer being the wing mai. Davidson missed the goal. With Creevey bock, the game took a more even turn, but there was an ab once of the many bright episodes which had characterized the initial half. The Warringtonians failed to take advantage of a faulty piece of fielding by D ividson The attack was once more realized by the visitors, and desperate play by Beetham and J. R. Clampitt wee checked by Tilley, the home custodian showing remarkable c manes,. Thomas and Johnston forced matters at half-way, but another penalty gave the visitors the advantage, and after Pepakura had got assay he lost possession, and the ball was fielded by Bouch, who showed splendid speed and concluded a fine effort near the corner. Davidson again missed the goal. The closing stages of the game were fought out with grim determination Broughton showing dashing play in the forwards and repeatedly threatening the home goal. Tilley and Brooks and Thomas eased the pressure repeatedly, and ia the last few minutes the " Wirepullers " beds chance if increasing their lead, but knocked on at the critical moment. Result : War- rington, two goals three tries (13 points) ; Broughton Rangers, one goal two tries (eight points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN V. BRADFORD NORTHERN.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN V. BRADFORD NORTHERN.</p>
        <p>..... In visiting Irvvell-lane, Bradford bad some hopes of being able to obtain their first win of the season, but they found the Cheshire men in a very determined mood. The "Linneta" were the first to score, as the result of a good c3mbined movement, Padbury getting over at the corner, for Richardson to miss the goal. Th 3 visitors were not long before they were ahead, as the result of a good try by Griffon. who also added the extra points. Moran win conspicuous, and scored beautifully, after bluffing several opponents, and Richardson this time made no mistake. Town, who fielded what was intended to have been a dropped goal, easily ran in a few mintres later, and equalised the score, no goal points resulting. Again Runcorn went ahead, as the result of an individual effort by Massey, who ran over and scored an unimproved try. Toe interval then arrived with the scor3 in favour of Runcorn by It points te lathe second half there was really only one team in it. Futther.tries were added in this half by Watton, Peacock, and Massey, none of thr.33 being improved, but Richardson added, good penalty goal in the last few minutes. Result : Runcorn 2 goals G tries (22 points), Bradford 1 goal 2 tries (8 points). WIDNES WIN BY A POINT.</p>
        <p>At Cavendish Park, Barrow, before 3,500 spectators. The game was fast throughout, and the slight advant- age held by Widnes in the first portion, through good combination, was neutralised by Barrow's constant aggression in the second prrtion. In the initial half Widnes scored twice through Reid and S. Aspey and Jones kicked two splendid goals. Mitchell broke through, and put in a short kink, and Hogg gathered the ball and scored for Barrow, the ume player failing at goal. Interval score :—Widnes 10 points. Barrow 3 points. In the second half, Dobson wan off the field for a short time owing to injury, Blacklock broke away, passed to Hogg, and the latter transferred to Gifford whl scored. Hogg Again missed the goal. Treloar next gathered near touch, and scored a third try, Irogan's kick at goal being futile. Widnes 2 goals 2 tries (10 points), Burrow 3 tries (9 points).</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS V. AUSTRALASIA. THE COLONIALS STILL UNBEATEN.</p>
        <p>The visit of the Colonial team to St. Helens on Saturday, was favoured v,ith ideal weather, a crowd of 12,000 being present to witness • display which was full of exciting incidents from beginning to end, and which was devoid of the rowdy element which was no rampant at Widnes. Play was vigorously fought, and as a result of kicking exchanges which favoured the " Saints," Fraser kicked into Greenwood's handr, end the latter opened the score with a dropped goal. Thus Imbued, St. Helens again invaded the Colonials quarters where Trenwi lb bluffed Broomhant, and banded to Flanagan, who paved the way for Prescott to score a try by eluding Farnsworth. Turtill failed. It was nearing the interval when Ilallett got away, and trans- ferred to Berecry, who crossed near the posts, for Russell to improve. Half-time score :—St. Helens, I goal and 1 try (5 points) ; Australasia, 1 goat and 1 try (5 points). Changing ende, St. Helens were at once dangerous, and from a free kick Molynenx just failed to land goal. The play was of a high standard, the home backs being prominent in subduing the vigorous onslaughts of the Colonials. It was in the last quarter when the Saints", staying powers were taxed, llclielvitt slipping away and giving to Craig, who raced over at the corner. Russell fulled. Continuing to force matters Hallett handed to Russell, who beat the opposi- tion, and after running half the length of the field ioored under the posts, and also kicked a goal. St. Helens, through 51avitty and Greenwood, got dangereus the latter crossing, but tte try was disallowed. lathe last minute of the game Craig forced his way over the line, tossell louis,. Result :—Australasia 2 goals and 4 tries (16 points„ St. Helens 1 goal and 1 try (5 points.)</p>
      </div>
