        <p>—...... . --.....— The draw for the sixth round is detailed below. The final will be reached on Saturday afternoon and the prizes will be distributed at the club's headquarters on Saturday, November 4th. Two o'clock.</p>
        <p>.1 Plompton, Grapp'hell 6 II Brornley, Longford 4 F Rice, Corporation 4 B Jennuar, Crostielde 5 E Blackledge, Rylends 1 Albert Gleave,Wcross in. J Ashton, Firth 1 A (Heave, Whymans... 1 Alfred (heave, P. and K 4 it Savory, Ryland, ... 3 W Ball, Whitecrosa G Melia,Monks 4 W Andrews, Orford ... 1 W Langley, Rylanda S T Clare, Rylande 2 THE FINAL OF THE WORKS ASSOCIATION HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>...... ___.._. The annual handicap promoted by tbe Works Bowling Association will be completed to•day (Saturday) on the green attached to the Horse and Jockey Inn. The nib round and semi•final Save to be played as well, so spectators may be assured of good sport in the bid for 1 the cup. Captain George Crnstleld has promised to I present the Cup The prizes are to be distributed at a I smoking concert to be held on Saturday November 4th I in the club room at the Horse and Jockey Ina.</p>
        <p>KING AND QUEEN CLUB. This popular club held its closing main of the season on Saturday. The club has made good progress this Selllsoll. lime semi-finalists were M S Riley, jun., E Maskell. A Jolley sod H Bighorn. S Riley, jun., handsomely defeated E Maskell. The game between Messrs Jolley sod Ifigham was very keenly contested and sinl les prevailed. Jolley defeated Eliftram, who is one of the best local players, by one point, after the best contested game which hes ever been witnessed on the King and Queen green. In the final Jolley, who in the individual merit competition defeated the ex-champion, Mr G Ball, Jon, had an easy win over S Riley, jun. The club has had a capital lessen, the membership for the first time having attained three- figure proportions. Mr JIV Davies has performed his duties as secretary very admirably daring his first: year of office.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>COMPULSORY MILITARY TRAINING.</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY MILITARY TRAINING.</p>
        <p>LORD. DERBY'S TRIBUTE TO LORD HALDANE.</p>
        <p>The Risk of Invasion.</p>
        <p>It was a happy inspiration on the part of the War- rington branch of the National Service League to secure the services of the Perfection Soap Works , Band for their public meeting in the Parr Hall on Wednesday evening. The band is always a great attraction but interest in its appearance on the present occasion was accentuated by the recent memorable triumph in the national contest and the announcement that the Crystal Palace test piece would be played during the evening. The combined War- rington halo Voice and Warrington Apollo choirs were also in attendant* to entertain the assembly with choruses and put songs before the meeting began. It would, of course, be unfair to attribute the crowded state of the ball entirely to the musical fare provided, but there can be little doubt that that factor had greater weight with the majority of the audience than a desire to listen to speeches on National Service. The promoters of the meeting appeared to appreciate that fact, for they judiciously reserved the test pitce until the close of the proceedings and checked a tendency to restlessness in the gallery by a well-timed reminder of the treat that was to come. When Lord Derby, in opening the meeting, expressed surprise at the splendid attendance, an obliging "voice "laconically interjected " It's the band."</p>
        <p>Shortly after seven o'clock the nores.rved parts of the hall were filled to overfhwing, and the doors had to be closed. Notic sof an overflow meeting in the Co-operative hall were displayed, and it was intimated that 'the champim band will play." For al hour the band and combined choirs delighted the Parr Hall audience with a choice programme, many of the items being of a patriotic and martial character. The hall was. decorated with flags and streamers.</p>
        <p>The meeting was fresided over by Lord Derby, president of the Lancashire County Branlll of the League, and the other principal speakers were : AdMral Penrose Fitzgerald, formerly second in command of China Station, and Mr A G Carruthers, a Radical workingman from Bedford. Mr G H Muir, a Liberal leader writer from Manchester and a student of Christ Church, Oxford, was expected but sent a message that he was detained in London. Amongst those present on the platform were :—The Mayor and Mayoress (Councillor Dr and Miss Joseph), Mr 0 F Shea (formerly secretary of the N.S.L. and author of " A Briton's first duty "), Mrs T II Lyon, Colonel and Mrs Edrlsten, Councillor and Mrs H Roberts, Captain G ft Crosfield (hon.sccretary of the Lancashire County branch),Rev Canon and Mrs Willis,Rev Canon Blogg, Mr and Mrs B S Townroe, Miss S Broadbent, Mr Brercton Fairclough, Mr W Peter Rylands and Mr E J Hail.</p>
        <p>L•qtera cf regret for non-attendance were received from Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall, Lord Wolmer, Lady Acton, Mr and Mrs J Cl.a rlton Parr, Mr Harold Smith, M.P., Mr A H Croafield, Mr J Ifarmood Banner, M.P.. and Mrs Banner, Mr and Mre H Greenall.</p>
        <p>LORD DERBY'S TRIBUTE TO LORD HALDANE. Lord DERBY replied to the speech made in the same hall last year by Lard Haldane. " There is no man" be said " for whom I have a greater respect than Lord Haldane. (Applause). Although I run abs riately opposed to,him in politics, I can say with perfect truth that I bare worked with him in every way in my power to carry forward the scheme which be has at heart. I consider that the</p>
        <p>- - UTMOST CREDIT IS DUE TO HIM for having so far advanced the organization of our army as to pot it imam infinitely superior position, to my way of thinking, thin it ever occupied before. (Applause.) Therefore, you will clearly understand that any criticisms I may make are not directed against him or aga'net his poi icy, but are only brought for a ard with a view, if possible, of still further improving on his saheme." (Rear, hex'.) ' First of all, continued Lord Derby, he would like to knock on the head one thing with regard to the National Service League which existed in the minds of many, and which he kne y existed in the mind of Lord Haldane himself, and that was that the National Service League was in any way opposed to</p>
        <p>TTIE TERRITORfACiORCES.</p>
        <p>Whatever it might be in other parts of the country, and he did not think it existed anywhere, he Nia9 perfectly certain the feeling did not exist in Lincashire. Those associated with the National Service League in the county had endesvoured in every way to help those who, like himself, were engaged in Territorisl work. (Hear, hear.) Tne most energetic man they hai in the Lesgua in this district, Captain CroslNd, stituvad in himself Jo h.% Iverriii of the swum, ion, I r it w tawhether he was a keener Ter'atorial is"r National Service man. (Applause.) If he thought the National Service League was in any way oppused to the Territorial Association he would resign from the Lsague within tastily-four hours. (Applause.) We wanted still further to Lund on the foundstions the Territorials hod pot in, in order that we might have a rampart against any foe that might p ssibly come against us. (Applause.) Having said that, be would put in the inevitable "hub" about the Territorials, and the " but" was—ls the training they hove sufficient to fit them for the work they might have to undergo, and are their numbers sufficiently large ? The first was a delsaleible point, but the second, to his mind, wan undehateable. He candidly admitted that he never had sympathised with those people who had run down the training of the Territorials an being worse than nothing. He did not think they had ever given credit to the rnen for doing what he and everybody else connected with the Territorials knew bad been done—that was that men did not stick at the minimum number of drills and they put in far more work than the minimum required of them. The man who put in, ns they did sometimes, three times the number of drills required of him and who served fifteen darn in camp was on the high road to becoming a thoroughly efficient soldier. Ile (Lord Derby) would never have accepted the doctrine laid down by Lord Haldane that the Territorial would be is thoroughly-efficient soldier, capable of meeting the best troops of the Continent, were it not for one thing which had been said over and over again—that an expeditionary force should not leave these shores until the Territorials had been embodied and had had Sk months' hard training. That, being more than the National Service Leagus asked for, would have qualified the men to be considered thoroughly efficient soldiers. But twelve months ago Viscount Haldane said</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>OUR RE.LATITNS WITH GERMANY – • •</head>
        <p>OUR RE.LATITNS WITH GERMANY - • •</p>
        <p>.... We thoroughly friendly, and he (the speaker) wished he could say the same now, because on the maintenance of friendly relations with Germany depended the preservation of peace throughout the world. But those who read the newspapers knew that probably no two big nations were so nearly being at war, without actually going to war, as Germany, France, and ourselves we's saitkis the last six weeks. They saw how coalitions changed within twelve months. There was a persistent rumour that our Government was prepared to send abroad the whole of our expeditionary force, not within six months, but within six days of mobilisation. That in itself was a great achievement for Lord llaldana and his organisation and War Office officials, but, mark this, if it were true, it absolutely destroyed that part of his argument which said that the expeditionary force would not be sent out of the country until the Territorials hart been embodied for sit months. If the rumour were untrue, he had nothing further to any ; if it were true he thought that they hai a right to say that the state of affairs had changed and ta ask Lord Haldane whether he was prepared to give them sows- thinf in its place (Applause). With regard to the question of numbers, he wanted to compare the Territorials in Lancashire with the old Volunteer Forces. In the latter there were 932 officers and 29,718 men, but in the county at the present time the Territorials possessed only 894 °filters nod 29,431 men. Toey must remember, of course, that the</p>
        <p>STANDARD lIAD BEFN SCRRVED yr,</p>
        <p>and efficers and men had to undergo a much Mo-e rigid test before they were even accepted for the Force. Ile did not think so much about the reduction in numbers. but the two sets of figures went to show that we had got down to the very bedrock cf recruiting enter a voluntary system. (Applause). Then there was, generally speaking, a falling off in that part of Lancashire in the nuruber of men attending camp for the full 15 days, which meant that they had got to the bedrock of willingness of employers to let their men come under the voluntary system. hear). The conduct of workingmen who were willing to sacrifice their time at the close of a bad day's work to make themselves elli.dent was worthy of all praise, hut he was disappointed with the number of officers, because he believed that there were many young men who had time and leheire on their hands, who ought to come forward and take their p sitions in the commissioned ranks of the Territorial Forces. (Applause). Lord Haldsee's cantentios was that the scheme of the National Service Leapt(' would stop recruiting for the Regular Army, but he believed the effect would be jint the opposite. War Office figures proved canclattively that a greater percentage of men went direct from the Territorials to the Regular Army than ever went in the old days from the Volunteers. He wanted to see</p>
        <p>F.YERY NIAN PUT ON AN NI:AL FOOTING.</p>
        <p>........ . - He would allow ample exemptions, as was d me abroad but there wart one exemption that unier no circum- stances would he ever allow—the exemption that could be purchased by money. Hear, bets.) If • man,having mooey, did not choose to fit bimself by extra study to</p>
        <p>take an ocer's commission, he the speaker', would net allow his money to excuse him from serving his country, and he would make him serve in the ranks like any workingman. (Applsuse.) Lord Derby con- tradicted the argument that compulsory trvining would provoke militarism, for he ventured to say there WU 113 truer statement than that the mam who had seen war never wanted to see it again. Ile (Lord Derby) wanted to see th 3 Territorial forces made up to the absolute minimum which bad been lined by Lord Haldane. At the present moment,which mg ht be called the boom of the Territorials, the force in Lencashire alone was 15 per cent, abort of that mini- mum. He believed Nation 1 Service would add to the physique and health of the men and secure for us the safety of the Empire. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>AN ADMIRAL'S CRITICISMS.</head>
        <p>AN ADMIRAL'S CRITICISMS.</p>
        <p>Admiral FITZGERALD /1180 took Lord Haldane to task with regard to his assertion that compulsory training would interfere with recruiting in the regular army. Was it not ridiculous to assume that a man would not volunteer to do a thing he could do, and I that be would volunteer for something he could not do ? Dii anyone suppose that if we had had universal training when the S. African war broke out, those men who volunteered, without being any use when they got there, would have been lets likely to volunteer If they bad known a soldier's duty ? It was asking us to believe an absurdity. We might, as Loud Haldane said, haves terrifically powerful fl set in ' the North Sea, but if it was to be kept in the North Sea, how was it going to defend our Empire? In the past our Navy was not confined to the North Sea but was in every quarter of the glole. The crux of the whole question wan: If we bad not got an army sufficient to repel an invasion, we mast keep the great bulk of onr navy in the North Sea, doing what he con- sidered to be the duty of the army. There was some- thing undignified, if not shameful, in a great country like England being in such a position that the Minister for War bad to stand up and nay "There is no danger of more than 70,000 men landing on your coasts, and I believe I should be able to repel them with my Territorial Army of five to one." Was that the position for a great Empire to be in ? We were afraid, for we knew Hatt our position was that we dire nlt let our fleet go more then 48 hours sail from the North San. We bad ample proof, concluded the Admiral, enthusiasm in war did not make up for lack of tr that discipline and Anse soldierly qualities which could alone be learnt by steady applicstion to the drills and exercises that soldiers had to undergo. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>SOCIAL REFORM ASPECT.</head>
        <p>THE SOCIAL REFORM ASPECT.</p>
        <p>Mr. C UttIOTHERS, of Raiford, said that, Radical as he was, he had long been a convinced believer in the aims and objects of the National Service League. (Applause.) He greatly deplored the divisions which at present tainted between rich and poor, employer and employed in this country. That condition of things was due to the fact that the worker i 3 not understand the master, and the master, in many Cases, looked with suspicion on the man. There was a great gulf between them. Under compulsory service both classes would serve together for four mouths and they would get to know and understand each other batter. (Applause.) Frankly he did not believe that four months training would be Euificient to make good soldiers. He wanted to begin at an earlier period—in the elementary schools—s that young children could be trained for that discipline which a great statesman once arid was the great need of the English people. (Applause.) Then when the time came for them to join the Territorial army they would be better fitted to serve efficiently in it. He believed that under com- pulsory eervice there would be awl a showing op of the</p>
        <p>SEAMY SIDE OP THE SOCIAL QUESTION</p>
        <p>that slams would be impossible in our great cities, a different public opinion would be created and men would come to understand things as they had never done before. (Applause.) It would be practically impossible for hooligans and loafers to exist under a compulsory system. At present we had no means of training those who were regarded as unfit to take a proper part in life. The National Service Lsagu 3's scheme held the field, and he challenged any mon or woman prxsent to tell him of a better sycem. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the Exemtive Committee for the following year consist of Mr T 11 Lyon, J.P., D.L president; Mr ti Roberts, J.P., chairmen of the com- mittee ; Mr E Ball, Miss S Broadbent, the Rev W Braceovmp, Mr U Dale, Mr Arthur Dawn, kfraEdelsten, Mr J Elwell, Mr Aylmer Frost, J.P. Mr E J 11.11, Mrs Lyon, Mr W Mason, Mr and Mrs B S Townroe, Mr.] H Smethurst, Mr L Voisey and Captain G H Crosfield (hoe. sec.); and that the General Committee consist of the above, together with Mrs D Faircloneh, Mrs B Fsirclough, Mrs Frost, Mr C Molars, Mr F W Monks J.P., Mr W P Rylands, J.P., the Rev Canon Wills, M.A.' the Rev Father Wilson.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. F. SHOE, in allying a vote of thanks to the speakers, said those who prsted of pence and naked us to believe we could reduce our armaments and discon- tinue the efforts which had made us grent,were speak- ing the worst of folly and leading us, if we followed their advice, to untold disaster.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. S. TOWNROE, in se moding, said that Mr. G. H. Mair, whose absence they deeply regretted, was a Liberal jcurnalist who held the view that so long as nations were arming relentlessly and steadily, it was necessary for peace-loving sad industrial communities like eau to arm in salt-defence. They all wished to see a reduction of the en3rmous expenditure on arma- ments, an] 53010 of them looked forw.rd to a time when the lion would lie down with the lamb, but the time hal not yet come for the British IL n to lie down like I lamb. (Applause). COUNCILLOR IL ROBERTS AS A " PROTECTIONim.</p>
        <p>H,,uncillor H. Runtrf'd proposed a file of thanks 0 Lord Derby for presiding. He said he load not felt quite no looely that night as he sometimes did when he was on a National Service platform. He had been delighted to hear another Radical speech on that ques- tion. (Applause). "Although I am no Tariff Reformer," continued Councillor Roberts," on this very important question I am a full-blooded protectionist." (Applause) There were only two questions involved in the pro- gramme of the League. The first which they would answer unanimously, was: Is it this duty of every man in the face of danger to the country to stand up and try and defend it ? (Cries of" Yes "). There was only one answer to that. What was the corollary ? If it was every man's duty to defend his country in case of need, it was also his duty to make himself efficient for that purpose. (Applause.) ihat was the most democratic and, at the same time, the roost conservative proposition that had , ever been submitted to the consideration of this great nation. He had heard it raised as an objection to the League's proposals, that they would engevelor a Jingo spirit. He did not Lel:eve it. The sante of respoosi- bility always brought with it a sobering influence. (Heir, hear.) The Jingoes were not the men who had to do the fighting. They were the men who were site at borne and woose fighting consisted in drinking whisky and water and smoking cheap cigarettes. , (Laughter.) In face of all the unrest which was taking place among Continental nationadt was time we put on our considering cops and thought the question out, lie had he.rd it said that it did toot very much matter if we were invaded, became things would go on pretty much the same as they did now. Well, he preferred a King he knew something about, and a Constituion which give him the fullest possible individual and personal liberty. No country on the face of the earth granted to its people the measure of liberty that we enjosed, and he was not prepared to pat that liberty in jeopardy. (Applause).</p>
        <p>_ _ Captain Cnoaritada. in seconding, laid stress on the contention that the establishment of a real citizen army would free the Navy tram the necessity of guard- . , tog our shores and enable it to protect our food supply and commerce. Then, in the event of hostilities break lug out, food ancrraw material would still come stead -ly , into this country, and we would not experience the real horrors of war. (Applause.) The vote was hearttly paused. , Lord DERBY in responding, raid National Service would bring all classes, sects and politicians together. and they would get to understand each other better. A good example of ttat was afforded on the platform that night. " Here am I " .aid his Lordship, 'A poor lamb between two Radical lions. (Laughter.) they have not devoured we. and I have made no attack on them. (Laughter. Oa the contrary, I think we may understand each other better, and it is became we have one common object to fight for. " (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>TIIE TEST PIECE.</head>
        <p>TIIE TEST PIECE.</p>
        <p>The patience of the audience in waiting for the test piece was amply rewarded. The band. nods, Mr. Smith's conductorship, gave a truly magnificent render- ing of one of the moat difficult compositions ever chosen for a contest—Nleyerbeer's " Les Hugenots "—every section of the instrumentalists acquitting themselves brillisntly. The solos were a tract to listen to. At the close the band received a remarkable °vatic n, the audience cheering them for several minutes. TIIE OVERFLOW MEETING.</p>
        <p>An overo or meeting, at which the champion bend played, was held in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street. The Rev. W. Bracecatop presided and addresses were delivered by Admiral Fitzgerald, Major Strad:on, Mr. Pointz and Ur. Shea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WALKED HOME.WITELA BROKEN NECK.</head>
        <p>WALKED HOME.WITELA BROKEN NECK.</p>
        <p>! Curious evidence wu given at an inquest at Wool- wich on William John Brooks Tomlinson, seventy.one, !master-mariner, of Charlottaystreet. Plummeted, It was stated that on Saturday afternoon he fell down some stairs at the house of a 'rural lie was able to walk home, but on Sunday morning he complained of diffi- culty of breathing, and dikd before the arrival of a doctor. Toe doctor said that the ruanhi neck bad been broken. The bone which WWI fractured en, OA One whieh is broken when a roan is Unwed, sod death was usually instantaneour. Trinlinson's death sus ilee to suffocation, c.aued by a braken neck. A verdict of " Accidental death " was returned.</p>
      </div>
