      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conductcd by UNCLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conductcd by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>HOW THE BABY IS WASHED IN AFRICA.</p>
        <p>This is how a Missionary, who is a friend of mine, describes the operation of washing the baby in Africa. The first morning I spent among the natives I heard a baby wailing as though his heart would break. I went to see what could to the matter with him, and found his mother washing him in front of her house. And do you think she had a nice little bath- tub and scented soap and warm water? 0, no! She was holding the little baby up on I his little feet, and was pouring cold water over him by handfuls. The poor baby was screaming at the top of his lungs and fight- against the cold water as hard as he could; but the mother paid no attention to that, and went on with the washing. Did she have nice, warm flannel cloths to dry him with, and others with which to dry him? No,but when the washing was over, she lifted the baby up and with her mouth blew vigorously into his eyes and ears to drive out the water, and that was all the drying he got. Then she proceeded to dress him. The dress con, sisted of a string of beads around his waist, one around his neck, and one around each of his wrists and ankles. The air and the sun did the rest of the drying." THIS WEEK'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Mrs. New-wife: "Your eggs are all very small to-day Mr. Jones. "</p>
        <p>~ Mr. Jones : " Yes'm, they are, but I'm sure I don't know the reason."</p>
        <p>Mrs. New-wife: "0, I expect you took them out of the nests too soon."—" The Sketch."</p>
        <p>A PUZZLE AND A POEM. WHO IS LIME WILLIE WINKIE ? Wee Willie Winkie rims through the town, Upstairs and downstairs in his nightgown; Tapping at the window, clying at the lock, "Are the lveans i's their beds? for it's n iw 10 o'clock.. Hey, Willie Winkie ! Are you coming, then ? The cat is singing sofily to the sleeping hen ; The do, is lying on the lbor and does not even peep, But here's • wakeful lnd,lie that will nQt 1311 asleep.</p>
        <p>Anything but eke?, you r:irte !--stAring like be</p>
        <p>moon, Rattling in an iron jug with an iron ap-on I Humbling, tumbling all about, crowing like a cock, Shouting like I doa't know what—waking sleeping folk. Hey, Willie Winkle, cau't you keep him still ? Wriggling off a body's knee like a very eel ; Pulling at the cat's ear as she gently hums— Hey, Willie Winkle !—See, there he (urnes !</p>
      </div>
