      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, OCTOBER 21, 1911</p>
        <p>If it be tr e that Mr, THE " 8.M.G." BALFOUR does not read MOVEMENT, the newspapers he must just now be escaping a great deal of vexation of spirit; for it is inconceivable that any of those who are popularly regarded as his followers could say to his face what they are saying to the public and to each other. There is Mr. LEO MAXSE, for instance. This gentleman is the editor of the " National R?view," and is entitled to be regarded as a leader and inspirer of Toryism of the more furious and inconsiderate sort. He is the sort of politician beside whom Lord HALSBURY seems to be an embodiment of sweet reasonableness. He is, too, an influen- tial person, if he is not quite so influential as he thinks. Well, Mr. LEO MAXSE has raised aloft a banner with the strange device " 8.M.G." He shall himself explain what it means. In an interview with a newspaper correspondent Mr. MAXSE said: "You want to fasten Mr. BALFOUR on our backs. But you won't succeed. You may take it from me that he will have to go. . . . We have got all the best elements of the Unionist Party on our side, and we know that we can make no progress while it is led by its present Leader." After this it seems almost super- fluous to explain that " 8.M.G." means "BALFOUR Must Go." The trouble is not a new one. Few political leaders, at any time in our history have exercised so absolute a power over their followers as did Mr. BALFOUR whilst he still remained at the head of a Government. He was more like an Eastern Grand Vizier than an English Prime Minister. He got rid of Lord BALFOUR OF BURLEIGH, Mr. RITCHIE, and Lord GEORGE HAMILTON almost as rapidly as if he had given an order for bowstringing them; but that is a long whil ago. There have been three General Elections since then, and they have all been lost by Mr. BALFOUR'S party, as the active spirits of the " 8.M.G." movement do not fail to remind him. There have been sporadic revolts against the old leader- ship at the rate of at least one a year. To name only the more important of these, there were the Confederates, whose policy was to rush Mr. BALFOUR along from behind; then more recently, there were the Die-Hards, whose policy was to act contrary to his advice; and now there are the " whose policy is to fling him out of the leader- ship for good and all. It is possible, of course, that if he does not read the news- papers Mr. BALFOUR may not know of all this. Or he may feel towards the " movement as the American trapper did towards one of the earliest of the very small -revolvers. "Stranger," he said," if you were -to shoot at me with that thing, and I heard of it, rd kick you, I would." But to the mere outsider the position seems unfortunate and humiliating. Hitherto the Conservative Leader has been able to recapture his pcsition, temporarily, by means of a speech, as Mr. Punch suggests, but his real safety lies in the unsuitability of his competitors. Nobody would upset Mr. BALFOUR to put Mr. " BOADICEA HUNT in his place, as the Member from Shropshire 4eemed himself to suggest. Mr. LEO MAXSE is not yet in Parliament. Lord MILNER was once a rather popular nomination as next in the running for the Conservative Leadership; but he has not held his ground, his Parlia- mentary talents being far less conspicuous than they were believed to be when less was known about them. Earl CURZON might make a fairly able,and even adventurous, Leader if he were the sort of man with whom other men could serve comfortably. But, as things are, Mr. BALFOUR is like the famous horse Eclipse, with the rest nowhere. And so we suspect that the " 8.M.G." movement will come to the same inconsequential issue as its predecessors. It is not' for us, of course, to suggest to the Conservative Party the means by which it would be likely to get itself back Into office ; but, if we had any right to speak on the subject, we should say that the real difficulty is not the leadership, but the lack of definite and real convictions. Why should the British electorate support a party which Is agreed upon nothing but the taxation of the necessaries of life?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Palace and Hippodrome.</head>
        <p>Palace and Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>Next week will witness the appearance of Percy Henri and Co. in a new musical phantasy entitled Concordia," which promises to be a real artistic production. The company includes an orchestra of 33 performers, who will also give a special 'turn" of their own. The programme also includes: Sisters Cora, in latest successes; the Oxford Boys, vocalists and dancers ; Phil and Nettie Peters, comedy enter- tainers Rosie Rosaire, soubrette and dancer; and 'Charles Lee, comedian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ilmoomminiffiliMii</head>
        <p>ilmoomminiffiliMii</p>
        <p>Wa r rington has this TOWN-PLANNING. week had the first of a</p>
        <p>short series of lectures by an expert in town-planning, and we are glad to think that our local authorities are more in sympathy with the movement and alive to the legal possibilities of which advantage can now be taken. The powers at their disposal in this respect have been strangely neglected, and in conspicuous instances even studiously avoided, up and down the country. As the enthusiastic secretary of the National Housing and Town Planning Council takes pains to show in his lectures, the basis of town planning is co-operation between the representatives of an enlightened municipality and the land- owners of the 'district concerned in such enterprise. As another specialist explained in our hearing not very long ago, the chief points to have borne in mind in the prepara- tion of a scheme are (1) the area to be included; (2) the roads and lines of com- munication; (3) allocation of certain areas for definite purposes; (4) the provision of open Spaces; (5) the number of houses to the acre which is the most vital point of all ; and (6) the modification of by-laws to be made in exchange for terms with the landlords. Another interesting point men- tioned by this speaker was that compensation for sinking holes on prospective coalfields would be ruled out under a town-planning scheme. He added that he had no faith in Acts of Parliament or regulations. What mattered was the spirit behind them. He believed that the spirit of the Local Govern- ment Board was right. We commend this to our readers. The subject is one of considerable fascination, and we trust that all good citizens will attend the lectures which Mr. Aldridge is giving in the Parr Hall, and judge of the matter accordingly.</p>
        <p>In another column we BOROUGH AUDITORS' print a detailed state- REVELATIONS. ment from the elective auditors of the borough Messrs. T. Pemberton and B. Silcock, which shows at a glance the financial position of the municipality and its various undertak- ings, and also draws attention to the large expenditure on deputations and other extraneous matters. Some of our city fathers have dealt generously with themselves in the matter of trips to various parts of the country on public service at the public cost. For instance, two aldermen and one official incurred an expense of £2B 10s. on a deputa- tion to Brighton for the Health Department. No doubt they found the stay at the South Coast watering place very enjoyable. Another trip to Glasgow on behalf of the Electricity Department cost £29 10s. 6d. for the same number of deputationists; while deputation expenses, and the expenses of the witnesses on the Corporation Bill, totted up together to the nice sum of £567. These are items which the eagle eye of an auditor has often failed to detect amid the mass of municipal accounts, but which the public likes to be informed upon. The auditors also very properly point out that on a capital charge of £2,514, the Gymnasium only shows an annual revenue of £227, including £l6O received from the Education Department. Unfortunately we are not told what the working expenses are. Then there is a word of caution about expenditure in the Water Department, where only £1,934 profit was made, with a capital account reaching the huge total of £374,403. The days of lelective auditors in Warrington are nearly at an end, and the thanks of the public are, therefore, all the more the due of the two gentlemen who have so conscientiously and painstakingly reported on things as they find them in the municipal year ending March 31st last.</p>
        <p>The ravages of tuber- THE WHITE culosie can be prevented PLAGUE, and the fell disease, at</p>
        <p>. - a not very distant date, exterminated—that is if all will co-operate. This is the faith firmly held by those best able to judge. But before this hope and faith can be realised much will have to be done to enlist the co-operation of all. We are glad to see that the Warrington Guild of Help has secured such an able exponent of this faith in Professor Delepine, who is to lecture in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening next. Those who are willing to help in this great fight—and it ought to enlist the active support of everyone—should attend the lecture. They can rely on hearing the subject treated in an authoritative, suggestive, and practical manner.</p>
        <p>The Tory Party wants WHY NOT the passing of the AT ONCE? National Insurance Bill</p>
        <p>postponed. Why? Because as Mr. Lloyd- George has pointed out, in every con- stituency there would be the tangible evidence of the Government's beneficent legislation at the next General Election if the Insurance Bill is carried. There would be the men who had come back cured from the sanatorium; homes that had been saved from ruin by the illness of the wage-earner. The black tide of suffering and ciestitution is to be allowed to run on for years if the Opposition have their way, because the Opposition would lose a Party advantage if it was arrested.</p>
        <p>The real reason for the TARIFF REFORM peculiar attitude of the BLOCKS THE WAY. Tory Party to the Insurance Bill is dis- closed in an article by Mr. F. E. Smith in the "Oxford and Cambridge Review." Mr. F. E. Smith tells his Party that they must look to the revival of their popularity in social reform. But the Tory Pary can hope for nothing in this direction if great measures of social reform like the Old Age Pensions Act and the Insurance Bill are carried through on a Free Trade basis. So, while one section of the Tory Party is opposed to all social reform out of sheer dislike for any- thing which infringes on vested interests, the Tariff Reform stalwarts fought against Old Age Pensions and the Budget, and would like to kill the National Insurance scheme, because they -want to present to the British working man the cruel alternative, " You must give up all hope of social reform, or accept a tax on your bread-and-butter."</p>
        <p>A TAME SEAL A seal in Loch Long has became so tame as to feed from the hand. It made its appearance at the loch, daily moving in and out among the plensure-boats, and showing little signs of fear. lAtterly, adds the" Field,' it has become an mach attached to one of the host- hirers as to answer his call and come to take Ash from his Land.</p>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday,</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>It is interesting to note that the Rev. F. W. Macdonald, who is to speak at the Bible Centenary celebrations next Thurs- day, is an uncle of the famous writer Rudyard Kipling. •</p>
        <p>The final meeting of the Town Council prior to the Municipal Elections, will be held on Tuesday. Among other business the Mayor is to be thanked for his good services during the past year.</p>
        <p>Councillor LashfOrd has been asked by the Sanitary Works Committee to withdraw his remarks made at the last Couucil meeting I with reference to a tender for bags, but the I Councillor has not replied. It a suitable candidate can be found for St. John's Ward on November Ist the offending Councillor may get the " sack " ! . • _</p>
        <p>The long drought has practically dried up the reservoir at Appleton, and yesterday the officials of the Water Department took the opportunity of drawing off the remaining water in order to clean the reservoir. This is the first time it has been emptied since its construction in 1846. Mr. Knight, of the Angling Association, was in attendance to see that the fish were safely transferred to the Bridgewater Canal. ...</p>
        <p>Alderman Pierpoint-will, we are glad to note, move a resolution in support of the Daylight Saving Bill at the Town Council meeting on Tuesday. The measure is one which, in our opinion, has a huge preponder- ance of argument and physical, moral, and financial welfare in its fagour. The diffi- culties are largely imaginary, and as the question can not, in any sense, be considered political we hope the Council will give the motion their unanimous support. _</p>
        <p>Groans from the -mueic-loving multitude! The Parr Hall Committee has once again considered the question of providing a new organ, and again decided that the proposal cannot be entertained. It seems to us that the only remedy is for some daring and enthusiastic member of the Musical Society to drop a good-sized bomb into the wheezy recesses of the instrument on the sth of I November. Something will then be done to the organ—and to the bomb thrower too, if he is caught and his good intentions wilfully misunderstood. * * *</p>
        <p>Lord Derby, our leading Lancashire Territorial magnate, made an interesting first appearance on a Warrington platform on Wednesday, when he presided over the N.S.L. meeting in the Parr Hall. Everyone who heard his speech must have been impressed by its moderation and fairness of tone. Lord Derby paid a notable tribute to Lord Haldane, of whose Territorial scheme he is an ardent admirer and supporter, and gave our War Minister credit for having placed the Army organization in an infinitely superior position than it has ever occupied before.</p>
        <p>We are glad to learn that a Preparatory Tutorial Class for workpeople is to be held during the present winter session at the Technical Institute. The subject to be studied is" Industrial History," which can- not but be of special interest to workpeople. The tutor provided for the course by those responsible for the movement is Mr. O. P. Eckhard, 8.A., of the Manchester University. The class is to meet on Tuesday evenings at 7.30 o'clock, commencing on Tuesday next. Those who are not already acquainted with the proposal and who are interested in the subject are urged to obtain particulars from the Secretary of the class (Mr. A. C. }links, 14, Arthur-street), the Librarian (Mr. D. Ridyard, 29, Barnard street) or the Director of Education.</p>
        <p>• • It seems an open question whether the musical or the martial features of the N.S.L. meeting at the Parr Hall were responsible for the presence of the majority of the crowd of 2,000 people and the necessity for an over- flow meeting. We are inclined to think that the" over-flow 'S rather gives the Compul- sory Service enthusiasts away. There has never before been any fear that all the National Service Leaguers of Warrington could not be got in one building much less than the Parr Hall,and we can only conclude, however unwillingly, that Lord Derby and the British Band ch,ampions were the chief "draw." Still, figures can prove anything. We notice that reports in the daily papers state solemnly that a National Service League meeting at Warrington attracted an audience of 2,000 people. It is to be hoped that there will be no delirium of joy at head- quarters in London. Even the band will hardly play compulsory training through the House of Commons!</p>
        <p>* * * It is pleasing to note the satisfactory character of the work which is being done at the Evening Schools in the town as evi- denced by the examination results. The Rev. Canon Stevenson voiced the satisfaction felt by the Education Committee at their meeting on Mondv and their appreciation of the efforts of the staffs of these schools which, coupled with the diligence of the students, have made such capital results possible. We understand that a further success by a stu- dent of the Technical Institute has since been</p>
        <p>obtained, the------------------------------------- Tu;i ' notified that they have awarded a King's prize to the student, Charles Mitchell Price, for his success in the Board's examination in Stage 11. of practical mathematics. The King's prizes for Art have on several occasions been gained by pupils of our School Of Art, but this is the first of such prizes for science to be won by a student of the Technical Institute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>MR. ROBERT PIERPOINT. The Freedom of the Borough.</head>
        <p>MR. ROBERT PIERPOINT. The Freedom of the Borough.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee, which consists of the whole of the members of the Town Council.= Monday, it was moved by the Mayor, seconded by Mr. Alderman Bolton, and unanimously resolved.</p>
        <p>1. That the Honorary Freedom of the Borough be conferred upon Robert Pierpoint, Eng , J.P., for his eminent services rendered to the Borough. 2. That a copy of the resolution confarring the Freedom be suitably engrossed on vellum and pre- sented in a casket to Mr. Pierp int. The ceremony f conferring the Freedom trill take place at a specially-convened meetina of the Couacil. • EMPIRE SKATING HALL.</p>
        <p>The attendance at this fine skating rink this week has fully justified our prediction that its opening would be hailed with joy by hundreds of people. There is nothing more pleasurable than whirling round this spacious, artistic and brilliantly lighted rink. The music provided by the efficient orchestra adds greatly to one's enjwment. There were a large number of skaters betides spectators at the confetti carnival held on Thursday evening, and many exciting momenta were experienced in trying to dodge the ammunition kindly provided by the management. Next week the rink is to be open on Monday. Thursday and Saturday. On theevening of Thursday a grand juvenile fancy dress carnival is sure to be a popular event, especially in view of the fact that valuable prizes are to be competed for.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Elsa Ellen Terry's Visit. _ _- As already announced, Mime Ellen Terry will give a Shakespearean recital at the Parr Hall on Thursday, November Oth under the auspices of the Warrington Philomathic Society. ?ler subject will be " Some of the heroines of Shakespeare's plays," with illustrative acting. Mr. James Ball will preside. The plan of reserved seats will be opened at Mr. R. U. Pearson's. Bridge-street on Saturday, Oct .ber 28th at 10 a m. -</p>
        <p>Manchester Poultry and Pigeon Association. The 13th annual show of poultry, pigeons and appliances, is to be held in*Belle Vim Guardens on Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday next. Racing Homers are provided with a class limited to birds who have won a Ist, 2nd, or 3rd prize in any recognized club race. 64 entries hare been received n this class and it should prove of great interest to he kcal fancier.</p>
        <p>" OSRAM '' AND " TANTALI'M " Lemee.—Agent. 3.8 GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge et., Werriegton.—Advt. Warrington Caledonian Association.—Whist Drive.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a very successful and enjoy- able whist drive was held by the Caledonian Assoc's- ' Son at Cairo-street rooms. The prizes winners were : —Ladies, 1, Miss Barlow ; 7, Mies Davie.; consolation prize, Mrs. Lackerbie ; gentlemen, 1, Mr J Buchanan; 2, Mr. Stirling ; consolation pr:ze, Mr. Meikle.</p>
        <p>Musical Competitions at Leigh. The seccnd annual uru-ic 11 festival in aid of the Welsh Presbytwian Chapel was held in the le.igh Co-operative Hall on Saturday afternoon knd evening. There was a crowded attendance at each session. The local succmses were : -Pianoforte solo (13 and under) : Ernest Hannam, Warrington. Challenge solo from "Judas Maccabreus " . Miss Mice Wilding, Warrington. FIRE GRATES. If you want a new grate our large and varied stook will interest y00.—.1. B. GARLAND, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Personal.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. E. i',pann, honorary secretary of the War- rington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade, has been offered and has accepted the position of instructor of the road-carriage building class at the School uf Technology, Manchester. Mr. Spann is a graduate member of the Carriage Builders' Institute, and holds the City and Guilds honours silver medal for carriage building. The Ambulance Brigade have decided to 'nett oa Wednesday and Friday night, alternate weeks, in order to allow Mr. Spann to attend on Friday. and so remain honorary secretory, a post be has energetically filled for several yews. Important Property Sale. On Wednesday last at the Blue Bell lon, Warring- ton, Mr. Frank Hickman conducted a sale by auction cif valuable freehold property at Warrington. There was a good attendance and 'the bidding was brisk, but the prices did not reach the reserves. The following lots were however sold by private treats : Lot I, property in Wildeispool-road, to Mr. Leslie for k 550. Lot 2, property in Chester-road, to Mr. Leslie. Lot 7, property in Xnutsford-road, to Mr. W. Turner. Lot 5, property in Orford. The solicit .1. was Mr. F. It. Roberts. It is to be regretted that the Corporation did not attend to make an offer for the pr perty at Wilderspoo'-road. The corner has for Km e time been regarded as one of the most dangerous spots in the town for traffic. VACUUM CLEANERS free the home from dust. Price 2to 7 guineas. 'lre one on hire.—J. 8. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge at, Warrington.—Advt. Miss Gornall's Matinee Concert.</p>
        <p>The plan nid be open on Mond.ty, at Ur. Pearson's, Bridge-street, fur Miss Gorcall's Matinee Concert, to beheld on November oth, in the Co-operative Hall. This is her second venture of the kind and we have every reason to believe it will bean great a success as the first. A Steinway named gran I pianoforte will be used. Tea will be provided during the interval, frea of change for all those in the reserved seats. The following artistes will take part :—Mies Hilda De Angelis, soprano; Mi‘s Hilda Cram-James, rontralx, ; Miss Arnethe Leadbetter, violionist ; Miss Marjorie Wigley, pianist ; accompanists, Mn. Llewellyn-Davies (of Wiexham), Miss Mat y Gornall,</p>
        <p>arneßy, Rodgers celebrated knives and forks,&amp;3. —Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge at., Warrington —Advt. • .. _ . . _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>PleC Death of Ministers Mother.</head>
        <p>PleC Death of Ministers Mother.</p>
        <p>iftwieh sympathy will be felt with the Rev. W. I Cooper, pastor of Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church, in the death of his other, which occurred in a tragic manner on Tuesday evening. The deco reed lady attended the Town Planning lecture in the Parr Hall, and while in the area she felt unwell er she pro- ceeded to leave the building. However, whilst in the vestibule sho bamome so ill that it was deemed neces- sary by her daughter-in-law and Councillor Archer, to call the Mayor (C,uncillor Dr. Joseph), who can presiding at the meeting. The Mayor immediately left the chair, did all he could for lire. Cooper, but in a few moments elle passed away. The deceased lady, who had been unwell for several months, was on a visit to her son. She is to be interred to-day (Saturday) at Muker. Sualedale.</p>
        <p>Warrlngion Philemathic Soclet!. The first ordinary meeting of this society f r the present session was held at the Academy, Bridge Foot, on Wednesday evzning, and a good attendaste of members and friends was recorded. After the formal elez,tion of seven new members, the President, M. Jamei Ball, delivered an interesting and provoca- tive address on Ceo•ge Bernard Shaw—the man and his meaning." The lecturer gave a reinm.; of Shaw's life and general characteristics, laying stress on his wit, mere, and roarching criticisms of many features of m-,dern life. His work and method as a dramatic and musical critic and as a playwright and so!ialist were also tevievved. notably his unusually full, frank, and explanatory prefaces. As a philosopher, Shaw believes that the evolutionary process has been retarded by nutis slavish adherence to convention cad tradition, and pleads for a freer individual life tending to the development of a higher type of man, which he calls, for lack of a better term, superman. The ensuing discussion was continued by Messrs. Elliott, A Hodgkinson, W B Forshaw, J Walsh. I E and A Corney and the Vice-president, Mr E GM:it-gess. ANTFIRACITE STOVES, Etiomonical, clean, cheerful Price from 97/6.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge at. Warringtoo.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Church Officer's Death. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Monday of the late Mr. Jcseph B. Ilamer, of Cartwright-street, sho for many years had been an active worker in connection with St. Baroabas' Church. Ths deceasel, who was forty years of age, had rendered faithful service as a member of the choir and when failing health caused him to resign his member- ship of that body he still continued to assist the church as a sidesman He took a @rectal into-tat in the scheme for ihe provision of a new organ, which had a successful consummation last Easter. Mr. Hamer was a highly sespectel member of the Oider of Foresters, having filled cum y office in connection with Court Mercy 240, of which he was a Nat Chief Hanger. For many years he bad acted as woodward and was always willing to do Lis utmost for the advancement of the Order. Ile leaves a widow and four children. The first part of the funeral service was held in Sr. Barnabas' Church' the Vicar (Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth), who officiated, paying a high tribute to the deceased's self-sacrificing labours on behalf of the church. Amongst those present, in addition to members of the family, were representatives of the church officers and choir, Order of Foresters, Refuge Assurance Company (of which the deceased was an agent), and local assurance agents. A number of beautiful II ral tributes were placed over the grave.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>SCHOOL SWIMMING.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL SWIMMING.</p>
        <p>Challenge Shield goes to Wycliffe. The Reid Walker Challenge Shield has bad a rest deal to do with the high enthusiasm of the boys of the various elementary schools in practising the art of swimming. As is now well-known. the team represent- ing Wycliffe School were successful at the gala ',old a few weeks ago end yesterday they bad the satiate:Con of receiving the shield at a gathering in the large room. The Rev. John Yonge presided, sod Councillor Henry Roberta made the presentation of the shield, al the tame tigse handing each hoy—Worrill, Holloway, Miller and Herbert—a photograph which depicted the team in their swimming costumes and also the headmaster (Mr Alfred Corney), Mr S W Jeffrey Mr 0 Sinclair (teachers) and Prof. Barlow. Among .those present were Alderman Bennett, Mr Jobs Pendle- bury and Mr J Moore Murray (director of edu -*Lion). The scholars contributed an enjoyable musical pro- gramme, the items includint " Where the he* sucks" " The sea is England's glory," "Cherry ripe' and" mother bids me tlnd my btart."</p>
        <p>Our new serial story "Sir Gregory's Silence" commences in to-day's issue. We can assure our readers that they will find it a novel of absorbing interest</p>
        <p>A Suffrage Challenge. Sir,—l feel reluctant to enter upon further news- paper controversy with Miss Waring on the Suffrage question, but another letter from that lady which appeared in your IMMO of Saturday last necessitates my contributing • reply. Subseouent ti my promise to take part in a debate at Warrington, business arrangements intervened which made it' just possible "(these are the words in my letter to Miss Waring) that after all I might be disappointed in carrying out my promise. Immedi- ately the poesibility of my absence arose I considered it necessary to inform Miss Waring of the fact, and my willingness to find a substitute. Two months elapsed before I received a further long letter from Miss Warin7, giving suggested details as to the debate, and I was also requested to continue my correspondence with another lad; Miss Waring having relinquished the duties of Hon. 'Sec. to the Warrington Suffrage Society. Ii my next letter, I re-iterated my willingness to find a lady speaker to take my place, and sugge.ted this course because a debate had already taken place at Warrington earlier in the year between Mr Crosfield and Miss Margaret Ilobertson,and I ventured to consider it would be an interesting contrast in speaker..</p>
        <p>As a matter of fact arrangements have been made, pending a few details, to hold a debate at Warrington, and it will be seen, therefore, that there has never been fur one moment the slightest doubt that a speaker would be provided. That Miss Waring should write enquifing through your coluenne whether the local Anti-Suffragists could 6nd " anyone out of the whole of Englan I to take Mr. Beanmont's place "ii both silly and absurd, and if she had been in touch with local affairs it would have been Leund quite unnemssary to trouble you with such a communication.—Yours Ac, JOHN W. G. BEACMONT, 28, Mentor-street, Longsight, Manchester. National Service a Party Question. Mr. Crostield's Conclusions.</p>
        <p>------- - Sir,- In the speeches I made at the Debating Society, and at the meeting of the Progressive Labour League the other day, and of which you were good ' enough to give excellent reports in the" Examiner," exigencies of time prevented me attempting anything like a full explanation of the facts and arguments which influence those of us who offer our determined opposition to the proposals that are being made for compulsory military service. I will not trespass on your space at any great length to-day because I shall take another opportunity shortlysto examine the position from the Liberal point of view. I say Liberal advisedly, becanso the assumption that this question is, or can be in the future, a non-party question, is quite fallacious. Will anyone argue that while at the Trades Union Co-gress the proposal for compulsory Military Service was defeated by an overwhelming mainity,the representa- tives of Labour support it or are neutral because at the Conf,rence a mere fractioa of votes was given in favour of Compu'sory Service? Or do any of its advo- cates think they have the support of the Labour Party in the House of Commons, because a stray Socialist with extreme views here and there is with them? On the contrary it is frankly recognised that the Labour Patty as a whole is latterly opposed to conscription in any shape or form. Aad similarly, while there are some much respected Liberals and Radicals who have lent their support to the pro- posals of the National Setvice League, and it secures some Whig support in addition, that does not alter the fact that the vast majority of the Liberal and Progressivo Party regards as dangerous and re-actionaiz propossls that are calculated to develop Militarism ;n this country far bey4nd what is honestly needed for national safety. liberals take note of the fact that members of the Nati, nal Service League have avowed their desire to extend Militarism in England to a print that would be a menace to Continental powers. It is against that sort of excessive Militarism that we are on our guard. We are convinced that, both directly and indilectly,re-actionary, consequences would follow, while we should run a real risk (when anther kind of reaction set ia afterwards), of damaging our over-sea service and the magnificent personnel of oar voluntarily recruited navy. May I also add that for some time past Liberals in the House of Commons have been quite alive to the Let that a large section of the Conservative Party has just been waiting its opportunity to spring these pro- posals for Conscription on the country once they have succeeded, as they vainly hope to succeed, in saddling us with Protection. lam aware that many moderate Conservatives are prepared to adopt the ,1 proposals of the National Service League, including the eminent Lancastrian, head of a historic house of which every one in the County Palatine is proud, who 'has been paying a visit to Warrington this week, and whose speech along with that of the distingui-hed Admiral who has also paid us a visit, I look forward Ito reading with interest in to-morrow's paper. But we cannot shutour eyes to the fact that in the House of Commons support hr the National Service League is very largely drawn from the mem- bers of the less responsible group of the " Die-Hards" sad front the extreme Protectionists ; while it is not with-mt significance that the most influential leader of the Conscriptionists in either House is the mon who was responsible for the intro- ductioa of Chinese quasi-slavery in South Africa. Is it to be wondered that John Burns should speak the other day of the " Jingo Rake's Progress,"—Protec- tion, Conscription, Slavery ? In face of facts like these I submit that if ever these proposals are seriouely put before the country as a question of practical politics of the day, they will inevitably be fought cut on Party lines,—Yotire etc. A. H. CRO3VIELD. 39, Hyde Park Gate, &amp;W. Oetolrr Nth: 411,</p>
        <p>'e Igtre , to the EDITORk k_,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>PERFECTION SOAP WORKS BAND. PRESENTATION OF THE 1,000 GUINEA TROPHY.</head>
        <p>PERFECTION SOAP WORKS BAND. PRESENTATION OF THE 1,000 GUINEA TROPHY.</p>
        <p>The great victory achieved by the Perfection otp Works Band in the national contest, at the Crystal Palace received public recognition last evening when Mi. Henry Iles, President of the contest, visited Warrington to present the 1,000 guinea trophy to the champions. Interest in this unique event ran h'gh and the second ''boise "at the Palace and Hippodrome, where the presentation took place, attrActed an unusually large audience. A brilliant rendering of the test piece —3leyerheer's " Hogenots "—was given by the band, their musicianly and inspiring playing arousing enthusiasm io a high pitch. Other much-appreciated items were a trombone solo by Mr. J. E. Hacking, and a cornet duet by Messrs. Bircltali and Fairhurst.</p>
        <p>The trophy, which was much admired by the audience., is al exquisite and oni9ne piece of workmanship, of gold and silver, inlaid with stones The cup stands on a centre pedestal and the design represents various phases of music, the names of eminent mnsiciaLs of the respective periads being irnroduced.</p>
        <p>Mr. lir, in making the preseotationsaid Crosfield's band was the first moat needy to realise his original ideas when he founded the festival with the late Sir Arthur Sullivan. (Applauee'. He reflected with satisfaction on the fact that he Lad a little finger in the pie in the formation of the land's progressive I, ditty which had led up to the prevent magnificent victory. (Applause). It was proheblv the most important victory achieved by • bra-s band on account of the great results that should naturelly follow - namely,the forces of example and encouragement to other brass band organizations. He wished to express his great appreciation of the splendid work of the band and ite distinguished con- ductor. (Applause.) Ile recalled how, in their early days, with dogged determination—that great , characteristic trait of North countrymen which was never to be denied—they prepared for the race of brass hand supremacy, proceeding from step to step, ever in the right direction, until the goal wan writ. (Applause.) Many bands wanted to commence at the top, and in doing no, received such a thock is their defeat that it was hard for them to recover. The success of Crosfield's hand would be of great service to bun in the futures. lie would now be able to point out the way to those many bands which were no constantly worrying him</p>
        <p>to give them a place is the Cat andchampiosehip sections. The only way was to follow the example of Crosfit Id's. (Applause). Mr. Iles went on to (apnea admiration of the carefully thought out plan of campaign, spread over many years. which had been carried out by Mr. Mouse (prcAidett) and his able lieutenants on the committee. The directors of the firm, too, by their money, influence, sympathy and kindly practical support of the band Lad set a worthy example to the principals of greet manufacturing concerns throughout the country as to what could be done in the development of a first class musical organization on the common- sense, sound business lines which they associated with the conduct of their ordinary business. Mr. Hee next pointed out what • great thi”g it was for workingmen to be able to devote so notch tone to the divine art of Inu.ic and said the progress of brass bands must Lave an appreciable effect on the cultiva- tion of the musical inclinations of the it itish people. In conclusion, he was ccufident that that would not be the last occasioa on which he would have to present the trophy to the band In ming as he did the sp'endid way in which it was organized 2nd trained train top to bottom. (Applause). The Mayor accepted the trophy oo behalf of the band and the MayoreAs p.eaented the bronze modals to the individual performers. LEIFER FROlt MR. A. if. CROSFIELI). Mr. J. Taylor, secretary of the band, has received the following letter from Mr. A. H. Crosfield : October 19th, 1911.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Taylor, I have just heard te-day that the presentation of the Shield to our band is to be mode to-morrow evening, and I w.ite at once to tell on how extremely sorry Jam that the date clic...a for this no important and interesting occasion, is one on shich it happens to be impossible f or me to come North to Warrington. lam fast here with prep orations for an engagement entered into months ago, which I cannot now p noibly cancel It happens to be an engagement also in the musical line, as my wife and I are this week giving a concert to assist tbe magical deve!opnent of the Garden Suburb at Hampstead. Well, on the 28th lam looking forward to meeting your good self and every member of the hood, and to having an oppot tnnity then to p rrsonally express to every one my congratulations on your mognifieent victory. You all know that from the very inception of theband thgoe is not one in Warrington who bas taken a ee-por interest in its progress than I have, or. I will add, felt more pride in Its achievements. lam sure therefore you wi'l undergtand that though unluckily I am perforce absent in the tle-h. I shall certainly he wirh you to-marrow night in the spirit, and I can well imogine the warmth of the congratulationia which Mr. Halliwell, your most able conductor, and the other members of the bind will receive from our fellow townsmen of Warrington to-morrow night. I add mine to the rest, and remain always—Very faithfully yours,</p>
        <p>A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
      </div>
