      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>VEY. ARCHDEACON MADDZN.</head>
        <p>VEY. ARCHDEACON MADDZN.</p>
        <p>An important erect in the religiou history of Warrington—the Centenary of the local auxiltaty of the British and Foreign Bible S,Jcieiy—i, to be cele- brated next week. To morrow Sunday) .i:1 be observed as " Bible Sunday " at roost of the places of worship. ard the chrims of the soc:ety mll be urged upon the congregations.</p>
        <p>rOu Thurel;:e7ening next a groat 122111 rneeirg I will be held in the Parr Hall, The Pechor (Rey. Canoe Willis, sill preside, an I the -?sakers will be the Lord Bishop of Cber,ter, the Rey. F. W. 113,oioriald (an es.presideot of the Wesleyan Conference and the Ven. Archdeacon Malden. of Liverpool. A noised choir trill sing t! e " Hallelujah Chorus." An endeavour is being made to raise sewn of .£ IO) as a Centenary contribution to the funds of the Societe. The British and Foreign Bible Society lost year distribeted no fewer Man 6,n20,50 copies of the Scriptures, and has now e.iyen some portion at least of the Bible in 432 different forms of speech.</p>
        <p>The bon. secs. of the Warrington auxiliary are the Recs. E. C E. Carhton and J. Ycnge and Mr. S. Percy Bilcock. Hr. J. C. Holland is the bon. Centenary secretary.</p>
        <p>EXTENSIVE STATION THEFTS. A PORTER'S DOWNFALL. A ram which has crested considerable interest locally was heard at the Borough Court on Saturday before Mr. J. W. Hayward (presiding), Alderman Bennett, and Meters. A. J. Jolley and T. Pemberton, when Walter Eastham, a porter at Bank Quay Station was charged on remand with stealing a hind quarter of lamb, value 12s. 6d., a Japanese basket, containing a quantity of lady's apparel, value .£7 Its. 4d. and also a number of other articles value lie , all the property of the London and North-Western Railway Co. Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins prosecuted on behalf of the Railway Company. Mr. Jenkins said that this %vas a prosecution of a most serious character. During the whole of the stammer thefts bad been committed at the station whilst at intervals valuable conaigments of goods had been missed. The railway company's detectives had been put to considerable trouble to detect them. The prisoner was suspected and two of the detectives con- cealed themselves to watch him, with the result that he was obeerved to take a hind quarter of lamb and con- ceal it under his overcoat. In the case of the lady's apparel the amount had been claimed by the lady from the company. A visit was paid to the prisoner's house end there the subjects of the second and third charges were found. He asked the Bench to mark their sense of the serionsnesi of the offence. Prisoner had been an excr adiegly cute man and did not deserve the least bit of sympathy at their hands and be hoped that the magistrates would inflict such punishment as would he a warnirg to ott era not to treat their employers as this man had done. Prisoner raid he was very sorry for what he had done and he hoped the Bench would la lenient. He had a wife and five children to support and he was unable to do this with the wages he received. Chief Inspector Williamson proved a number of previous convictions against the prisoner who was sent to gaol for six months. FOOTBALL FIELD SCENE. After the business of the Court on Monday, an old man of nearly 70 years of age, named Thomas Knockton, employed by the Hippodrome Management as a sand- wicbman, applied for a free summons against a youth .for assault on the Warrington Football Club's ground on Saturday. The applicant stated that whilst walking round the field during the half-time period of the match he was struck in the eye with a clinker which was deliberately thrown at him by the youth. The ELMILOODS was granted. The sequel to the above was heard at a Juvenile Court on Thursday before Mr. A. .1. Jolley as the presiding magistrata, whoa John Bradshaw, aged 15, residing with his parents at 78, Sharp-street was an Intoned for animating Thomas Knockton. The son of the complainant stated that when he saw the stone thrown by the lad at his father, he caught hlm and gave him into the charge of a constable. He afterwards found his father suffering from a bad cut across the eye. Complainant said that he had been receiving similar treatment at the hands of lads for the last three weeks. He had previously been struck. There were three or four boys throwing on this OCC3IOOII. Defendant stated that be did not mean to strike the complainant, but was only throwing at the board.. A sister of the defendant stated that her father had promised to leek after the complainant ar d pay all the costs. Die Chairman of the Bench said that if the father MS willing to c3mpensate complainant to the amount of 103. and pay the costs they would withdraw the charge so that there would be no conviction against the boy. ANOTHER JUVENILE'S MARKET THEFT. A further case of a juvenile's theft from the market wile heard at a Juvenile Court on Tuesday, before Councillor H. Roberts and other magistrates, when Thomas Gibbins, aced 14, 2, Berry's-yard, was charged with stealing 42 rabbit skins value is. 61., from a box at the tear of a stall in the market, the property of William Chapman. Chief Inspector Williamson said that on Saturday Isst the prosecutor placed a large number of dam skins in a boast the rear of his stall about 8 p.m., but when be went to check them at a quarter to eleven many of them were missing. The matter was reported to the police. A constable on Monday ohserved the defendant with a suspicious looking parcel under his arm. He went towards the boy who dropped the parcel and ran away but the constable overtook him. Upon examination the parcel was found to contain ten rabbit skins. The home of the defendant was afterwards searched and 24 more skits were found. When charged the boy stated that he took the skins from a basket at the rear of another stall but later admitted taking them front this stall. The police asked for an adjournment in order that further enquiries could be effected con- cerning tie prisoner, likOM they suspected of being concerned in another case of a similar character. 'I he mother of the defendant stated that he was completely out of her control. The case was adjourned for a week.</p>
        <p>Borough Police Court. SA7PRDAY.-Itefore Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chair), Alderman Bennett, Messrs. A. J. Jolley and T. Pemberton. A FHA RP PENALTY FOR AN ASSAF LT.—Patrick Warren, a collier, of no settled abode, was charged with being drunk and disorderly and with assaulting Thorns. Bradshaw. P.C. Tolley said that at 8.5 the previous evening whilst on duty in Lyme-street he raw the prisoner, who was drunk, deliberately kick the witness on the in-step. Thomas Bradshaw said that he did not know the prisoner. His foot was very painful and much swollen that morning. Prisoner was fined 20s. and costs with the alternative of one month's hard labour. MONEY THEFTS FROM CHILDREN—James Jenke, 58. Priestley-street a shoemaker, and Thomas Longhorn, of no settled abode, a clerk, was charged with stealing ss. from a boy named Albert Gleave in Bylands-street, the Property of William Glasse. Chief Inspector Williamson said that during the past five or sic weeks reports had been received by the police of thefts of money from children who had been sent upon errands by their parents. The police had been put to consider- able trouble. Detective Holland from enquiries ascertained that the prisoner Jenks was the offender. He arrested him and took him to the Police Station where in reply to the charge prisoner immediately replied •' If you will allow me I will makes clean brest of everything, I am sick of it." Jenks also said that Langhoro was with bins on one occasion when he stole ss. and he received a share of it. When arrested Langhorn admitted being with the prisoner on one occasion and receiving 2s. 63. as his share. The police asked that the case might stand adjourned to enable them to make further inquiries. The Bench granted an adjournment of seven days. A DEsm4DANT's BIGUEST.—The adjourned case in which Mary Ann Nfaloy, 17, Carlisle-street, summoned her husband Patrick Maley for assault was again brought before the magistrates, when the complainant asked that the ch.irge might be withdrawn. Since the adjournment her husband had given her no cause to complain. The defendant " Make her keep her tongue still, she's very ' naggy. ' " The case was withdrawn. MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the cirri r). Aldermen Tionion, Bolton, James Evans, Nlessra W Furness, W Roberts, S Wright, H Thornton, G Pilling and T Lecke?. DRUNKMCNESS.—The only business of this court Wail to bear four cases of drunkenness and disorderly behaviour. MONDAY—Before Councillors H Roberts (in the chair) C Jackson Holmes, Messrs J Rymer Young, W Richardson and T Looker. SHOCKING CASE OF NEGLECT.—Thomns Taylor, 4, Woods'-court, a labourer, was charged with neglecting his family kis manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering one injury to health. He pleaded guilty Mr A Browne who prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. said that the defendant was in receipt of reserve pay amounting to 6d a day. He was also formerly employed as a wharf casual labourer at Messrs Crosfield'a after- wards being pot on to regular work. He however, gave sip the situation, hut subsequently obtained employment at itichmonds. Whilst there, in two weeks he drew 23s and 2ls 7d rearctively, but only gave his wife 23s and So, the remainder Icing spent on borne-racing and drink. He also received £2 Os reserve pay, but only gave his wife £2. The case came under the notice of the Sooiety on November last and since that time the wife had bad to be supplied on different occasions with food. But for the help of the officers the children would undoubtedly have starved. On Saturday last the family were in such a plight that they were advised to go Into the Workhouse. One of the children had been seriously ill through want of food and was still receiving atten- Om—Prisoner had received warning after warning from the Society's WU-era—Prisoner who had nothing to say, was sent to gaol for one month. ALLEGED LOITERA SO WITH INTENT.—John O'Brien, a labourer, of no settled abode, was charged with loitering in Sankey-street with intent to commit a felony. Chief Inspector Williamson said that shortly</p>
        <p>after 1140 p.m. the previous night P.C. Edwards noticed the prisoner acting in a suspicious manner in Sankey-street. He vas walking from Dixon-street to Hold-street examining the shop doors. He afterwards went into the door, aa of a tobacconist's shop where the constable found him in a stooping position. In reply to the conatatle the prisoner said" You have been too sharp for me, I meant to have a pop at the jeweller's shop." Prisoner had not worked for ten years. He asked that the case might stand adjourned for further enquiries to be made. Prisoner who stated that he was drunk at the time and could not remember anything about it, was remanded until Saturday.</p>
        <p>Tit CIISD Y.—Before Messrs A J Jolley (in the chair), W Roberts, W T Williams, T Locker and Dr Naden. AFTER ELMER YEARS.—William Gavin, late of Barnsley, was charged with running away and leaving his wife and two children chargeable to the Union. He pleaded guilty. Mr B Shearer said the prisoner deserted his wife and children eleven years ago and they had been chargeable to the Union on and off during the whole of that time. It was impossible to state the total cost but during recent years it had amounted to no less than RIM 'fhe woman was paralysed and could net work. The police had been put to great trouble in apprehending the prisoner the warrant having been sent ell over the country, and at lent the Gusrdians had to offer a reward of £2. He was employed chiefly in rod mekrng.—Prisoner wry sentenced to three months' hard lobour.</p>
        <p>MARRIED WOMAN'S THElE.—Elizabeth Lavin, 21, Portland•street, s married woman, was charged sith stealing a pair of lady's boots value Gs. Gd., the property of Harriet Jenkins, on'the sth inst, and with stealing a sweeping brush, value 43d., from a shop, 65. Winwick- -1 road, the property of James Crank, on the Ilth inst.— Toe Chief Constable said that it was apparent that the prisoner must have gone into the shop of Mrs. Jenkins who carries on the business of a diaper, wardrobe and boot dealer, in Orford-lane, and taken the boots from the counter. She afterwards tried to dispose of them at Messrs. Jacksons. The assistant, noticing that the boots had been scrapod, became suspicious and asked the prisoner to bring a receipt. She went away and failed to return. The prisoner had been going about in slippers no that the shopkeepers could not hear her enter. She must have entered the shop of Mr. Crank in this way and taken the brush. She then went lower down the road and offered the brush for sale at a fish and chip shop, asking 20. for it, and stating that she wanted to buy a loaf. Prisoner also endeavoured to steal some meat from a butchers shop which she entered but the hook upon which the meat wAis hung dropped on the floor and attracted the attention of the butcher. She did this kind of thing to obtain money for drink.— The Bench sent prisoner to gaol for three months. FRIT/AV.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs W Furness. A J Jolley, S Wright, H Thornton, G Pilling and T Locker. . .</p>
        <p>BAD Luce.—William Holland, Norcot Brook Whitley, was summoned for ill-treating a horse by working it in an unfit state. He pleaded guilty. Inspector Dee said that at ten minutes to nine on the 7th inst. he saw the defendant's wife in Arpley-road driving a brown mare, which Was' very law attached to a greengrocer's cart. The animal appeared to be in great pain. Witness afterwards interviewed the defen- dant who replied that he knew that the enimal had swollen joints end he had tor some time been rubbing liniment into it. Defendant stated that he had bad a great of bad luck through the suiden death of a previous horse. The Chairman said the Beach were satisfied defendant had done his best for the animal and dismissed the case upon payment of costs. Wars DESERTION.—John Lester, 20, Parr-street, was summoned by his wife Hannah Lester, for desertion.— Mr. C. H. Unsworth appeared for the complainant and Mr. A. Browne defended.—Mr. Unsworth said that the defendant had been out of work through Blues% He had, however, been receiving Ins a week from the Union Tannery. About three months ag s he left home and went to Manchester. The complainant followed him there and brought him back. On the 19th inst he again left home and went to Liverpool. The wife kept rho house open for a week, but was then compelled to give up owing to arrears of rent—Defendant said that he went away because there was no food in the house, the complainant having taken it away. He wrote three times for the complainant toga and live with him, but she refusal. He came back again but found the house empty, and wen compelled to sleep out.—Mr. Browne said that the chief complaint was that the whole of the complainant's family were disappointed because the defendant recovered from his illness—The Bench granted a asparation order of 10s. a week with half a guinea towards the advoczta's foe. A CHANCE TO RlCFORM.—Catherioe Farrington, 2, Hall-street, was summoned for neglecting her family.— Mr. Brown said that the cite was adjourned to enable the defendant to reform. In the meantime, Inspector Wainwright of the N.S.P.C.C., bad visited the defendant and found a great improvement, but unfortunately on the 10th inst., she was locked up for being drunk and disorderly and lined 10s. and coats. Since that time, however, she had shown further improvement. The case was adjourned for a mouth. ENMITY BETWEEN FAMILIES.—A Cave Of a CEOs% summons for assault was heard, the parties being Annie Bate, wife of John Bate and John Bde aged nine, 7, Hawthorn-street, and Beatrice Marshall, wife of George Marshall, 84, Folly-lane the other party. Mr. Browne appeared for Mrs. Marshall and Mr. C. 11. Unsworth represented the tvt6 Bates. Mr. Browne said that the trouble arose through that little "wretch "(messing the boy Bate) pulling Mrs. Marthall's front door bell -and then running away and also repeatedly heating Mrs. Marshall's daughter. She, however, managed to ca'ch the boy and gave him a good shakicg. In con- ' sequence of this the mother of the boy came to see Mrs. Marshall end words led to Mrs. Bate seizing Mrs. Marshall by the hair and throwing her to the ground. The boy then caught hold of hire. Marshall's yard- brush and struck her on the head several times. Mr Unsworth said that there had been enmity between the , families for some time, and it was in consequence of , certain remarks made by Mrs. Marshall about Mrs. Bate who was no exasperated that she did strike Mrs. Mershall, although not before Mrs. Marshall had first struck her with the brush. The Bench bound both women over ; each to pay their own costs, while the charge against the boy WAS dismissed.</p>
        <p>Lrn the I = OURTS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Warrington County Petty Sessions</head>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions</p>
        <p>The above were held on Wednesday before Coun- cillor H. Roberta (in the chair), Messrs. C. E. Parker and J. Backbouse.</p>
        <p>... .. ................... THEFT OF POTATOES.—Joha Grounds, 204, Winwick- road, Winwick, was charged with stealing six penny- worth of potatoes the property of Herbert Challoner, a farmer, Winwick. He pleaded guilty. P.C. Woolf stationed at Wiawick said that at 9.15 a.m, on the 10th lost. he saw the defendant go into a field and (loaner. ately uproot the potatoes. Mr. T. S. Steel, instructed by the family of the defendant, said that unfortunately for some time past defendant had suffered from- delusions and evidently must have been so e uffering at the time of the theft. Defendant was 69 years of age and for some time had been retired, lie had gone through life without any previous trouble of the kind. While living at Thelwall he had been employed at Reynolds' Tannery for 22 years. On the day in question he went fora walk and seeing the gate of the field open went In and pulling the potatoes alp, put them into his pocket. The constable then appeared and he handed the potatoes to him, and gave his name and , address without the least trouble, and then went home. He (Mr. Steel) hoped Ole Bench would appreciate the fact of the defendant only being one year off the old- age pension, of which a conviction would deprive him. The Bench dismissed the case upon payment of costs. WEIGHTS AND MEASURES PROSECUTION.—John Brien Pennington-lane Burtonwood, was charged under the Weiehta and Measures Act with keeping a weighing machine which wan unjust.—Defendant pleaded guilty to a technical offence.—Petar Kaye, Weights and Measures Inspector for the Widnes division, spoke to visiting the defendant's house on the 12th anst and finding the machine give an unjust weight to the amount of 20n5. 14joirs.—Mr. Steel slid the defendant took over the farm from his father two years ago sons to grow sufficient food for his horses and family. Defendant was engaged as a colliery contractor, but never used this machine for trade purposes, but only as a check no that when he sent his oats to be ground he could be sure that he got the right quantity heck. Defendant would give an undertaking to destroy the machine and not have it about the premises—The case was dismissed on payment of costs, while two cases of deficiency in weights were withdrawn. ALLEGED LEAD THEFF.--30Serb Naylor, a labourer, ' of Earlestown, was charged with stealing 45Ibs of lead value £1 2s. 64., the property of some person unknown.—Thomas Chadwick, hawker, 36, Turner- street, Warrington, said at 1.50 p.m. on the 17th inst. the prisoner came into the Coffee Tavern,Orford-street, Earlestown, and asked him if he would buy some metal which was at Newton. Prisoner got on the back of witness's cart and they went to Newton where the prisoner produced the metal from underneath a hedge. There were three pieces and the prisoner asked 75. 6d. as the price for them. Witness asked the prisoner where he had obtained the metal from, but be did not reply.—lnformation was given to the police and prisoner was /merited but said that another man had given him the metal to sell.—The police asked for a remand for further enquiries to be made.—Prisoner was remanded to Newton Sessions to be held on the 26th inst. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Examiner Sa</head>
        <p>The Examiner Sa</p>
        <p>BABY SHOW.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING SCENES AT THE</p>
        <p>PARR HALL</p>
        <p>A chorus of infant wailing greeted the vis'tor to the Parr Hail on Thursday afternoon. Warrington's second annual baby show was in progress, and many of the precious exhibits lifted up their voices and wept to find themselves on public view ia unaccustomed surroundings. Even the Mayor, expeiienced as he is in handling ecimewhat unruly assemblies, had a Idifficulty in gaining a hearing, so lusty were the cries of tiny intorrupt.ra. Mothers and babies filled the body of the ball, and the marked improvement of the arrangements as compared with last year was a subject of general comment. The trans- ference of the show from the Gymnasium to the much more spacious Parr HO had made an immense difference. Last year the entries reached suA unexpectedly large numbers in the course of a few days that the Gymnasium proved inadequate to accommodate satisfactorily all who came and the defective ventilation added to the discomfort. it the Parr Hall, however, everyone was assured of ample air space and the convenience of the mothers was studied in every direction. Within a shot., time of their arrival the competitors had been placed in their respective tections, according to the ages of the ibies, and everything was in readiness for the judging to begin. A large number of local medical practi- tioners responded to the request to act as judges in the twelve classes, and the walk was therefore accomplished wi.h much greater facility than last year, when, with nearly 100 more entries, the whle of the jailging devolved upon the Mayor and the 11,dical Officer of Health. In the opinion of the Mayor, the baby show has come to stay and so great are the possibilities of its</p>
        <p>.. ... 4100 f 41IN _ 1 0, - ...1: ~41,1414.......„ 1 , •• . A • I , • t 1 - t 10 ill ', •40' -.11,'",* • . . . . 1.. VIP •.:,. ..., . , ..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Photo bone.</head>
        <p>Photo bone.</p>
        <p>Top flow: Mrs. Wild, Mr. W. T. Flood, Dr. Hutt, Dr. J. Coote Hibbert. Bottom Row: Miss M. Broadbent, the Mayor and Mayoress, Councillor Wilson.</p>
        <p>influence in the reduction of infant mortality, that every encouragement is forthcoming from the Health Committee who not only bear a proportion of the cost, bat place the time of their staff at the disposal of the promoters. To Councillnr Wilson must be given the credit for initiating the show, and he has received invaluable help from all connected with the health department, !viably Dr. J. Conte Hibbert (medical officer of health), Dr. Hutt (assistant medical officer), Mr. W. T. Flood. (chief inspector), and the lady inspectors —Misses Poke., Brown, and Hughes. Any baby up to the age of I 3 months, born in the borough , of Warrington, was eligible and prizes were offered for the he althiest, best developed and most carefully tended infant in each class. The entries numbered 243, as against 331 last year. The arrangements for the show had, however, been some- what huriied and this made it more difficult to enlist the same widespread interest in the project as was manifested twelve months ag3. Al the babies had been weighed ia advance at the Health Office. A wise discretion had been ezercis d in the choice of prizes which took the form of useful household g oda. The parents of the two " champion " babies orre presented with handsome clocks, as special awards A large number of the general public viewed the show from the galleries.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph), who was are=- ponied by the Mayoress, opened the show in a brief speech. The baby show, he said, was an institution which had come to stay, and the credit for its i naugura- ation was due to Councillor Wilson (Applause.) From one point of view every mother ought to have a prize, but looking at the matter in another light no mother should need one, because it was the duty of all mothers to do the best they could for their children. He looked upon the show not merely as an occasion for the presentation of prizes but rather as a marshalling of forces after the yearly battle against disease and death amongst the infants. After all, the great thing they had to fight against was ignorance and nowadays nobody should be ignorant. He believed every mother had a pride in her babies, but she was not always capable of kn, wing the best way of treating them. There was, however, little excuse for ignorance now, because so many efforts uere made to</p>
        <p>Boys between nine and twelve months : 1, Fred Potter, 72, Scott-street; 2, William Hongh,ll, Dakin's- yard ; 3, Stanley Stover, 9 Block, Orford Barracks ; he, Henry Elmdm n 69, Charley-street ; Enoch Ravenscroft, 20, Mason-street ; Victor Russell, 46, Marsh-street. rl .1 between nine and twelve maiths : 1. Beatrice G lbert, 77, Grafton-street ; 2, Lily Hewitt, 11, Dickenson-street; 3, May O'Brien, 12, Catherine- Street ; h.e., ithrgaret Curtis, 11, Owen-street; Eileen Lund, 22, Willis-street ; May Jolley, 72, Ellesme-e-street. _</p>
        <p>Bye between twelve end fifteen months: 1, Ronald Roberti, 133, Ellesmere-street ; 2, Morris Owen, 1, Ford-street ; 3, Rowland Kinsey, 27, Norah- street ; h c. Willian Drinkwater, 17, Florence-street ; Ernest Milnes, 4, Clifton-street..</p>
        <p>Girls between twelve and fifteen months: 1, Elsie Lee, 47, Oxford-street; 2 Amy Cotton, 40, Amelia-street ; 3, 31 irgaret Titcht:rd, 39, Battersby- lane ; h c., Barbarba Burke, 67, Padgate-lane ; Clara Bibby, 12, Canterbury-street. Prema.urely Born Infants : I. Thomas Williams, 7, Elaine-street, and Florence Wilkinson, 38, Sharp street; 2, Isabel Drummond, Belmont, Latchford; 3- nettles, 4, Ford-street. Twins 1, Lucy and Wilfred Bill, 69, Kimberley- btreet ; 2, Amy and Annie Spier. 22, Dudley-street; 3, Hilda and Eva Dalton, 37, 01111 am-street. A vote of thanks was passed to the judges, on the proposition of the MATOR seconded by Councillor Br ti: 4. N.</p>
        <p>The Mayor was thanked for his interest in the show, on the motion of Alderman JAMES EVANS (chairman of the Health Committee), seconded by Alderman SMETIMRST. . . . .</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, in responding, said their thanks were mainly due to Councillor Wilson, who had inaugurated the show, and bad worked night and day to promote its success.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT seconded the vote of thanks to Councillor Wilma which was heartily accorded. During the afternoon the mothers were provided with tea, which was served under the supervision of a large number of lady helpers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>ROYAL NATIONAL LIFEBOAT INSTITUTION.</head>
        <p>ROYAL NATIONAL LIFEBOAT INSTITUTION.</p>
        <p>Formation of Local Branch.</p>
        <p>The Committee of Management of the Royal National Lifeboat Institution held a meeting at the Town Hall on Thursday afternoon for the purpose of forming a local branch and a Ladies' Auxiliary. The Mayor (Councillor Dr. Jeseph) occupied the chair and was supported by Messrs George F. Shee," M.A. (secretary of the Institution) and Edgar H. Johnson (district organizing secretary). There was a fair attendance. Letters of apology for non-attendance were read from Captain Croseeld and Mrs. Carleton. The CHAIRMAN said that he could not see why the Institution, which was a most praiseworthy undertak- ing, should not be a Government question. It was a national question and therefore ought not to be left to private interest. Mr. SHER said they were met to discuss the question of the saving of life from shipwreck on the storm- beaten coasts of theßritish Isles. This great question affected no more than any other counury in the world, as having the greatest shipping industry of the world. If people only knew what the lifeboatmen suffeiel in order to serve and help humanity he was sure that the Institution would receive a much more generous response than wali at present experienced. Their whole lifeboat system was a model for the whole of the civilized world and he appealed to them to put forth all their efforts to make the service the most efficient and perfect it would be possible to find. When men were asked to go out voluntarily to rescue their fellow creatures from s watery grave it was only to be expected that they should be provided with the best possible material but these conditions required a very large expenditure. They ought to take into account the fact tint most of the lives saved were those of their own ootintrymen although the noble life- brat men were prepared to risk their lives to try to save others—no matter of what colour or nationality. During the year,' Ib2l to 1910 the total number of lives saved by the Institution's men was 49,394,an average of about 600 or 700. year. Since the Institutions forma- tion 60,000 lives had been saved,equal to the population of a county borough. He appealed to the public to help what was one of the most heroic, uncontroversial and noble causes that could appeal to the members of the British race, as 90 per cent of those lives saved were their own kinsmen. (Applause.) The MAYOR then proposed the following resolution : —" That this meeting sympathising with the national and humane work of the Royal National Lifeboat Institution, resolves to form a Warrington branch on its behalf." This was szconded by Canon Worts and carried unanimously. The following ladies and gentlemen were appointed as the committee with power to add to their number, on the proposition of Miss BROADBENT, seconded by Captain WARING :—Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bunt, C.V.0., Canon Willis, Mr and Misses Broadbent, Messrs F A Frost, B 8 Townroe, Nash, Bradshaw, Morris, H Rogers, II Hope, G Walker, W Peter Ryland', Captain Crosfteld, Councillor Bellian and Captain Wareing. Alderman BEN.NETT also moved the following resollt- Hon :—" That the Mayor and 'Mayoress for the time being be appointed presidents of the Branch and Ladies' Auxiliary respectively that Mr Bradshaw be appointed bon. treasurer, and Mr E Morris hon. secretary.' This was seconded by Mr G WALKER and curried. Mr. Boar proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor for his services in the chair, which was seconded by Mr U WALKER, the MAYOR briefly responding.</p>
        <p>A Local Dot Fancier's Success. At Wallasey Open Dog Show. on Saturday last, Mr. F. Townley, 441, Wellfield-street, Warrington, was successful in winning 2nd prize.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>By the visit of Mendel, the blind pianist, this week, lovers of muiic are provided with one of the most interesting pianoforte " turns " ever seen at this house. Mandel is undoubtedly possessnl of remarkable abilities as an improvisor and executant. His repetition of any original melody played by a member of the audience once only and his rendering of three tunes, coupled with the singing of a different song, are only samples of his achievments. Vernon Watson' imperaonations of Wilkie Bard and George Formby are very clever, although in the latter the voice is far away from that of the original "George." If lacking in this respect, however, he soon makes amends by the singing of a coster song, which does not fail to earn its due reward. An abundance of mirth is pro- vided by Fre I Maitland and Miss Pauline Walker and Co., in their catital presentment of " Roots, Rash Chemist." The flrmer an manager of the shop prescribes for his patients either to "kill. or cure"— in the first respect as a help for his pal, the under- taker. Eina Latonne as a male impersonator is quite at borne and her songs are well chosen. A number of really clever tricks at diabolo are performed by Sergeant Brennan and Julian Ross in a saccess as a light comedian and actor vocalist. Doris Dame, a dainty little girl, and Eileen Hall in her specially arranged song scena, also contribute a couple of pleasing items. The picturas are still a prominent eature of the programme and the orchestra contri- utes choice selections with their usual taste.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Provost s-f Oats</head>
        <p>Provost s-f Oats</p>
        <p>are the nutriment of the best Oats that the fertile farms of Scotland can produce, freed orall husk and waste, made ready for your table in the most perfectly equipped mills in the world by special processes unknown to other millers.</p>
        <p>Look for the word Provost on the packet, and firmly decline all imitations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>assi-t and advise mothers in every sensib'e way as to the proper treatment of their offspring. Each mother should be willing to put her pride in her pocket and avail herself of instructioa in those matters on which her knowledge was at all defective. (Applause.) The judging than began, and at half-past four the MAY4III announced the results and presented the prizes and certificates as follows : _ _ _</p>
        <p>Best Babies in the show : Frederick Potter, 72,1 Scott-street • Alice Thompson, 33, Scotland-road. Boys under three months 1, Edmondsoo, 383, Wellfield-Street ; 2' Chorley, 17, Booth-atteet ; 3, Thomas Rigby, 112, Liverpool-road ; ho., Robert lleakio, 8, Litton's-row ; Richard Morland, 20, Amelia-street ; Was. Barlow, 79, Wellfield-street. Girls under three uvtotht : 1, Nellie 1.,e, 31, Thelwallione ; 2, Elsie Wilson, 26, Clifton-street ; 3, May Connor, 51, Foondryratreet ; h.c.. Phyllis Wake- field, 11, Lezden-street ; Annie leywoxl, 385, Wellfield-str, t.</p>
        <p>Boys between three and six months: 1, Wiirred Monks, 3, Arnold-street ; 2, George Strettle, 363, linutstord-road ; 3, Thomas Isherwood, 31, Greenall- street ; h.c., John Woodhead, 8, Clifton-strt3t ; "homes Snell 176 Gorsev-lane. . .</p>
        <p>Girls between three and six months: 1, Alice Thompson, 23, Scotland-road ; 2, Bertha Hill, 28, Wellfield-street ; 3, Christina Neston, 4, Coronation Buildings; h c., May Leeson, 33, Clegg.street ; Lilian Fairbrother, 105, Wellfield-street. Boys between Fl 2 and nine months : 1, Frederick Fr, man, 26, Ernest-street ; 2, John Bolton, 70, Selby-street; 3, John Dolan, 68, Algernon-street; hc, Charles Warren, 73, Knutsford-road ; William Mason, 21, Napier-street. Girls between six and nine months 1, Margaret Longhorn, Watercress House, Lymm (born in the borough) ; 2, Emily Jones, 96, Wilkinson-street ; 3, ELI la Hought m, '290, Knntsbrd-road ; h.c, Selina Corbett, 191, Liverpool-road ; Dorothy Rue, Wharf Cottage, Quay Fold: Olive Vcst, 36, Cumberland- street.</p>
      </div>
