      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>ph A FAMILY DOCTOR.]</head>
        <p>ph A FAMILY DOCTOR.]</p>
        <p>TO TTIOSE ABOUT TO MARRY.</p>
        <p>Well, Edwin and Angelina, I hear you are going to get married this year, and I offer you my good wishes. I sympathise with you in the unfortunate circumstance that every- one you meet gives you good advice: good advice is terribly boring .to young people.</p>
        <p>However, it hattpens to' be iity liusin'ess' to help the British public to keep the doctor out of the house. Are von quite sure that sour health is as good is you can make it? know how hard you have to work, and that you cannot always choose the way of life you are compelled to lead, but are you doing your best? Edwin, if you stand before the altar and solemnly take oath that you will cherish Angelina, you are belying your oath the moment you take it if you bring to your marriage a feeble stock of health. I am very pleased to know you have saved up a store of money; now take a little more trouble to save up a store of health.</p>
        <p>0 GOOD WISHES.</p>
        <p>Dear Angelina, the yeals go 1:y very quickly; in ten years there will be others to look after besides Edwin; pray that those others may be strong and healthy. Ask Edwin to join a Friendly Society, so that 50nes.1-"g will be coming ; n if he is ill. Re- member, Angelina, that nothing ruins a I borne like an unhealthy hnsband ; and as for you, Edwin, there will i.e no excuse if a young man like you falls ill. If everyone enjoyed good health I should be ruincd, but none of your friends, del.r children, wishes you good health more heartily than I do; I only ask von to remember one thing, that if you fall ill through carch ssness you must not expect sympathy frost_ me. but rebukes, fining, imprisonment, and finally, if you do not reform, deportation.</p>
        <p>CRUSADE AGAINST CONSUUPTION. At the present time a fierce crusade is being waged against consumption, and I want to he in it. The crusade is under the auspices of the National Sue:ety for the Prevention of Consumption, and the main object is to enlighten and educate the people on the methods of prevention. Ignorance is to be dispersed and indifference banished. Lectures will be given, and an exhibition opened free to the public. The microbes of consumption are beginning to shiver in their shoes. This campaign particularly appeals to me, because I am always preach- ing the blessed doctrine of fresh air and good food. Consumption is to be fought, not by buckets full of drugs, but by fresh air and good food. No more stuffy parlours, ,no more stuffy bedrooms, no more stuffy churches; away with blind alleys where the air cannot get a free run through; pull down the dark houses where the light of day never penetrates; ft esh air in the schools, in the streets, in the houses—fresh air first, last, end always.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>FIRST PROCESS.</head>
        <p>THE FIRST PROCESS.</p>
        <p>In the manufacture of any article it is the first process that is of greatest importance : if the first process is badly done all subse- quent stages of manufacture must be corre- .pondingly defective. Our bodies are manu- factories, and we have to make brain, bone, and muscle (especially brain, please) out of gga and bacon and bread and butter. How .an you expect this wonderful process to be properly contrived if you have bad teeth? The first process is mastication, and if you have no grinders you throw a lot of extra work on the stomach and liver; and when these organs complain you start your favourite pastime of drug-swilling. To put a bottle of medicine in your stomach be- cause you have no grinders is folly. Have your mouth put in order, and your indiges- tion will disappear, provided you have not delayed too long. As for the man who has foul teeth, with a septic discharge around them, I give him up in despair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT.</head>
        <p>ROOM FOR IMPROVEMENT.</p>
        <p>I was reading a book of reminiacences by An old lady of eighty-four recently, and noticed her remark that bath-rooms were unknown in country houses in her child- hood. How shocked we all are to read that! We have made same headway since eighty years ago. Nearly every house has a bath now some people find it very convenient to put the coals in. In another eighty years what will people be saying of us and our habits of cleanliness% I think there is no doubt that the condition of the heads of school-children, as *et out in the official records, will be regarded with amazement and abhorrence, to the everlasting shame a modern parents. Hundreds of thousands of English inotkrs would rather have their children dirty than clean, and hundreds of nurses have to be employed for no other , purpose than to examine the children. Cats take such endless trouble to keep their kittens clean. Darwin said we were de- suended from animals: if only we could ascend to their level nivain !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>LESSON IN BREATHING</head>
        <p>A LESSON IN BREATHING</p>
        <p>I said a week or two ago that many of you do not know how to breathe, and a eQrresp,ondent asks me to explain further. Tly2 reason I brought this accusation against you was that when a pupil begins a course of singing lessons the breathing exercises generally give rise to a headache or giddiness. When the pupil has learnt how to take full breaths the blood le aerated in a mare complete and satisfactory manner. and, far from having a headache, the head feels clearer. The breath should be taken through the nose, because the air is warmed and tiltered of floating impurities the Cm- st must expand all round by giving free ! play to the ribs (ladies with tight cc.rsete !tleose note), and the diaphragm. that sheet !of muscle which separates the chest cavity from the abdomen, must descend. This diaphragm, or mid-riff, gives us the link between respiration and digestion. If the 'stomach is blown out with wind or occupied hy a surfeit of turkey and plum-pudding. the diaphragm is unable to descend, and the consequence is that the diaphragm is im- peded, and the breath is not taken properly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>PUTTING TOUR FOOT DOWN</head>
        <p>PUTTING TOUR FOOT DOWN</p>
        <p>The foot is an arch, in fact, we rest our weight on only three points in the sole of the foot—the ball of the big toe, the ball of the little toe, and the heel. Thus the foot is constructed to give springiness to the gait cad to reduce t shock in jumping,</p>
        <p>nuu ratting. 5n06,1”..fg [AO, a in your foot, even in ordinary walking, with your eleven stone of weight, the jerk would be considerable if it were not for the cane- fully-planned mechanism of the bones and ligaments.</p>
        <p>PENALTY 03 ILL-FITTING BOOTS. My complaint is that you upset the mechanism of the foot by wearing ill-fitting boots. "Oh," you say, "wonderful mocha- die/11, is it? We will soon alter that." And you proceed to screw your feet up until they are deformed, or, what is far worse, you take the delicate, soft-boned feet of your little children and distort them. Result : Bunions, corns, fiat-foot, in-growing toe- nail, hammer-toe, rheumatism. For grown- up people who deform their cwn fea the punishment is three months' without the option, but for those who ruin the feet of helpless children it is six months' hard labour and a shameful reputation amongst the more thoughtful parents in the neigh- bourhood.</p>
        <p>-:0 : -</p>
        <p>ANSWERS TO CORRESPONDENTS,</p>
        <p>A inik.—Sorey, cannot deal with .yout subject.</p>
        <p>trf.tp on enlarged thyroid, but you had 1,..11.4., /30.1 V it to a doctor.</p>
        <p>Worried.— All tujuours me( 14 seen by • iturpon. Troubled.--fh, s doctor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>NOTES ON NEW BOOKS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON NEW BOOKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Maurice Hewlett's new book "The song o Benny" (published by Messrs. Macmillan and Co.). is a romance of "those dim days when Maximilian 111. reigned over 'Lelia. and Sir Prosper le Gai adventured in Morgrannt, as has been related elsewhere." and this will explain that it is to 'The forest lovers" that one must torn to find any points of resemblance to Mr. Hewlett's former books. It int:mute the history of the headstrong, bloodstained House of Renny, and the story of the loves and hates, the nobility and ignominy, of the various actors in the drama is told with unabated vigour and picturesqueness. " Fairies Afield " is the title of Mrs. Molesworth's new book for young readers, published by Messrs. Macmillan and Co., with charming illustrations by Miss G. D. Hat.mond. The took coutaics four stories, in all of which the " good people" play a prominent part, the lesson to be learnt that fairy gifts are only bestowed upon the deserving, and that patience, and pity, sympathy, self-sacrifice, and such good qualities, reap always the magic reward of the " hundred-fold " raurn.</p>
        <p>" The Wonderful Garden. "Miss Nesbit's new story for young people is so charmingly told that it must at once become a favourite with every child who reads it. The three C.'s% Caroline, Charlotte aid Charles, whose parents are in ludia, are a charming trio of two sisters and a brother who go to spend a long holiday with an uncle. He lives in a beautiful house that has a still more beautiful garden —the Wonderful Garden from which the book takes its title. It is with the doings of the children dur;ag their long visit here that the (dory is concerned. They plan and carry out the most delightful and original of acherres, and their last eaterprise of all earls so splendidly that - bat it would not be fair to tell. The little reader mu‘t find out for herself or himself, and in doing so 101 make acquaintance with a very j fascinating story that appeals equally to boy or girl. It is illustrated by Mr. H. It. MiJfar and published by I Macmillan.</p>
      </div>
