        <p>" The doctor explained that St. Vitus' Davos frequently followed Rheumatism, and said that Mend bad • very severe attack. She took mcre medicine, but made no better progress. Tie mueees of her neck and face contracttd and drew her features out of shape; then she lost the power of speech. At last ' Maud got no hopelessly afflicted that we could only tete her cut of dome in an invalid's carriage, strapping her in to keep her from jerking herself out. "It was a neighbour who recommended Dr. William&amp; Pink Pills. At first we gave Maud half a Pill ctusbed in milk. After some time the convul- sions were less severe, and as we persevered with the treatment she regained the power of speech. Gradually all hysteria and nervousness ceased away. Then in time Maud cruld get about without assistance. . _ . .</p>
