      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>DRUIDS AT THE CRYSTAL PALACE.</head>
        <p>DRUIDS AT THE CRYSTAL PALACE.</p>
        <p>Quaint and picture,que scents were witnessed at the Crystal Palace recently during the planting of an oak sapling by the Imperial Grand Lodge of Druids, to connnemorate the Coronation of King George V. The ceremony was performed with 113Vstic rites by the Arch Druid of England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>INSURING SCHOOL CHILDREN. Cheshire Education Committee's New Departure.</head>
        <p>INSURING SCHOOL CHILDREN. Cheshire Education Committee's New Departure.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Cheshire Education Committee, at Crewe, on Monday, a discussion took place with reference to insurance against risks in connection with accidents to school children. It was decided to insure all children attending Council schools. A question was asked as to liability for children attending nonTrovided schools. . . .</p>
        <p>Professor (loaner mad the committee had taken the opinion of counsel on the subject, and that was to the effect that the county could not undertake the statutory liability of the managers of such schools,who were responsible for any structural defects in non- provided schools. It was decided to give the managers the opportunity of being included in the policy taken out by the educa- tion authority on condition that they contribute the amount of the premiums in respect of such children.</p>
        <p>A RGENTA.</p>
        <p>Warringtonians are each -week having opportuni ties for testing the qualities of American chilled beef and New Zealand chilled mutton ani lamb. The Argenta Meat Company, Ltd., which spealizes in this clam of food, are exhibiting huge supplies at their Branch No. 35, Bridge-street. A trade Jurnal, speaking of the products such as are obtainable at the Argenta shop, , says," Nothing can he liner, more tender, toothsome, and palatable than American chilled beef. It is not to be classed in the same category PS frozen beef. It is as good as, and usually in better condition than prime English or Scotch.' What applies to the beef applies equally well to the mutton and lamb, for the latter is drawn from New Zealand which, as it well known, produces some of the finest sheep in the world. A large supply or sheep and lambs, and beef in corres- pondingly large quantity, are waiting for purchasers, and an inspection of it, and a comparison in prices, is certain to prove sufficient incentive to a trial. A large refrigerator at the shop, worked on tbe same principle as those on the vessels on which it is brought to this country, keeps the meat in excellent condition, and purchasers wiil lind a view of it highly interesting,</p>
        <p>LORD WOLMER AND PAYMENT OF MEMBERS. Lord Wolmer, M.P.. speaking at Blackbrook, on Saturday night, after adversely criticising the Insurance Bill, referred to the payment of a salary of .C4OO to M.P.'s, and said he did not go into politics to mike a living out of it. They might be mire he NM not going to potato), of the money into his pocket, for it would be much better spent on many institutions in the division.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>CHESHIRE STUDENTS AND CANADIAN UNIVERSITY.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE STUDENTS AND CANADIAN UNIVERSITY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of Cheshire County Education Com- mittee, at Crewe, 011 Monday, Dr. Hodgson presiding, the Rev. J. Want Bird (chairmen of the Higher Education Committee) moved that the director of education be instructed to communicate with the authority of the MGM University, Montreal, with a view to ascertaining whether arrangements could be made by which students holding Cheshire County 1 scholarships in engineering or other similar subjects I could be transferred to that University for the latter portion of their university career,the M'Oill University recognising the lira portion of their course here towards the portion required for its degrees. That was, he said, a proposition of considerable importance, and if they were able to carry it out it would be a mutual:advantage. The Cbairman said this would provide a better outlet for their scientifically educated students than they had in this country. Of course, there would have to be reciprocal relations between the English and the colonial universities. klanchester and Liverpool Universities would have to be approached,because they received most of the Cheshire students now.He thought such a scheme might do something towards linking up this country and the colonies educationally with the highest industrial departments of life. The recommendation was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>CHRONIC BRONCHITIS and BLOOD-SPITTING. Cured at 76 by VENO'S LIGHTNING CO JGH CURE.</head>
        <p>CHRONIC BRONCHITIS and BLOOD-SPITTING. Cured at 76 by VENO'S LIGHTNING CO JGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joe. R. Hunt, 38, Byron Road, Small Heath, Birmingham, writes r—" I am 76 years of age, and for years had b?en a great sufferer from bronchitis and blood-spitting, brought on probably owing to my trade of brava-mixer. I tried various so-called remedies in vain, and was at a lose what to do to find relief. Then I began taking Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, and although I only took one bottle, the cough wax at once eased and the blood- spitting is now entirely gone." Venn's Lightning Cough CIITA am awarded the Orand I'm and Gold Itte&amp;l for surety and ellicacy at the recent l'aris Health Exhibition, and is a perfect remedy for all cheat, lung sad throat troubles. Price 90., Is. Id. and 2s. 3d. or all chemists. 'l. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>.0,...."..,‘,....".,,,„\,..",..,„.* ANTIQUARIAN CHAPTERS</head>
        <p>.0,...."..,‘,....".,,,„\,..",..,„.* ANTIQUARIAN CHAPTERS</p>
        <p>HUMPHREY NICHOLS.</p>
        <p>A DELIGHTFUL MANCHESTER</p>
        <p>ECCENTRIC.</p>
        <p>Enshrined in the pages of " Temple Bar" of a full generation ago there is a brief refer- ence to Humphrey Nichols old-time parish clerk and a Manchester worthy. The facts given proved sufficiently appetising to send us in quest for more, and as they include a Warrington incident which deserves preser- vation we have decided to devote a brief chapter to Humphrey Nichols. There may be just a few people living who remember seeing a shabbily attired old gentle- man, who though,verging upon his ninetieth ' year, trudged regularly to Shudehill market on a Saturday morning, haggled long with the hucksters and finally carried away his purchases in one of the coloured pocket handkerchiefs known as a " breakfaster." A stranger in Manchester would have passed him by as a penurious and poverty-stricken bachelor, and would have been surprised to learn that he was one of the richest men in the city.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>ECONOMY EXTRAORDINARY.</head>
        <p>ECONOMY EXTRAORDINARY.</p>
        <p>A rich man Humphrey Nichols certainly was, and in the getting and the giving of his money he made himself decidedly interest- ing. He was parish clerk of Manchester when the old order prevailed and when ecclesiastical dues were claimed for duties done for the whole parish. Possibly this drawing in of small fees revealed the possi- bility of making many small sums into a large one, and he may have been consti- tutionally careful in matters of money. Certain it is that in middle age he was a scientific saver, and settled into penurious ways of which people to-day can form little conception. In threadbare garments,and always accom- panied by an old-fashioned horn-handled gingham umbrella he was a familiar figure in Manchester streets, but not the most expert huckster in the city ever hoped to live to score over him by a single farthing when it came to buying and selling. Was it a matter of potatoes for Sunday's dinner or the price of cabbage? the venerable clerk would enquire at a dozen stalls lest an odd farthing should go astray. When he went to live in the suburbs and newspapers could not easily be seen free of charge he began (wondrous prodigality) to purchase one. The newsagent, pitiful at seeing an octogenarian trudge through all weathers to fetch his morning sheet offered to deliver them for a penny a week. "A , penny a week" said the indomitable econom- ist, "I will come for them myself." And he did.</p>
        <p>The same suburban newsagent repaired umbrellas and when one of the whale bone ribs of the old gingham came loose, Mr. Nichols favoured him with an enquiry. "How much will you repair it for ? " "Oh, threepence Mr. Nichols." The parish clerk looked adamant. "I can get it done for two- pence in town." And the umbrella went to town. _</p>
        <p>Mr. John Kay recently wrote to a Man- chester paper to say that he was old enough to have seen and to remember this altogether wonderful old _man. He writes :</p>
        <p>I once, and only 011CO3 had the felicity of seeing Mr. Nichols. It was a Saturday morning. I stood at the counter of one of our Manchester banks. Whilst standing there in came an elderly gentleman clad in threadbare robes, carrying an old-fashioned and well-worn umbrella, and a small bundle of field-grown rhubarb. 'ls my b ,ok ready?" he asked. "It is, sir." "Then I i will take t," he said, ;tad left. "Do you know that gentleman ? " said the teller. "Ida not," said I. " Well," he said "It is Mr. Humphrey Nichols."</p>
        <p>He invested his savings in ground and chief rents which gave him little trouble in the collecting. He was his own book-keeper and clerk and made out the demand notes himself. For these he would use up old ledgers cutting out the portions left blank, and as disused ledgers could be had for asking he was able to run on for years with- lout expending a single farthing on paper. The provision of envelopes was more difficult, but he managed to turn inside out the ones which came to him and the waste Ipaper baskets of the city yielded a supply whenever he called to make a courteous request for a fresh supply. The man who had thus collected paper and envelopes was not likely to waste money on postage and Nicholas completed his com- mercial economy by delivering the notes himself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>LEFT HAND AND THE RIGHT.</head>
        <p>THE LEFT HAND AND THE RIGHT.</p>
        <p>And now the other side. If some observant scribe could have chronicled his benevolences the story could have been aptly placed under the title "The left hand of Humphrey Nichols," for the right hand never knew what the left was doing. This strangely made mortal after saving a fraction of a farthing by turning an envelope would place in it a five pound bank note and push it under the door of some poverty stricken home not even waiting to Fee that it was discovered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>1:11AT "ELDERLY GENTLEMAN."</head>
        <p>1:11AT "ELDERLY GENTLEMAN."</p>
        <p>I The Manchester papers would herald a Igift in language like the following : "An elderly gentleman called yesterday at the-Hospital and left a roll of bank I notes value £l,OOO without telling his name." The treasurers knew the identity of that "elderly gentleman "and smiled. The founder of the Boys' and Girls' Refuges in Manchester, Mr. Leonard K. Shaw, said that Mr. Nichols was an abiding friend of cripples, orphans, widows and the destitute He kept an open bowl of money and wh e Mr. Shaw went to present another of hi numerous requests the bowl would be placed before him with a request that he would help himself; and the bowl went back without the money being counted. . . . .</p>
        <p>Strange to say, this parsimonious haggler of Shudehill market never enquired whether cases were needy. It was enough that Mr Shaw reported upon them. There was. never a denial and the name of the donor was never allowed to leak out. HE PREPARES TWO urrLE SURPRISES.</p>
        <p>The two charities in which he was most interested were the Salford Royal Infirmary, and the funds for the widows and orphans of ' the clergy of the dioceses of Chester and Manchester. Towards the one he gave in all 'fifteen thousand pounds; towards the other seven thousand: but the way he did so was extraordinary and characteristic. One day, when he was in his eighty-second year, he called at the office of the treasurer of the Infirmary, and, on finding that official out, he took up a scrap of paper lying on the desk, and standing by it wrote: "H. Nichols will be obliged if Mr. Sbelmerdine will be at H. N.. 8 house to-morrow, Friday morning, by ten o'clock, on urgent business." At the time appointed the treasurer found the old gentleman sitting without a fire, with his overcoat on for warmth. He excused himself for not having a fire on such an inclement morning, alleging that he was shortly going out. He then laid upon the table Ten notes of £5OO each,</p>
        <p>nine of £lOO each, three of £4O each, twenty- three of .£2O, twenty-one of £lO, forty- two fit £5, sovereigns .£7o—in all .£7,000 'These 108 banknotes were all spread straigiA out and placed between the covers of an old book from which the leaves had been torn, tisd round with a piece of old cotton tape, and handed over without receipt or acknowledgment.</p>
        <p>HIS LITTLE TRIP TO WARRINGTON. Next morning an old man and gingham I umbrella were visible in Church-street, Warrington, and the old man was enquiring for the Rectory. The Hon. H. Powys, after- wards Bishop of Soder and Man, was then Rector, and was treasurer of the widow and orphans fund of the Chester Diocese. The Bishop had guests and was just going down to dinner. 1 The footman confronted old Humphrey at the door and was about to tell him to go round to the back door, when the Rector caught sight of him, and asked him his business. This was soon told; he had come to give a trifle of money to the fund for the widows and orphans of the clergy. On being called into the study, a shabby old pocket- book was produced, and a banknote—a very dirty one—laid on the table. The Rector took it up, thinking it a five-pound note. "Have you looked at it?" said Mr. Nichols.</p>
        <p>"Yes," said the Rector. "Look again." The Rector could not believe his eyes : It was for five hundred pounds. " Perhaps," said the old gentleman, " I'm keeping those fine folk waiting for their dinner; but if you are not tired of counting, lam not tired of giving." So the upshot of it all was that he put seven thousand pounds down, but not without a sly hit at his reception. "Tell that fine gentleman of yours," said he, with reference to the foot- man, "to be civil to an old man, even if he should happen to wear a shabby coat." Mr. Powys asked him to stay to dinner. " Dinner ! Why, I ate my mutton chop before I started from Manchester. What would your lords and ladies say to sitting beside a shabby old fellow like me?" The "Temple Bar" reference to Humphrey Nichols declares that he was "as munificent as he was mean." But was he mean? Some- how we cannot agree to this. He lived to save and he lived to give, a double purpose that blended into one. He simply saved to give and became an adept. His economies were not mean, they were courageous— splendidly courageous, and none but a hero will ever repeat them. He lifted economy to the level of a scientific hobby. And in a world where people are allowed to choose their own hobbies, why should the old man be grumbled at? A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>THA' MUN WAIT!</head>
        <p>THA' MUN WAIT!</p>
        <p>AN INCIDENT IN A LOCAL 'BUS.</p>
        <p>One afternoon when returning to the village of P----- from Warrington, I enjoyed about a quarter of an hour's great fun in listening to a real I broad-tongued country-woman in animated argument with the 'bus conductor and a passenger. The afternoon was a hot and sultry one, and when we hhd got a mile or 83 from town, the 'bus stopped Ito pick up a woman of ample proportions, very red in the face and burdened with innumerable orcels and 'baskets. When she had finally settled on the seat, I she occupied space that would have accommodated four ordinary people, and with • gasp of relief pro- ceeded to mop her perspiring face. At this juncture the conductor appeared with his box for the lady's fare. For a moment she did not nee it, and was brought to realizing her duties by the sharp remark from the youth : " Fare, please." " Tha man wait," said the woman tartly. " Da'ant you come stickin' t' box in folkse's faces afore them time to breathe. I tell thee tha won wait !"</p>
        <p>The boy being of a phlegmatic nature, never moved an inch but still held the box before his irate Pas.srg?!... .</p>
        <p>" I tell thee tha moo wait," she reiterated?' Here's me, 'urried like a don't-know-what to catch this 'ere 'bus, and as soon as I sit down on comes and sticks t' box in front o' 819. Now, go on, tha men wait. Tim' want's a box beind thi, tha does, yer imperdent lad."</p>
        <p>Just then, a man who was sitt'ng next to the ruffled lady, unwisely intervened. " Minus," said he, " tbour't a woman as wants to be someone, and can't." "Thee hold thi noise," said the woman. " What's it got t) do with thee, you imperdent creature yer." " What ho! she bumps," said the man irrevelantly and irreverently. "Do you know you ought to pay yer fare as soon as you get in t"bus, and you've gone contr'y to the boy's rules. He's bin told to collect fares, and you won't pay him," be concluded. " Won't I," retorted the woman, now more heated than ever. "1 wants to cheat no one; am an honest livin woman I am. And, young man let me tell thi tha'll rue thi words, tha wilt! Wait till I get out o' this 'ere 'bus, and the'll see! " _</p>
        <p>To the boy—' Ere fetch that there box" (dropping two halfpennies in). "Now, have I put mi fare in t' box? tell me ? Go On! The'll 'ere about this from thi bore, tha will, mi lad—and thee" (turning to the man) " thall rue thi words yer imperdent young thing !! . .</p>
        <p>Just then the 'bus stopped, and the irate &amp;roe gathered her belongings together and descended. She was still red in the face, and as she moved slowly away, the rumblings of the storm could still be heard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SCHOOL NURSE ASTONISHED.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL NURSE ASTONISHED.</p>
        <p>Wonderful .am-Buk Cure of Ringworm Confirmed by Medical Officer.</p>
        <p>The remarkable way in which Zam-Buk cleared little Willie Ward's head, body anil limbs of shocking ringworms astonished even the school nurse who knew the badness of the attack. The boy's parents live at South Essex Water, Woodford Bridge Road, Barking- side, Ilford. The father, Mr. A. W. Ward, said to a reporter :— . .</p>
        <p>" Willie caught ringworm at school, where other boys had been down with it. The outbreak began with quite a small pimply sore on his cheek - a simple enough looking thing. But in a few days it bad spread into a wide ring of inflammation, and the skis was dry and itchy. A chemist told me it was ring- worm, and the school nurse confirming this, we were ordered to keep Willie at home, and base medical advice. The ringworm spread to his body, legs, and head. He had twelve sores on his body and legs, and several big ones on his face and head. "The doctor burned the ringworms, but though some died away from Willie 'a body fresh ones appeared on his head, and we were no nearer a cure. At last the doctor saw the usefulness of proceeding further with this treatment, and said we had better take the boy to the hospital and have him operated on with X-rays. Poor Willie was in a wretched state, for the itchy ringworms gave him no peace night or day. There seemed nothing for it but the hospital, especially when the school's iredical officer confirmed our doctor'e advice.</p>
        <p>" But I dislike operations and decided, before send- ing Willie to the hospital, to try Zarn-Buk, which I bad heard very well spoken of. The ringworms were first washed with Zam-Ruk Medicinal Soap, which pro Ned very cleansing. Zarri-Buk emeared orer the VON% ',topped the itching and cooled Willie's is/famed akin. It didn't take a lot of pereeverance , with Zam-Buk to show us that the balm was killing ' the ringworm, for the ugly sores gradually shrivelled upend died away, replaced by clear healthy skin. This took place all over Willie's body and limbs until he was quite free from disease. " Willie was rest; tied ari rompletely eared by die Medical Officer of health, and returned to school. When the school name saw him she was astonished, and said she couldn't have believed that anything could have cured such a shecking case in et, short a time.</p>
        <p>Asm-Blik is accomplishing those marvellous cures every day. Lout do the same in your case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>ON GENERAL STRIKES.</head>
        <p>ON GENERAL STRIKES.</p>
        <p>The Danger to the Workers and to Society.</p>
        <p>It is obvious to any intelligent person who reads , the newspapers, if only cursorily, thet recent labour troubles in this country are not s'mply isolated out- bursts of unrest, but are evidences of a widespread discontent common to all civilised Rations. ,The discontent is gsner.il and deep and cannot be dis- regarded with impunity, especially in view of the fact that a sense of ibe solidarity of labour is becoming more and more international. It is not proposed in this article to enter into any detailed examination of the canoes of this sidespread unrest ; but it is fairly otvious that the main causes are the following : Increased cost of living is not bclanced by adequate increass in wages. Refusal of certain important capitalist interests to confer with men's delegates and officers. Increased facilities for quick production of goods, tending to greater irregularity of employ-</p>
        <p>. . meet. Impatience of men with slowness of legal methods of reform, and a su4picion of bias in the Courts of the land.</p>
        <p>The last cause makes the idea of a general strike more attractive to the workers, and it is in their view a quick and p3s.ibly conclusive method of attack and defence in the age-long warfare between themselves and the powers of privilege and monopoly. This article is intended to discuss the general strike as a method.</p>
        <p>When we speak of the genera strike we must begin by defining the word very clearly. We are not concerned with the general strike of a single trade. We are not thinking of the limited movement of one trade no matter how vast its extent. On the other hand it would be futile to say that there could not be a general strike unless ell wage earners in art depart- ments of production quit work simultaneously. The working class is too much dispersed for such unanimity to be possible or even conceivable. A general strike means that the most impotent trades, those that dominate the whole productive system, shall stop work at the same time. If for instance the railroad employees, the miners, dockers, and longshoremen, the employees in the spinning and weaving industries, and the building trade employees in the great cities, were to quit work simultaneously, we would say there was a general strike. It is not necessary that every workman in these trades should be out. It is sufficient if those trades, where the power of aapital is most concentrated and the power of labour best organised, decide on a suspension of work, and it is enough if they are backed up by such a largo number of workmen that the work of those trades is practically stopped. It cannot be objected that a general strike of this character is either illusory or useless. The growth of the labour movement makes increasingly possible this kind of concerted action. Such action can exercise an enormous influence on public opinion. It is no longer a single trade, however important, that refuses to work, but a whole union of trades. The movement ceases to be a trade movement. It becomes a class movement. Such a movement could not be barren of important results, organised and carried on by the essentially productive class, that class for which no substitute can be found, because none exists.</p>
        <p>There is no magic virtue in the general strike. A general strike is practical or otherwise, useful or dis• astrous according to the 'conditions under which it takes place, the method it employs, and the end it</p>
        <p>proposes. There are three indispensable conditions for the utility of a gene-al strike. Ist. The working class must be deeply and fully convinced of the importance of the object for which it is declared. 2nd. A large section of public opinion must be prepared to recognise the justice of that object. 3rl. The general strike must not seem like a disguise for violence, but simply the exercise of the legtl right to strike, more systematic, vaster, and with a more clearly marked class character.</p>
        <p>It is essential that the bulk of organised labour should attach very great importance to the object for which the strike is declared. Neither the decision of the Trade Union Congresses nor the orders of Work- men's Committees would be strong enough to drag the workers into a t !niggle that is always formidable. To brave privation and misery, even with the object of escaping front a si•uation in which ens is sutk, requires great energy. Such energy cannot be roused in an entire clam without the influence of really passionate feeling. And passion in its turn is not aroused in men's souls to the degree when it becnnea a working and fighting force, except by an interest both very close and very overwhelming, by a very important aim that can be immediately realised. The working class is willing to fight for definite and great ends, for rositive, extended and immed ately practicable reforms. Uuder conditions such as there, but under no others, the signal given by the labour organisations will be obeyed. It is not enough for it to follow its own inner impulse if it has not also received a mandate from without. It must have demonstrated to a notable fraction of public opinion that its claims are legitimate and immediately realisable. Every general strike will necessarily bring alx ut disorder in economic relations ; it will upset many traditions, and go counter to many interests. The opinion of the mass of the nation will therefore be very emphatically ranged against those on whom rests the responsibility for a prolongation of the conflict. This opinion will not fix the responsi- bility on the capitalist class and will not condemn it with any force unless the justice of the strikers' claims, and the possibility of satisfying them immediately, have been clearly demonstrated by an ardent and serious propaganda. On the contrary, if the neutral masses have not been prepared beforehand and partly Neon over they will decide against the strikers. And as no force, even a revoluticnary one, can hold out against the public opinion of the nation, the working class would suffer a widespread defeat. The theory is that a general strike of the meat important trades wruld be euongh to bring on a social revolution, that is. the fall of the whole capitalist system. The economic life of the country would be suspended, railways would be deserted, the coal neces.ary for industry would remain buried in the ground; everywhere there would be stoppage in distribution and production. Naturally great discomfort would result. The workers, in stepping exchange and production, would be starving themselves, and would therefore be forced to adopt violent methods in order to live. They would seize food and other provisions wherever they could lay hands on them. The privileged classes, threatened alike in their persons and powessiona, would be shocked and frightened by the movitable anger of the workers, whose time honoured sufferings would be intensified by the crisis of misery and hunger. Hence would come inevitable conflict oetween the workingclasses and the paoic-stricken guardians of capital. At the end of a few days, then, the general strike would become pnrely revolu- tionary in eharacterAnd as the capitalist power would be scattered by the very necessity of keeping watch over the most varied and widespread movement, as the army of repression would be scattered and sub- merged in the flood, the proletariat would be able to overcome the obstacle against which it had hitherto only beat itelf in vain, and, master of the social system at last, would install labour as sovereign. That is the idea. Given this revolutionary meaning the idea is a false one. A tactical movement is especially dangerous when it cannot fail a single time without involving immense disasters for the whole working class. .</p>
        <p>The partisans of the general strike, taking the word in thin sense, are obliged to succeed the first time. If a general strike fail after having recourse to revolutionary violence it will have left the capitalist system intact and armed with implacable fury. The fear of the ruling charnels, and even of a great part of the masses, will express itself in a long succession of reactionary years. And the proletariat will be disarmed, bound and crashed for a long time. Great movements are never set on foot for the attainment of remote and vaguely understood ends. They need something solid to work for ; they demand a clearly-defined specific issue. The most practical representatives of the theory of the general strike are perfectly aware of this. They propose to rouse the working class to action in the first place by certain definite and Fubetantial claims. They hope that this movement, when it has become revolutionary in character, and it is certain to do, will expand naturally in complete commnniem. But precisely here lies the essential error of this policy. It is a trick to entrap the working classes. It propeees to drag them by an irresistible mechanical action, far beyond what was indicated to them at first By the attraction of certain concrete, definite, immediate reforms they are to be led to decide on the great 0 pe r a t ion of the general strike, and ills supposed that once they have become involved in the network</p>
        <p>of the machine, they will bo conveyed almost ant' matically to the Communist Revolution. In a democracy, this is contrary to the who's spirit of t rogrosa. There can only be revolution where there is self-consciousness, and those who construct mechanical contrivances to convey the workers to revolutim,almost without them being aware of what is happeniej, and fancy they can lead them to ti-e point desired by a sort of surprise are going in a direction quite opposite to a real revolutionary movement.</p>
        <p>If the working class is not fully and definitely warned at the outset that it is going on strike for the whole Communist revolution ; if, when it leaves the mines, the lailroads, the factories, the yards, it does not know that it is not to re-enter ther tuna it has accomplished the whole axial revolution, if it is not prepared and resolved to the very centre of its being, and frcm the very beginning, it will be alarmed during the progress of the movement by the tardy revelation of • programme that was not sub- mitted to it. decision before tits initial action was taken. No artifice, no conjuror's trick, will be able to substitute the hidden aim, thus suddenly discovered, for the aim that had been avowed at the outset. . . .</p>
        <p>To imagine that a tocial revolution can result from a misunderstanding, and the workers can be led on beyond its depth is pure childishness. The trans- formation of all social relations cannot be the result of a marrauvre. And, if on the other hand, the working class is prepired beforehand, if it is told in so many words that it ia leaving the workshops not to enter them again until it has abolished capitalism it will be warned by instinct and reflection able that a Society as complicated as ours is not revolutionised by a popular rising of a few days, but by an immense cm- tinuous effort of organi.ation and transformation. From that movement it will shrink back from an I enterprise et vague and illutory, as one would shrink I from an abyss. It ha been said that revolutions are I not decteed. It may be said with still greater truth that they cannot be manufactured and that no machinery of conflict, no matter how vast or how ingenious, can teplace the preparation of events and of minds.</p>
        <p>Moreover it has not been proved at all that the general strike will force the present system to capital sta. Society will set up a resistance proportionate to the magnitude of the interests at stake. Is other words, to a revolutionary general strike that requires of it a sacrifice of its were existence, it will oppose a resistance to the limit of its piwers. Now neither stoppage of production and transportation, nor intended violence to property and pereons, is enough to bring about the overthrow of a Society. Great wars have paralysed industry and transport, yet Society has recovered with surprising elasticity. A general strike cannot do more in that respect than a great war. If the proletarians take possession of the mines and the factories it n ill be a perfectly empty ownership. They will be embracing a corpse, for mince and factories will be no better than dead bodies while economic circulation is suspended and production is stooped. So long as a class does not own and govern the whole social machine it can seize a few factories and yards, if it wants to, but it really possesses nothing. To hold in one's hand a few pebbles of a deserted road is not to be master of transportation. Destruction will be the only resource open to the working class, astonished as it will be at ita powerless. nese in the midst of apparent vicmry. But what good would acts of destruction accomplish except to give a savage character to the rising of the workers ? The tactics of a general strike have for their object, and result in, ilie decomposition, the infinite sub-division of economic and social lift. To stop the locomotives, tie up the stemma and deprive industry of coal, is to substitute the scattered I.fe of innumerable local groups for the unified and general life of the nation. Now this cutting up and sub-division of life is exactly counter to evolution. Division is a return to feudalism. The stoppage cf transportation proposed by the Eupporters of the general strike would lime society to the conditions of an inferior cirilizatiom The owning and governing class has as yet learnt to surrender too small a port of real power to the workers, the possmseir of such formidable force, which at any moment it maybe tempted to use. The owners have given, or rather they bare allowed the working class to retain, so small a measure of confidence in legal evolution, that this class is fascinated more and MOM by the idea of refusing to work at all. Labour dreaming of refusing its service, the heart meditating stopping; that is the profound internal crisis to which we have bees brought by the selfishness of the privi- leged claws, the absence of any definite plan of action on our part. Towards this abyss of a general strike the woiker it feeling himself more and more drawn, at the risk of raining himself, should it fall over, but dragging down with it for years to c 'me either the wealth or the security of the national life. The general strike, quite powerless as • revolu- tionary method, to none the less in its very idea an indication of the highest importance. It is a pro- digious warniag to the privileged classes, rather than a means of liberation for the exploited classes. It is a dull menace in the very heart of Society that, even if it comes to nothing in the end but an impotent out- burst, is witnem to an organic disorder that can only be healed by melt transformation. Finally, if the governing class were mad enough to lay hands on the poor liberties that have beea won or on the wretchedly insufficient means of action of the proletariat, if they threatened or attacked the suffrage, if persecution by employers or by the police made empty f rms of the right to unite in trade unions and the right to strike, then a violent general strike would certainly be the form that a labour revolt would take. It would be their final and desperate mend, more as a means of injuring the enemy than of saving themselves. But the working elms would be the dupe of • fatal illusion and obsession if it mistook what can be only the tactics of despair for a method of revolution. Apart from those convulsive upheavals that escape all forecast and are sometimes the final supreme resource of history, there is only one a,vereign method—the conquest of a legal majority. Only by the aid and support of an enlightened and educated public opinion can the workers be permanently benefited or come to their own. nit public opinion has yet to be created.__</p>
        <p>NODS DEUX.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>HONING PIGEON</head>
        <p>THE HONING PIGEON</p>
        <p>Successful Local Fanciers.</p>
        <p>The annual dinner and prize distribution of the Lancashire Combine of Flying Clubs took place at Blackpool on Saturday. During the season the Combine organised two Mee, one for old birds from NS land one for young birds from Swindon. In the Nantea ' race 8,460 birds competed, and the prise money amounted to .6822, excluaive of local club prizes and 'pools. The winners of the principal prize., which were distributed by Mr S P Griffith,,, of Holten Crow, Northwicb, were as follow. :—Caldwell Brothers, Runcorn ; Flrookman and Clarke, Lsmrn ; Johnson, Warrington ; Newton, Lymm William., Widnes ; Barton and Barlow, Warrington ; Mouton, Lymm. In the young birds' race from Swindon nearly 16,000 birds competed, and the principal winners were : Western Section : Ashcroft and Gorryoott, Earlwatown; Chamberlain, St. Helena ; Barnes, St. Helens ; gibes!, Earlestowo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>GIRL GOING BALD WITH RINGWORM.</head>
        <p>GIRL GOING BALD WITH RINGWORM.</p>
        <p>"I cannot speak too highly of the Cuikons Soap and Ointment. My little girl had ringworm very badly !on the bead and face for quite twelve months I tried all tiodi of remedies but found no benefit from any. ! I took her to a doctor but was tAd it would take a !long time to care her, so decided to go to a skin spital. The ringworm still continued to spread, in ! fact, ehe wan losing her hail through it. I was afrsid she was going bald, and I got quite disheartened until one day I met a friend who had just tinted her little ! girl with the Cuticura Remedies, and she advised me to try them I bought the Cutieura Ointment and Soap, and with the first application I could see an improvement. In a few weeks the ringworm bud disappeared, and the hair which had previcusly fallen off her head was growing fast, many thanks to .Cutieura Soap and Ointment. My little girl will now bear inspection by anyone. She has a splendid skin and • lovely head of hair. . . .</p>
        <p>"Wymoher bad a bad ulcer on her ankle, and it is quite cared with Cuticura Ointment and Soap. I would not be without the Cuticura Remedies and tbe 'Soap is so ref rrishiog anJ cooling in the bath. It is , worth two tablets of ordirary scalp for cleansing ' purposes. I have recommended the Caticura Remedies to ell my friende with equally good results." (Signed) Mrs Lexley, Hale End flame, Hale End road, Walthanistow, Essex, January 14th, 1911. Posit free, from F. Newhery and Sons. ?7, Charterhoose Sq , London, samples of Cuticura Slap and Ointment, with 132-p book on skin treatment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>NEW SERIAL TO-DAY. _</head>
        <p>NEW SERIAL TO-DAY. _</p>
        <p>tAILL RIGHTg REIIERVID.) (Copyrighted in the United States of Americo</p>
        <p>by Alipur W. Marrionont.)</p>
        <p>jie pressed her hand and smiled and went</p>
        <p>off. the eyes of Sir Gregory and Mr. Lyons fol- lowed him, and the lawyer said almost re- luctantly: "I am afraid I think it should have been announced to-day, Sir Gregory, or even before all this. It will come as the greater shock; and it must be told some time or other." Sir Gregory sighed half impatiently. "I !ear you are right, as usual, Lyons. I hate concealment and all its ways. It is nearly always bought at too high a price. But If Lotta knew that everything would be hers nstead of Bulmer's at my death, she—" he broke off with another sigh and glanced across at his wife. "You know what I mean." "I know her temperament," replied the lawyer drily. "It is a constant marvel to me that your two children should be so different; but it is not for me to criticise your daugh- ter. At the same time the property will be hers. It is a monstrous provision; but we can't alter the arrangement." "You touch me on the raw there," cried Sir Gregory, wincing. "That's why I am so anxious that Bulmer's marriage with Mrs. Caulfield should be settled. I don't want the boy to feel that he is making a mercenary marriage; but if it can be done without his knowing how necessary it is, it will be a blessing." "Does Mrs. Caulfield know that your boy will not have anything except the title? " . . .</p>
        <p>asked the lawyer. "No; but she is willing to bring half a mil- lion sterling into the settlement.' "Then it's a pity Buimer is not more mer- cenary. Speaking of marriages, has Mr. Gerald Grainger ever hinted anything to you? I mean about your daughter!" Sir Gregory started and looked sharply at the lawyer. "Gerald Grainger? Why do you ask? I don't like the fellow." _ _</p>
        <p>I;Nor do but—well, as a matter of fact, he cornered me the other day—of course this mustn't go any farther—and poi some ques- tions to me in a roundabout I\ shion as to Miss Trevellock's prospects." "Be can't know anything of that succession business, can he?" Mr. Lyons shrugged his shoulders. "I didn't tell him anything; but, of course, someone may remember that once before— no, twice, I think—the eldest child has been a girl, and the property would have passed to her if the matter had not been arranged in _</p>
        <p>the family. I thought you ought to !rave a hint about Grainger." The two were interrupted then, and the lawyer went away, leaving Sir Gregory some- what ill at ease as the result of the couversa-</p>
        <p>Lion. " You look quite tired and almost worried, Gregory," said Lady Trevellock, when the two were alone in her rooms in the evening. " 1 here has been no hitch in anything, I pe." " Oh, no, dear. And the way you have stood the fatigue of it all has been splendid. Just the brightest spot of it all to me," and he kissed her very affectionately. "You are fel, ling all right?" 'Wonderfully well; but I am getting a little tired. I should go to bed, only Bulmer told me he had something important to say to me, and I am waiting until he comes. I wonder what it is." "I hope it's about his marriage." " That's just what I expect it is," she replied with a smile. "He wishes me to be the first to hear the news. He is so thought.</p>
        <p>There was a knock at the door, and Bulmer came in. "I'm afraid I've kept you out of t!ed, mother, but I just couldn't get dway," he said kindly; and then, seeing his father, he hesitated. "But thought you would be Ohne." Lady Trevellock glanced at her husband. "You are not afraid of your father, surely?" she jested. "I hope not, indeed; but I told you I had some news for you, and—and I think I would rather have told it to you alone first." "Shall I go, then?" laughed Sir Gregory. The belief that it was about his marriage that Bulmer had come to speak had quite re- stored his spirits. "Oh, no, sir"; and Bulmer appeared to make an effort to lay aside his nervous self- consciousness. "It would be best, perhaps, for you to hear it as well. It is about Mrs. Caulfield and my—my marriage." "I think I know what you are going to say, lad," said Sir Gregory genially. "And I am sure I do," declared his wife. Bulmer's face clouded, and he squared his shoulders. _</p>
        <p>"I'm afraid you do not," he said, after a very slight pause. "Of course, I know what your wishes are in regard to my marriage, stopped on seeing the change in his father's face. "I hope you have not set too much store on my marrying Mrs. Caulfield', sir?" he added anxiously. .</p>
        <p>Sir Gregory sat down and averted his head, so that neither his wife nor Bulmer should see his face. "Well?" he asked.</p>
        <p>"I trust with all my heart what I have to say will not be such a disappointment as your manner suggests, father?" Your father has no wishes except for your real happiness, Bulmer," said Lady Trevellock. You know that. Do you mean you cannot marry Mrs. Caulfield?" "Yes, dearest, that's just it. I don't care for her as a man should care for the woman he is to marry, and—l do care for someone else. I meant to ask you to get her to come down to the Chase, mother, so that you and my father could see her and know her for yourselves." There was a long pause. "Kiss me, Bul- mer," said Lady Trevellock then. "Of course she shall come," she added, as he kissed her. . .</p>
        <p>"Thank you, dearest. You are the best mother a man could wish for."</p>
        <p>But Sir Gregory was silent; and his wife glanced nervously across at him. "Father !" said Bulmer, appealingly. But he made no response. "Tell us all about her, Bonnie," said Lady Trevellock, using a term of endearment she had not used since he was a child.</p>
        <p>But Sir Gregory's persistent silence em- barrassed him.</p>
        <p>"She is Miss Helen Paxton, and lives in London," he began awkwardly, and then, with an apparent effort, he drew himself together, as if to face the opposition he now anticipated. "She earns her own living as a typist and in other ways, mother. But she is the bravest and best—" He stopped again as Sir Gregory rose and he saw the expres- sion of his face. Sir Gregory kissed his wife and said, "I must have a talk with Bulmer, dear, before he says anything more." "You're not angry with him, Gregory?" she asked anxiously. "No, my dear. I have not been angry with him for years, and shall not choose this day of all others to begin," he replied with a rather heavy sigh. ne is a man now, wad must decide these things for himself— Fit must know what he is deciding. Come, Buliner." , Bulmer kissed his mother, and bade her good night, and then followed Sir Gregory to his study in silence, lie was greatly dis- turbed by his father's unusual conduct. They had always been the closest friends and com- panions, and the confidence between them had been frank and complete. As he closed the door behind them he went and stood close to Sir Gregory's side, and laid a hand on his shoulder. "I hope you are not going to—" Sir Gregory raised his hand and inter- rupted him. "I shan't attempt to thwart you, my boy; but before you go any farther you must know what, perhaps, I ought to have told you long ago. Have you spoken to this —to Miss Paxton yet?" "No; but I think we understand one another." . . _</p>
        <p>"There is one thing neither of you can un- derstand." He paused and passed his hand across his eyes. 'lt will be hard for you .to</p>
        <p>hear, my boy. It's this. At my death Jou Will, of course, succee# to the title, but every stick and stone of the Chas, will Fio Is Lotta. That is in accordance with rlause in the settlements of the Trevellock efitates. and I am powerless to prevent it. acy id:. is assured for ten thousand pounds, and ,at your mother's death, if she survives me, that will be all the fortune you will ever have." The son was speechless in the first rush of consternation. Twice he made as if to creak, but each time checked himself, and in the embarrassing pause Sir Gregory looked at him almost nervously. "I am very sorry, Bulmer," lie said at</p>
        <p>length. "It is not your fault, sir, except that I wish I had been told before. All this busi- ness to-day then was a sham?" 'You see now why I have built so much on your marrying Mrs. Caulfield. For your sake and the—the future of the family.' For a moment the young man's face har- dened. "I hope you don't mean you would have had ILO sell myself, sir. You have at ways stood to me for a type of honour."</p>
        <p>_ an , 'urge, 0111 PO Sir Gregory. "I hoped you would have had a different motive in making such a mar. riage." Impulsively. the young man thrust out hir hand. "Forgive me, father. I see nor what you mean." Sir Gregory took his son's hand. "You can understand now why I was hit so hard when I heard about this—about Miss Pax- ton," he said slowly, almost wistfully. Bulmer's face brightened with a confident smile. "Oh, she won't mind, sir; and of course it will not make a scrap of difference to me. I'll set to work and earn my living. I'm only sorry I haven't started before. In sure of her."</p>
        <p>"Do you mean—?" began his fathet slowly, leaving the question unfinished. "I mean what you would have meant it you had been in my position, sir. That so long as Helen it willing to wait for me. I will make no other woman my wife. And I'm sure of her... _ . . .</p>
        <p>His eyes lighted as he repeated the phras&lt; and his strong face was instinct with confi- dence in the woman he loved and with re. solve to win her.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER 11. Silt ortmony's FIRST ORDEAL.</p>
        <p>"Let the boy have his way, Gregory," said Lady Trevelloek when Sir Gregory had told her everything. Her pleading prevailed, and it was ar ranged that Bulmer should carry up a letter from his mother asking Helen eaxton to stay at the Chase for the coming week-end. "Lotta must also be told," she declared. "Let there be no more concealment. It makes me miserable, and it is so unlike you in everything." He promised, but shrank from the task: and dalikd and procrastinated weakly until the chance of making a voluntary statement to her was lost.</p>
        <p>Two days later, on her return from a ride, Lotta went to him and in her bluntest and, most aggressive manner announced that Mr. Grainger had asked her to marry him. "01 course I know how glad you all will be to gel rid of me, so I have accepted." "Very well, Lotta, but I hope you have a better reason than your words imply. How- ever, I wish you all happiness." "In a sort of wish-you-may-get-it tone. But I want something more than good wishes, father. I want an explanation. 'Who will have the Chase at your death?" "Why do you ask that "Why does one generally ask a question I want to know, of course." _ _</p>
        <p>He passed his hand across his brow in thought. "I was going to speak to you about it, but your conduct makes it so difficult," he said. "In the absence of any other arrange- ment the Chase goes to the eldest child." "So I was told. I am the eldest child, and of course it will be mine. And of course you will tell everybody, and I shall take my proper place as the future mistress of the Chase," and without waiting for his reply she went out. •• '</p>
        <p>Triumphing in her new position, she changed her dress quickly and went to gloat over Lady Trevellock. Mrs. Caulfield was present, and had just been told the news; but her presence did not restrain Lotta, who put the blame for the long concealment on her stepmother. "Of course, I know why you never said , anything," she sneered. 'You wished your paragon of a son to have all the honour, while I—" She broke off as she saw Sir Gregory had entered and had overheard this. His face was pale with anger; but after a pause she finished the sentence in a hard, defiant tone. "While I was just your drudge." "How dare you, Lotta!" cried Sir Gregory hotly. "I-will not allow you to speak in that way to your stepmother. Since the time when you were less than a year old she has been a mother to you, and you owe her more than you can ever repay. And instead of! trying to repay her, you appear to take a ' pleasure in such disgraceful conduct as this." "She has kept me out of the knowledge of my birthright, if that is being a mother," replied Lotta doggedly. "That is untrue. She did not know this until Bulmer'a birthday, and then urged me to tell you at once. Don't dare to sneer at me, girl," he said angrily. "And remember this: you arc not yet ir istress of the Chase, and while I am master :on shall do as I de- sire, or leave the house. Now go out of the room, and the next instance of impertinence to either of us will lead to your being sent</p>
        <p>away." She had never seen him so angry before, and with a shrug of the shoulders went out of the room. His outburst of temper secured peace for some days; and at the end of the week Bul- mer returned, bringing Helen Paxton with him. Lady Trevellock, to please him, was down. stairs to welcome her, and kissed her affec- tionately. "You are welcome, my dear, for my boy's sake now; but I am sure you will soon be so for your own," she said very sweet- ly. "Kiss me, child; you have a very sweet f see." "I am very glad you have come down, Mist Paxton" said Sir Gregory, taking the cue from his wife and shaking hands with her cordially. "I hope we shall be the best of friends." "I —I think I am very nervous, but I thank you both so much," replied Helen simply, her cheeks high coloured and her eyes shin- ing. "Thank you, mother," said Bulmer, bend- ing down to kiss Lady Trevellock. "And you too, father," he added, turning to shake hands with him. "And now that the formal reception is over," said Lady Trevellock, smiling, "run away, you two, and leave Helen to talk to me a while. Will you stay to cheer up an old invalid, my dear?" She held out her hand and Helen pressed it impulsively, the tear's standing in her eyes. She had indeed a sweet face, as Lady Tre- vellock had said, and a tall figure with a free, graceful carriage, instinct with self reliance, and saggestive of both strength and indepen- dence. Large, heavily fringed, dark blue eyes, eloquent of womanly sympathy, com- pelled attention instantly, while the rest of the features, regular, comely, and admirably moulded, confirmed the impreftion of char- acter and strong individuality. Sir Gregory had scrutinised her very earnestly, and was favourably impressed by her. "She is a very beautiful girl, Bulmer," he said, when they were alone. "Yes, sir. Of course I think So," was the proud reply. She reminds me of someone; I can't think who it is. I cannot have seen her before, GM I?" "It is scarcely likely. She has not bees long in, this country, and has never been near here before." _ _ .</p>
        <p>"I can't understand it. It' not a face one would be quick to toilet, either. Where does_ she come __from?".</p>
        <p>"From the States." "She's an Americhn then? She has no accent," "She's from Boston, father, where they pride themselves on their English." "Then, of course, I can't hate seen her I,e- -be noted vet it is</p>
        <p>strange. nave you told her yet of the change in your prospects?" "No, sir. I thought it would be fairer that you should tell her and see for yourself the effect." "That's like you, my boy; but—it will be a somewhat embarrassing thing," he said weakly, not at all relishing the task "Tell me, how do you stand exactly with her? Have you asked her to marry your "No, sir. I would not until she had been down hem But I think she quite under- stands things. In fact, she said that she had something to tell you about herself. She is candour itself; but I have never asked her anything." "Like a young man in love, of course," said Sir Gregory, with a faint smile. "Cer- tainly the first impression is all one could de- sire; but the future, Bulmer, the. future,"</p>
        <p>"I am not afraid to face it, sir; and I don't think Helen will be either." "Ah, well, we'll see. I think she has cap- tured your mother already." Gregory was right about Lady Travel• lock, as he found the moment they were alone together. She is a most charming girl, Gregory. One of the sweetest and dearest I have ever met. She will make it hard for us to resist the boy," was her verdict,</p>
        <p>"Wei 'always the poor girls that are charm- ing," replied Sir Gregory a little ruefuny. "It's a precious awkward 'business, and Bul- mer has left me to tell her that he has no- thing to live on." Later in the afternoon Sir Gregory took Helen into his study -under the pretence of showing her some of his books, and then faced the task of telling her what had to be told. Disliking it, he did it awkwardly and bluntly. "Now we must have a chat. Bulmer has left me to tell you something which you ought to know at once, Miss Paxton. That a great change has taken place In his prospects, and that, in fact, he has no fortune at all."</p>
        <p>Her reply surprised him. "I never thought that he had ,any, Sir Gregory," she said with a smile. "Until three days ago, I did not even know that he wan the son of—that he was your son. We have never spoken of such matters in regard to either of us."</p>
        <p>"God bless my soul!" exclaimed Sir Greg- ory. "Well, I'll tell you now"; and in a hesitating, nervous way he described the posi- tion, and that a marriage without money meant genteel beggary for his son. But youth and love laughed at the idea. "I do not care for money," she said; "but if Bulmer is afraid of the future I should not wish—l mean, it is for him, and of course for you all, to decide. You see, I have never had much money, and should not know what to do</p>
        <p>with it." Sir Gregory recognised with a sigh that be could make little headway against this. "He says you have something to tell me," he said, after • pause. " Yea. When Lady Trevelloek asked me to come down here, of course, I understood, although Bulmer has never asked me actually to be his wife. And there is some- thing that should be told before then; and can best perhaps be told to you. You see I have no one in the world to speak for me or even to advise me."</p>
        <p>The touch of pathos in this appealed to Sir Gregory. "Tell me In your own way," he said. "It is about my birth. I have always been known by the name of Patton; but that is not my own name. My father de. serted my mother when I was • tiny mite, • month or two old. They were at the time in Montana State. He was any. thing but a good man; and my mother took me to her own people in Massachusetts. She died very soon afterwards, and a sister of hers adopted me, and I took her name. Less than a year ago she died, having lost all her money, and I was sent over here to another sister. But she could not afford to do any. thing for me; and so I set to work to, earn mi own ...living. That is all my story. '</p>
        <p>You did pa tell me your owl; nam;V:</p>
        <p>"My father's name Itad Gregory Powell, an Englishman, I believe. I have his photo- graph here, and my mother's marriage certi- ficate."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>SIR GREGORY'S SILENCE</head>
        <p>SIR GREGORY'S SILENCE</p>
        <p>ARTHUR W. MARCHMONT, An ho r of "The Man Who \Vas Dead," 'By Right</p>
        <p>of Sword," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER I. TEI COMING ON AON.</p>
        <p>It was • great day at Travellock Chase when Sir Gregory Trevellock's son, Bulmer, cgdpe of age. Half the county had as- ft-Mailed to join in the rejoicings, and the verdict of all was unanimous that he wee a, sphndid young fellow, and a worthy son of the man whom everybody delighted to honour. Sir Gaspard Revnhier voiced the general opinion as he took leave of Sir Gregory. "We hope to have you with us for many a year yet, Trevellock, for there is no man whose MSS SO the county would be snore severely felt, and none who etands in higher esteem with us all; but we can all nee that there is another Sir Gregory coming on in your boy. God bless him, I say. You're a lucky man." "1 m very proud of him, Reynhier ; he has never given me an hour's uneasiness and never crossed a single wish of mine," Was the reply. "Only another way of saying that he is no honourable a fellow as his father; and we all hope to sof much more of him in the future. Get ham to marry and settle down amongst ue." "I hope there will be some news of that soon"; and Sir Gregory glanced across at a very pretty woman who wits talking to his son. _ _</p>
        <p>Sir Gaspard chuckled as he followed the look. "The pretty Mrs. Caulfield, eh? Well, I have thought as much. They tell me she's very wealthy, too. Well, they'll have my good wishes, I can assume you;" and with that the two shook hands cordially, and Sir Gregory went over to his wife's couch and told her what his old friend had said. . .</p>
        <p>Lady Trevellock was an invalid, and had left her room for the first time for weeks in order to witness her son's triumph. "I hope it is all right about Bulmer and Beth"—this was a pet abbreviation of Mrs. Caulfield's Christian name, Elizabeth—"but I have been watching them and—" Ehe paused and shook her head dubiously. "Be dces not look at her quite as a lover should, Gregory." "It will be all right. It must. You will see." _</p>
        <p>"He has said nothing to anyone to-day," she murmured; "and Lotta has made me very bncomfortable about it." This was her stepdaughter. Sir Gregory made a. gesture of annoyance and frowned as he stood thinking. Lotta's conduct was a jarring discord in the har- mony of life at the Chase. Before he had succeeded to the title and estates and had taken the family name in place of his own— Powell—he had been a penniless adventurer in the West of America, and had married Lotta's mother under circumstances he could never think of without a shudder of horror— the one really black hour of his whole life. And from the moment she had come into the world the child had been a source of trouble. The mother died in giving her birth, and soon afterwards he had married the gentle, patient, loving woman —hie present wife and the mother of his son—who had exhausted every means in her power lin a vain attempt to win Lotta's heart and 'restrain her way- ward nature. Always rebellious, Lotta's dislike of her stepmother had strengthened with her growth and spread to her father no that, when be had unexpectedly succeeded to the title and estates, he had eent her away to a school on the Continent. Then, some few years later, came a most disconcerting discovery. Sir Gregory learnt from Mr. Lyons, the family solicitor, that while the title went to the eldest son, a most unusual provision of Om old settlements gave the estates to the eldest child, whether male or female. Lotta was thus the heiress. She was brought home at once; but Sir Gregory, fearing that when she knew she would one day be the owner of the property, her conduct to his wife, who was very ill at the time and not expected to recover, would be worse than ever, resolved to keep the fact a close secret between himself and Mr. Lyons until after his wife's death. Lady Trevellock had rallied, however; and taus Lotta had not been told. Sir Gregory's intention was that Bulmer should make a wealthy marriage; and he had put off the disclosure until then. The chance had come when wealthy and pretty little Mrs. Caulfield had fallen in love with Bulmer. Lady Trevellock's reference to Lotta had started these thoughts and brought the deep frown to his usually placid face. "What did Lotta say about it, dear?" he asked after a pause. "It is not what she said so much as what</p>
        <p>she implied; but perhaps I am only a</p>
        <p>fldgetty and 'querulou; oldrwoman." Then her eyes lightened as Bulmer came across to her. Tall and broad-shouldered, with the carriage of an athlete, his hand- some features bore a striking likeness to his mother's, and his steady blue eyes eiere elo- quent of courage and frankness. A son to be proud of, indeed. He bent down and kissed her as he said very gently: "You look tired, dearest."</p>
        <p>"I would not have missed it for the world, Bulmer," she replied, smiling up at him. "There's nothing to miss now but the hand- shaking. Let me carry you up to your room." His voice was as pleasant as his smile. "I would rather stay a while. I'm too happy at your success to take any harm." "There are some more folks wanting to shake hands with the hero of the hour," said Lotta, who had come up unobserved. Her voice was high-pitched and she spoke in an acrid tone, and with more than a suggestion of a sneer. "You'd better go and be wor- shipped spin, Buimer.'' "Lofts!' said Sir Gregory sharply. She fumed to him and threw up her head as if resenting the protest. She was utterly unlike the others. Slight of figure and very -thin, the plainness of her features was accen- tuated by a sallow complexion, and her eyes, small and dark, with black straight brows, suggested an ill-temper which the thin lips with their downward tendency at the corners</p>
        <p>confirmed. "You would not have me consider Balmer anything except a hero to-day, father, surely? If his head in not turned, it won't be his fault," she said waspishly. "Your manner of putting it was unfortun- ate, Lona," replied her father. "Thank you. You are always referring to any manner. Of course, we can't all be per- fect, like Balmer," and she pointed the re- tort with • snappish little laugh. "There is Mr. Lyons looking as if he wished to speak to you, Gregory," inter- posed Lady Trevelloci, laying her hand gently on his. "'too need not trouble to interfere between my father and me. I don't thank you for it; although I suppose you think the pose of a peacemaker suits you," said Lotto very un- pleasantly as Sir Gregory turned away. "You should not speak to my mother like that, Lotto," put In Balmer, quietly but steadily, seeing Lady Trevellock wince. "Deis.. me! Shouldn't I? But of course when the paragon in this devoted family speaks, the pariah should hold her tongue. But I shall not. I shalt say what I please, and how I please," and with this change from •..sneer to anger, she turned away. "I am so sorry, mother." "It is best not to take any notice, Bulmer. I hay° learnt that. But there are heaps of people waiting to say good-bye. Go to them, dear; and come to me afterwards."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
