      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <p>We are the ONLY FIRM IN WARRINGTON which</p>
        <p>sells New Zealand Mutton and Lamb exclusively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>We do not sell Frozen Beet</head>
        <p>We do not sell Frozen Beet</p>
        <p>mere umore mm—ln nis power to neaten,. The temptation to destroy the fateful evi- dence thus put in his power appealed to Bit. Gregory too fiercely to be denied. He would destroy the proofs. He must. He could de- cide what to do for Helen afterwards. But welf-preservation was the first instinct, sod be did not stay to let the impubie pass. With quivering fingers he picked up the paper and photograph and carried them to the fire, and was thrusting them into the tames when again he paused. His courage failed him. He shrank from the actual crime. He could not at once fall so low as that.</p>
        <p>Hovering over the fire, he paused to think, and then another idea occurred to him. Hastily searching in his drawers, he found some paper like that of the certificate and a photograph. These he thrust into the flames, so intent upon his work that he burnt his hand and felt nothing of the pain as he held them until they were no far consumed that only • fragment of each remained—just enough to suggest that they were Ines scorched remnants of the real papers. Then ha emit back to hie table and locked the paper an photograph away in a secret recess of his desk, and sat trembling and shaking as he thought of what to tell Helen when she returned. After a while he lit a cigar. That would afford a plausible pretext. A drink of brandy restored some measure of confidenee, and pinging his bell he sent for her. _ _</p>
        <p>_ She came at once, smiling and full of sym• pathy, and with an agitation which was real enough, but the cause of which he misun- derstood. He told her that hehad an "acci- dent." Lighting the cigar, he had felt Bud- denly faint, and the match bad dropped on her papers and burnt them. The lie fell haltingly from his lips, and shrank from meeting her eyes, full of sym- pathy though they were, mingled with con- cern for her loss. He winced and cowered and trembled under the stinging lash of his own self-contempt. And when he implored her forgiveness, his head fell forward on his arms on the table.</p>
        <p>(To be Continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <p>one was feeling in her pocket for the papers, and thus did not ace the start Sir Gregory gave, the sudden whiteness of his face, nor the convulsive grip of his hands on the arms of the chair.</p>
        <p>"They are here, Sir Gregory"; and she held them out to him. "But you are sir," she cried in alarm, noticing the change in him, and rose to call for help. He stopped her with a gesture, put his hand to his heart, and forced a smile. "It is—nothing. I have a little trouble—here, ;sometimes." His fingers shook so as he took the paper and the photograph that he could scarcely hold them ; and he looked at them at first furtively and in fear, and then intently, holding his breath and pressing his lips together tightly. Then he let them fall on the table and clasped his hands to his face.</p>
        <p>--"--Let me call for help," she said anxiously. "Your face is as grey as death, sir." "No —no—please. My wife knows— nothing of this, nor Bulmer. Please say nothing. If you will leave me alone for a short time. I shall be all right; but say nothing, nothing of this at all, please." With an expression of intense compassion in her eyes she went slowly out of the room, and as soon as the door closed behind her he crept to it, locked it softly, crawled back to his chair, and taking up the papers stared at them as a man might stare at some hor- rible phantom. To him they were in truth as a plisntom rising from the grave of the past to denounce him for a deed of the long ago, menacing him with dishonour, disgrace, and the loss of all he possessed in the world. "Gregory Powell." "Helen Paxton." Tbe names stood out in letters of fire and blood before his eyes. At the sight of them, the dcad past rose up to denounce him; and one scene was flashed upon his memory with the vivid colourings of terror and remorse.</p>
        <p>The small tare room of a squat wood. built shanty in the Far West of America; the still form of a dead man lying prone with a bullet hole showing red and ghastly in the white forehead; the icy cramp of horror at the deed; the sickening terror at the immi- nence of capture red-handed, but yet inno- cent; a woman, Lotta's mother, at his side, distraught and frenzied with passion, vow- ing, unless he consented to make her his wife, to denounce him for the deed which she herself had committed, and to drive the accusation home by the aid of the wild letters in which he had threatened the dead man's life.</p>
        <p>Once again he felt himself at bay; hunted and desperate, torn by a conflict in which honour bade him refuse, while love of life urged his consent; during the terror-filled moments of torturing hesitation as he waited for the coming of those who would haul him away to death olden he agreed to her terms.</p>
        <p>The memory convulsed °him now with the old agony, until he groaned and shuddered, as he recalled the consent which was wrung from him in his panic and weakness; the bondage of shame involved; the course of deception along which he had been driven with relentless force from that hour; and the bitter, bitter days and nights of im- potent regret. Then the gradual growth of the sense of safety; the building up of a good name and honourable repute; the acquiaitiov of the Trevellock inheritance; his pride in it, and the esteem and respect which he had won from all. And now this—the climax!</p>
        <p>Tbe child of Gregory Powell and Helen Paz. ton! He had had no thought that there had ever been a child. Against her he had sinned in MI innocence; and in ignorant* had pos sessed himself of her birthright. The Chase and all that went with it was hers; and she could turn him into the street a beggar. Worse than a beggar, indeed, for she could claim • return of every shilling he had ever hal since the time he had first come there.</p>
        <p>And worst of all, her right could only be acknowledged by a confession of that terrible prat. A frenzy of rage against fate seised him, and he glared at the evidence of her claim as if it were a poisonous dagger whose stab would destroy not only his life but his honour. Then came temptation. No one knew this save himself. Only his own lips could betray the secret, and the sole evidence of it lay</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>DisTßicT tiEws</head>
        <p>DisTßicT tiEws</p>
        <p>Fearnbead.</p>
        <p>RILEY INDIPIINDINT MIITFIODIST Cstracts.—The harvest Festival services werepreched in the above piaci, of worship on Sunday. Mr. Dunkley of Warrington preached morning and evening to very large coagrega• Lion. A large and varied collection of fruit and vegetables was arranged in the chapel. The pulpit was nicely decorated by Mrs H Bradburn, Miss Ashton, Mrs J Monk., Mr J Houghton, Mrs J Higham, Miss Smith, Miss 11 Bate, Messrs John Highsm, Cook and Houghton being responsible for the other decorations. On Monday night a ssle of the fruit &amp;c., was held in the schoolroom which was well attended, the total receipts sale and collections, amounting to £3 Iss</p>
        <p>Moore. WEILICY Geti.o.—On Wednesday evening a good number assembled in the schoolroom to hear Mr. Bishop, of Stockton Heath, lecture on " Rambles with a camera." The lecture was illustrated with 200 original lantern slides, all perfect examples of the photographer's art and depicting rambles in many directione. They included holiday rambles, scenes in the slums of Warrington, thwer studies, cloud and storm pictures. seascapes, and a very fine series of Old Morton Hall, Congleton, Cheshire. Mr. Jackson presided, and at the close of the lecture Miss Dutton expressed the very sincere thanks of the Guild to Mr. Bishop for the pleasure he had given by showing such a magnificent and varied series of elides. This was seconded b 1 Mr. Warburton. The lantern was ably manipulated by Mr. F. Wilkinson. Hatton.</p>
        <p>TIIE SOCIAL COMMITTEE.—The annual committee meeting was held in the Parish Room, Hatton, on Toes- day evening, when the folio% ing members were present i—Rev T T Lee Jones (presidentl in the chair, Messrs C Blackshaw, G Hayward, W Fowles, IV W Leach, Mr W Dooley, secretary. All the old com- mittee were re-elected with one exception, Rev W S Adams being elected in the place of Mr W Mullen. The balance in hand was very satisfactory considering that a lot of the old members keep moving. This year about it new members have joined. The vicar has promised another billiard handicap and to give a little stimulus to the games the prizes will be lls., 103., Is., to be played off for Christmas. The president thanked the committee for the way in which they carried out their work and hoped they would meet together on many occasions. It was proposed to have a concert during the coming season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Great Sankey.</head>
        <p>Great Sankey.</p>
        <p>DEATH OY AN OLD RESIDENT.EarIy 012 Monday morning the oldest female inhabitant passed away in the person of Mrs. Grace Hampeon, of Vine Cottage, Great Sankey, she having lived to the ripe age of 99 years. She was a native of Sankey end lived in the Parish all her life. Years ago she was a constant attendant at St. Helens market in connection with her business as a market gardener. Unfortunately, she has been suffering more or less for some two years.</p>
        <p>LOCAL FARMER'S DF.ATH.—NIOCh regret was molest throughout the district when it became known on Sun- day that Mr Joseph Hlshaw had passed away that morning. He bad carried on business as a farmer at Ivy House Farm, Cow-lane, Sankey. Mr. Kilshaw having died in the house in which he wan born and had lived all his life. The Farm has been in the same family for over a century. The deceased gentleman who was most highly respected WHS in his 53rd year He was always willing to do his best for the interests of the parish generally. He had been a metal ir of the Great Sankey School Board. In politics he was a Con- servative. As • Churchman he had always given his support to St. Mary's Church, teing connected with it from a boy. Much sympathy is felt for the widow and children in their bereavement. The funeral took place at Great Sankey Church on Wednesday afternoon, the Rev. J. R. Jones conducting the burial service. Besides the family there was s large number of farmers and businessmen from the surrounding districts in attend- ance. A 1 trge number of wreaths were sant from relatives and friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Penketh.</head>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>THE L SCOUR PARTW.—A meeting was held on Thursday evening last week to arrange for the winter session. Mr. G. Faulkner presided and arrangements were mode to hold meetings on alternate Thursda3s at 7.41 p.m., commencing October 26th. The building fund w,a reported to be in a satisfactory state. Reference were made to the death of Mr. Bennet t,a late member, and the removal of Mr. and Mrs. Clay. --------</p>
        <p>PARISH COTINCIr4 A special meeting of the a.bove was held on Monday evening in the Council Schools, Stocks-lane. Those in attendance were t—Dr. Murray (chairman), Messrs. William Davies, James Ditobfield, Ftichard Routledge, James Woodward and E. Foster (clerk.)—Dr. Murray informed the meeting that they had been called together to consider the reply they bad received from the agent for Captain Michael Hughes (Mr. H. B. Bates) in regard to the question of right of way leading from tie Ferry Inn to the Ferry Lrcks.—The Clerk said that as instructed by the Council he bad written to the agent protesting against the attempt to close up the footpath, and bad received I the following reply dated Oatober 11th, 1911 I would point out to you that this is a private road I for the use of our tenant at the Ferry Inn. My instruc- tions from Captain Hughes ore that the road must be closed. We are prepared to maintain our right. Faith- fully yours, signed H. B. Bates."—Mr Routledge pro- posed and Mr Davies seconded, that the following resolution be forwarded to Captain Michael Hughes, agent, re the footpath leading from the Ferry Inn to the locks. Heat Sir,—My Council cesteulty censidered your reply of the 11th inst. to their protest to the closing of this footpath I urn instructed to point out to you that the I.ublic of Penketh have, without inter- ference had the use of this footpath for at least 70 years. I Lave also point out to you that this spot is a favourite place for the inhabitants to take their children on fine rummer days. Only Cu Sunday October Bth there was quite a crowd of people lining the river from the ferry to the Iccks for the purpose of watching the high tide and the shipping My Council do not consider the public have any right to the land. But they are at a loss to understand why after so many years of public use a claim should be made to close the footpath. Hoping that when you Isy this letter before Captain Hughes a favourable view of this question will.be taken and that when your fencing is done it will be arranged in such away that the nubile of Penketh may continue to enjoy the use of this old footpath.—Youra faithfully (Signed) E. FOSTER (Clerk to the Penketh Parish Council.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. JOB GROUNDS, OF HILL CLIFFS.— We regret to announce the death of Mr. Joe Grounds of Hill Cliffe-road who passed away on Monday last. Mr. Grounds was born at Orford 54 years ago and spent his early years in the neighbourhood of Hill Cliff.. After a few years there he returned to Lower Walton sad laboured there as quarryman at the ILII Cliffs quarry. For twenty-six years he has been associated with the Baptist Church of Hill Cliffe and has d the church in the capacity of deacon for a apace of twelve years. As a teachers of the Bible Class he gained an influence with the young men and his advice and example are gratefully remembered by many. He served also in the various offices of the Sunday school. Though follow- ing other vocations he always found his bobby in gardening, and dining the closing years of his life followed it as a business. The funeral took place on Thursday afternoon at Hill Cliffe burial ground amid many tokens of regard and affection. The members of the Church walked in procession from the home of Mr Grounds to the Chapel, where a service WM conducted by Rev. J. Curtiss. During the service and at the grave side Mr Curtiss spoke of the warm regard which Mr Grounds hurl inspired in the Church and in the hearts of all who knew him. Mr Grounds leaves a widow, two sots and two daughters. ItILL CLIFFE AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BALED OF Hon.—The opening entertainment of the season pgdrAFtt. a niV,73 Q! this n9ye heici_worked by tile lull tante ilaptist and Stui....t.on Heath Wesley' Churches took place on Monday evening in the Wesleyan Schoolroom. The Rev George Curtis, pastor of Hill Cliff. presided, being introduced t 3 the meet- ing by Rev , C Wesleyan mioister of Stootton Heath Church. Toe spmler was itev J S Chedbara, of lAtohford. A goA programme WAS given Including iMigs by Nagle Grounds Dully (-Jain. ,EIsIP Whittled, Ethel Houghton, Itvelyn Pollltt and Mtn May Isherwood; recitations by Genie CL He, Gladys Pemberton, Mies Maud Kirkland and Miss Amy Spencer (Temperance Olean), Master Fteggie Moss contributed a pianoforte solo In pleasing style. The sctioa sang " Caller Herrin " was gi ran by the Prize Action Sang Party and Wan greatly appreciated. The event of the evening was the appearance of the Stockton Heath Banjo, Mandoline, and Guitar Band under the conductorship of Mr. Caleb Whitley, who contributed several items to the great delight of the audience. The humorous part of the programme was supplied by Mr Gordon Starkey. The accompanists were the Misses Turner and Soden, and Messrs T Pearse and A Fairclough. A vote of thanks to all who had contributed to the evening's enjoyment was carried on the proposition of Mr G Whitfield, secretary. The officers for the suislan are as follows : President, the Rev 0 Curtin ; vice-presidents, Rev It C Hutchinson, Messrs J Moss, W H Bradford and II Brereton ; secretary, Mr 0 Whitfield ; assistant secre- tary, Miss Shod Kirkland ; treasurer, Ur J Moss ; superintendent., Messrs J Mose,W Twins, AV Drewitt, and A Jones ; representatives to the Warrington Band of Hope Union, Messrs G Whitfield, J Moss and II Brereton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Winwict</head>
        <p>Winwict</p>
        <p>MOTOR Accarrimcr.—A motor accident—luckily no one was hurt—occurred on Thursday night, about half-way between Winwick Church and Newton Station. A man named Mark Wood and his wife were returning from Newton to Warrington in a low cart when a motor car belonging to Mr. Unsworth, of Claiborne, ran into them. Both wheels were torn off the cart, both shafts smashed, and the occupants thrown into the hedge. The driver of the ear had a very narrow escape frcm being injured, the glass wind screen being smashed to atoms, and the front axle broken. WHIST DR successful whist drive and dance promoted by the Winwick branch of the Primrose League and Tariff Reform League, was held in the schoolroom on Friday night. There was a good attend- ance, about 72 people sitting down to whist-. Viacount Wolmer arrived about half-time and played the second half, and afterwards presented the prizes. The prize- winners were :—Ladies : I, Miss L. Brown ; 2, Miss C. Brown. Gents 1, Mr A Clarke ; 2, Mr. W. Sweetlove. The ladies' first prize, a handbag, was given by Mr. Hodgson, the gent's first by Mr. J. W. Knowles, and the two second prizes were given by the committee. Dancing was indulged in until about 2 a.m. Mr. C. F. Dreenway had charge of the whist, and Mr. W. Atkir proved an efficient M.C. for the danca. Mr C. Helsby I undertook the duties of pianist. The catering was in the very able hands of Mrs. Sweetlove.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>WRSLEY GUILD.-0o Monday evening Mr. Birchen 'lectured to a large audience his subject being" Wire- lees Telegraphy." As a practical operator, the lecturer is evidently quite master of his subject, and the interest- ing manner in which it was presented WPM greatly appreciated by those present. WEiLEYAS MOTIIMS' MEETING.-011 Tuesday last the Mother's Meeting in connection with the Wesleyan church, which had lapsed for a considerable time was recommenced. There was a good attendsoce, and the refreshments were given by Mra. John Houghton.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN SUND.IY SCHOOL.—With the object of reducing the debt upon the new premises a series of entertainments hes been arranged for the winter months. The first of these, held on Thursday evening, took the form of a concert provided by the members of the Bold-street Wesleyan choir and proved a great success. The choir sang the part songs" Now is my chloris fresh as May,"" The Snow." " Moonlight," end "The long day closes." They also gave the anthem " Sun of my soul." Except in the anthem, where the I tenor Fan rather too pronounced, the balance was very; good, and throughout the tone and attack were excel.! lent and the pitch well maintained. The soloists were local favourites whose reputations are well established. Miss Davey was well received in the two songs she contributed, for one of which the audience demanded an encore. Miss Emily Taylor received swell merited encore for her song" Let me dream again" and she gave a capital rendering of "When the heart is young." Mr 9 Hassell seemed to be in good form and gang a couple of songs for the second of which he was accorded an encore. Mr \V Warburton contributed " Glorious Devon" and as an encore "Down the Vale." Mr E H Pearce, A.L.G.M., gave a lice rendering of " The Bugler " and " Revenge." In response to vociferous demands for encores he sang "01! to Philadelphia" and" ike Philosopher." Mrs A Booth MS heard to 1 advantage in the somewhat intricate 'Jewel Song" (Gounod's Faust) and as an encore delighted the audi- ence with the ever welcome" Annie Laurie." She also conducted the"Ladiee' Choir" with noticeable ability in Elgar's part song "The Snow "which was much appreci- ated. Nothing but the highest praise can he given to Miss Carrie Hastings for the songs she gave. For each of these—" Pleading " (Eiger) and " Goodbye" (Tosh)— she received enthusiastic applause and acceeded to the request for an encore in each case. Seldom do Padgatonians hear a soloist possessing such sweetness , and purity of tone and flrmnem, yet flexibility of voice to say nothing of the naturalness and charm of manner displayed by Miss Hastings, Mr. F. Hickman accom- panied the soloists with his usual ability. After the singing of the National Anthem, bind while the choir partook of refreehmente, the Rev. E. Morgan and Dr. Bennett voiced the thanks of the Padgate friends and Mr. T. Hickman suitably responded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>SIR WILLIAM CROSSLRY'S FUNERAL.—The Lvmm Liberal Association were represented by Messrs. J. H. Mather(chairman) and R Harry Gotobed (ex-chairman).</p>
        <p>LYNItt WESLEYAN CHURCH.—At a recent representa- tire meeting of the congregation it was unanimously Agreed not to hold any bazaar or sale of work in order to liquidate the small outstanding debt on current work. Officers were appointed to arrange for all friends to be asked to contribute to a subscription list either by regular instalment or in one sum.</p>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL CHRISTIAN SERVICE B \NO.—A good meeting was held on Monday evening last in the school vestry. The address was given by Mr William, Mort "Os BOMB of the miracles that happen every day.' It was very interesting and helpful to all. A solo was rendered by Mr G Willan entitled "Only remembered" In a most ecreditsble manner. Miss Fanny Sutton presided. DEATH Or MR. THOMAS DIXON.—It is with deep regret that we have to announce the unexpected death 'of Mr. Thomas Dixon of Foxley Cottage, Statham. Mr. Dixon was a well-known and much respected man and had a cheery word for everyone in all walks of life. He was going about until Tuesday but was confined to the house the following day. After tea his son went upstairs and found Mr. Dixon dead in bed. He had suffered from bronchitis and heart disease.</p>
        <p>MATH OF AN OLD RESIDICNT.—We regret to announce the death of Mrs. Elizabeth Gregson widow of the , late Mr James Gregson, which occurred at her reai- donee " Leckonby " Lymin on Monday last, after an I illness of some weeks duration. The funeral, which, in keeping with the wishes of the family was carried out as quietly as possible and was only attended by the near relatives of the deceased, took place on Thursday at St. Mary's Church, the Rev G B Thurston M.A , rector, officiating. The mourners present were Messrs Harold Speiglit (son-in-law), C J Birch (grandson), T Glegson, Hamble- ton, J Gergson (Hyde), J Mee (Denton), and L Kenyon (Denton), nephews. The number of beautiful floral tributes amply testified to the respect and esteem in which the deceased lady was held. The funeral arrange- ments were in the hands of Miners Bowen and Co., Lymm. SAD ACCIDILNT TO A LT:WM 130 Y.—On Wednesday evening, Arthur Goffin of Grove-terrace, Booth's Hill; Lymm, met with a sericite accident. It transpires that be was playing near a building that is rising erected in Newfield. One of the labourers, Thomas Drinkwater (who is only a youth), SAW him and told him to go away. Goffin refused and began calling names, Drinkwater threw half a brick at Galin and hit him in the eye, with the result that he had to be taken to the Warrington Infirmary to undergo an operation, fie has lost the sight of one eye and it is feared that he might lose the other, but on inquiry he seems to be progress- ing favourably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ISMNI PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>ISMNI PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Lymm Petty Sessions were held on Wednesday morning. The magistrates were Mews A ‘Vatkin (in the chair), W P Ryland:, A W Callender, Major E G Groves and Admiral Jones-Parry. RIO:SAL OF POOR RATF..--A PRFCEDENT.—Tbe assistant overseer of High Legh asked for the exousal of the payment of the poor rate of Is Id on land and 21 2d on buildings, doe from Miss Hannah Show, of High Legb. The Clerk asked if she could not go into the Workhouse and the overseer replied that she could just earn a living by going out washing. She well 69 yeers of age. Itt another year she would be entitled to an old age pension. In reply to the Chairman as t 3 whether they had the power to excuse the payment of the poor rate the Clerk said they had on the Eround of poverty. Mr. Watkin (the Chairman): Ido not think we have had a case before. The Clerk : We often have them at Altrincham. After consultation with his brother mogistrates the Chairman said the application would be granted, although they did not lilts doing it. They would certainly watch on principle,any case which might come up in the future.</p>
        <p>WASHING V. SCHOOL—WIIIiam Stubbs, Chapel-lane, I waetingtoa, was summoned for neglecting to seed his child, Harry Stubbs, aged 13 years, to ecnoci arrt• ington on the 20th October. Mr. C. E. Hill (the school attendance officer) said the child only attended school 37 times out of 68 in seven weeks. Defendant MS represented by his wife who said that her husband was a labourer. Sly) had kept the lad at home while she went out washir,g. There w•e a lerge family. Two were working and there were six others who were at school. The Chairman said that she must send the lad to school. The State said so and the State paid for They disliked to have ceses like that and would only Inflict a penalty of 2t. 64, A CLA.ial DV 'MI6 I:2lL'AdtDlANS.—William B!ooks, Rookery, Lyme, appeared in answer to a summons.— Mr. LIT. Mercer. claimed on behalf of the Guardians of the Rucklow Union EL for medical fees on loan for the treatment of a fractured arm of Lilian Brooks, his child, at Lymm.—Defendant admitted the amount to be due.—Mr. Mercer said that defendant promised to pay Is. 6d. a week when he gave him the note for the doctor. The defendant had paid nothing though the child was treated several months ago, lie seemed to set the Union at deflance.—To reply to the Clerk the defendant said ho got between 265. and 30r. a week. He had three weeks play at the time of the Railway strike. —The magistrate ordered the defendant to pan Is. 6d. weakly. He would also have to pay the costs Bs. 64. ' MEN ASLEEP AT THE Bali K men were charged with sleeping at the brick kilns, Statham on t the 17th inst. and without any visible means of sub- ; sistence. Three of the men were discharged and two others were sent to prison for 14 days and 7 days respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>INTERIOR PADGATE</head>
        <p>INTERIOR PADGATE</p>
        <p>New Church Lecture. " No Such Thing as Death."</p>
        <p>Taking as his subject " Death, Resurrection and Judgment " the Rev W H CLAX roN of Kearsley at the Masonic Hall, Bold-street on Wednesday evening, pro- ceeded with his explanation of the doctrines of the New Church. . .</p>
        <p>The rev, gentleman said that amid all the uncer- tainties of human existence one thing WM certain that sooner or later we had to leave this earth and do what the world called die. We had to face the fact that we had to go out of this existence into an unknown one and for a man to be content to let his mind rest without trying to know something of that great unknown, would be nothing abort of cowardly. While he urged that, however, he was bound to tell them what seemed paradox, that there was no such thing as death. Death was an appearance and not a reality, it was simply the Lord calling us to sleep. It smash-11ply the putting off of the tabernacle of this life—the flesh and blood— which cannot enter the Kingdom of God, the passing of the soul that had lived for a certain period on the earth's plain. Tnere won nothing to be afraid of in the new death, and he was glad to say that fear was departing, and men were beginning to realise that the thing to be frightened of was not death, but evil. Death was simply a sleep, and when the form was laid in the grave it was simply the giving hack of the mortal remains to the earth from which it sprang. The resurrection was the rising of the sold out of the body into that new sphere where the things and conditions are much sin.ilar to the things that are left behind, This waa a work accomplished by the Lord Himself. who withrh• w the spirit, leaving the body to decay Upon the soul's arrival in the new world it would linden angel waiting on the threshold to receive it, when it would be taken amongst those whom he had known in this world. There a revelation of the person's own nature would be effected and we would be seoo as we really were inwardly and not outwardly only, the Judgment being to reveal the truth. The Judgment would not bathe gathering of persons from all ends of the earth before the Lord on His throne, but although they believed He was the judge of all thing-, He desired that we should determine for curselves to reap our eternal destiny. That was not forced upon man, but it was expected of him and life hereafter would be the continuance of our present life, we being the very same except for the fact of our being devoid of eternal covering. On the outward form of the man's face would be revealed his true character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SECRET OF FIFTEEN YEARS.</p>
        <p>Before the Widnes Bench, on Monday morning, Arnie Elizabeth Lytligoe, aged forty, residing at 26, Davies- street, West-bank, was charged on her own confession of the birth of her child on December 26th, 1896. Her husband, John Lythgoe, was also charged with aiding and abetting. The confession was made in a letter sent to Superintendent Strickland on Sunday.—Acting under the instructions of Superintendent Strickland, Inspector Orrnrod and Detective-cergeent Davidsen proceeded to the address sit en in the letter. The inspector handed the female prim ner the letter sent to the superintendent , that day, and asked her if she knew anything about it. She raid, " Yes ; I wrote it. I wish to tell all." He then warned her that anything she said would be used in evidence ngeinst her. Her husband was present at the time. She raid, "I wish to tell ell. It was dead when it was born. It is about fifteen years ago, and I have never been able to sleep since." John, her husband had been going out of his mind. The husband tits° said it was he who teok it away. "I put it in a pit near the New -road, fifteen mars next Boxing Day." l'risonera were remanded until next Monday. Bail was allowed. FARNI LABOURER'S INJURIE3.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn County Court on Tuesday, Arthur Grine, farmer, Halton Lodge, applied for a teview of the com- pensation payable under on registered ageeement with the Court to William Cobb. a farm labourer.—Mr. Ellis, for the applicae, called Dr. Fisher, of Little Sutton, who said that he believed the man now to be suffering from a degenerative disease of the spine, and from certain symptoms he doubted if the disease was in any way ens to the accident of April 22,1910, when the man was struck by a cart-shaft while at work.—Mr. Dolce gave evidence, and lc cross-examination, said he agreed that the man was " never the same again " after the accident. —Mr. Cuthbert Smith called Drs. Maxwell, Carruthers, and Brunton, who expressed the opinion that the man was, in fact, the victim of the accident which had damaged his spine.—ln awarding in favour of the workman the Judge tnggested that the insurance com- pany might refund to Mr. Grice the money he had given the man when the company had ceased payment on the ground that Cobb had worked for some time, whereas they bad only cncseuted to pay during total incapacity. Total incapacity was now proved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>,t9l,</head>
        <p>,t9l,</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Photo Burrell.</p>
        <p>ai 1,-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Wycliffe Congregational Church</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church</p>
        <p>The above church had been very tastefully decorated for the harvest thanksgiving . cervices which were held on Sunday. The preacher both morning and evening was the pastor, the Rev. John Yonge, large congrega- tions being present at both services. In the morning the choir give an egcslient interpretation of the anthem "The eyes of all wait on Thee" (Elgy), while in the evening they were similarly successful in" 0 gladsome light" (Sullivan) and "Sing unto the Lord " (Sydenham). A harvest hymn specially composed for the occasion by Mr. Alfreu Cornea. the headmaster of Wycliffe Day Schools, was sung. We quote one of the verses:—</p>
        <p>- --Bless thou each field of lowly toil, Where neighbour meets with neighbour; Forbid that hate and discord spoil The fruits of love sod I tbour Keep far from us the noise of strife, That all in concord meeting, May bear above the fields of life The Master's holy greeting. The collections were in aid of the maintenance fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Latchford Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>The autumnal anniversary sermons wero preached by the pastor, the Rev. .1. S. Coedhurn on Sunday last. n the morning he took for his subject of discourse " Cast the net on the right side of the ship and ys shall find." In the afternoon at 2.30 the choir rendered a Cantata entitled " The two Seasons " which was much appreciated. At the evening service thepastor again preached, this time from the words "The master is come and calleth for thee." The choir also sang several anthems during the day. The collections were above the average of past sears and considering that the successful Sale of Work had only been bald on the previous Thursday and Friday, they gave both satisfac- tion and cause for much thankfulness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening last, in the absence of the Pastor, the Rev. W. Reid, the services were conducted by the Rev J Glover, of Risley. To-morrow, Sunday, the preacher will be Mr Reid. who on Wednesday evening, as will be seen from advertisement, will give, at the mid-wre's service, an exposition of the Sunday School lesson At S p.m, on Monday, 22rd inst. Mr Reid will give a lantern lecture it St. John's on "Wale,, her scerery, her churches, her history."</p>
        <p>AN ENGLISHMAN'S CASTLE.</p>
        <p>As Simeon' Sessions on Monday, Jane 13utterirorth of Penketh-lane, summoned Elizabeth Handley for assault.</p>
        <p>The Clerk asked the old lady, a well-known local character, what the assault was,</p>
        <p>Complainant: She thrashed me in my own house. I won't be threshed in my own house. The Clerk : How did she assault you ?—ln my own house. (Laughter). But how did she assault you ?—She thrashed me with her lists. She struck me a good many times in my own house. I ordered her daughter out, and then she hit me—in my own house, and I won't stand it in my own house. (Roars of laughter), I'm going now. I am having no more questions. (Laughter). The Chairman : Look here, if you don't behave yourself I will have you put below. We can't have the place turned into a playhouse. Defendant admitted the assault, and was Hoed the costs Is. Gd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>POISONED WHILE SLEEPING.</p>
        <p>At St. Helena, on Monday night, the Deputy County Coroner (Mr. Jones) inquired into the circumstances attending the death of Patrick Whelan, a local labourer, which occurred on Friday night at Messrs. Bishop's glass tube works, St. Helene. The deceased's brother said Whelan was thirty-four years of age. For many years he had been addicted to drink, neglected himself, and followed no regular employment. Occasionally be bad been employed at Messrs. Bishop's worka, but bad done nothing for several weeks. The evidence showed that Whelan went 0 sleep in the" cave "St the works, and next morning was found dead. Whelan had been warned not to go to sleep in the works. Dr. Unsworth, who bed made a post-mortem exami- nation of the body, said death WAI due to poisoning by carbonic acid gas. A verdict to that effect was returned. OLD ST. IniLENS, DRAWINGS PRESENTED TO THE TOWN.</p>
        <p>At the St. Helens Parks Committee on Wednesday, Mr. Alfred Lancaster, curator of the Public Museum, reported a gift of five water-colourand pencil drawings of views of Old St. Helens from Dr. Fred Knowles the mil jects of which ere :—" Old Market-place," " Chnrct:street, front the Black Bull," " Church-street from the Raven Hotel," "The Old Congregational Chapel, viewed from the top of Church-streer, formerly Chapel-lane," and" Raven-street from the Canal Bridge." The drawings are over a century old, having been executed about the year 1805, by John Knowles, grand- father of Dr. Fred Knowler.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>NEWTON'S NEW CHURCR. 'rho Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Cbarame), Archdeacon Spooner, end Canon Willis, spoke at Newton-le-Willows Church schools on Tneedey evening in support of a scheme to build an additioral church at Newton, to be called the Church of All Sainte. Lbrd Newton has given the site, valued at £OOO, neat to the new Technical schcol. The estimated cost of the first part of the building is .C 3,000 and for the completed structure £4.100. Towards the total cost £1,1.10 has been sub- scribed, end an additional sum of ENO hen been promised. The Biahop referred to the splendid abilities of lb. Rev. B. E. Taylor as a leader, and paid • tribute to the zeal of his wife, speaking of their influence in Wootton and their work already acccmplished. Archdeacon Spooner said that the new church was to be the centre of a town-plenning scheme. Unfortu- nately, in Liverpool, they were something like fifty years too late. The environment of Liverpool had barn largely altered owing to the work end earnestness of members of the University and citizens of Liverpool generally ; but, at the same time, a great deal more might have teen done if theme ideas had been conceived years ago. A resolution in support of the schen-, was carried unanimously, and ivot, of thanks to the Bishop and the Archdeacria was moved by Caron Willie.</p>
      </div>
