      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY'S OPERA.</head>
        <p>MUSICAL SOCIETY'S OPERA.</p>
        <p>"SAN TOY'S" 1</p>
        <p>After a brilliant series of successes in the realm cf opera, the Warrington Mus:cal Society's opera section has this year turned its attend, n to the lighter field of musical comedy. Julging frcm the excellent audiences attracted to the I arformances cf " San Toy " at the 8., yid Court Theatre this week, the departure has been welcomed by that large Erection of of the public which has come to regatd this annual amateur enterprise as one of the mom delightlul features of the local theatrical season. Although "San Toy " has no great musical pretensions and falls comiderably short of the conspicuously high standard set in that respect by Sullivan and German in the well-varied mei happily thesen succession of works previcusly staged by the society, it has distinc- tive and irresistible charms. In these strenuous days, that class of entertainment which off-re the merriest and most strikingly contrasted relief frcm the sterner pursuits of every-day life, is iccreasing'y sought for by patrons tithe theatre. And what could be brighter or more iriesponsible in its gaiety than" San Toy? " The music is sparkling, tuneful and vivacious—so melodious and " catchy," in fact, that the leading numbers recur to the listener nith quite phenomenal persistency. The story on wl.ich the comedy is ostensibly built is ill reality an aim, at negligible factor—at any tate it is so slender as nmer t t demand serious attention. In many instances one suspects that the spoken words are introduced for no more important purpose tl an to link together a wonderful medley of light musical items. But of oomicalities there is an almost inexhaustible store The jokes and witticisms are itt ii the subtle variety, innumerable ludicrous situatims and much clever 'business "being the chief elements in mirth production, hut he who Gann( t laugh heartily at "San Toy "is indeed tote pitied. The escapades of that wily Celestial Li should slum be</p>
        <p>sufficient to make the meat morbidly inclined cast care to the winds, for what amazing achievements are not possible to the Chinamin who gets drunk on eau-de-Cologue and, when sentenced to strangulation with his oan pig-tail at cock-crow, escapes that ignominious end by strangling the unhappy rooster which should have announced his doom ? Sentimental and dramatic episodes Lava but small place in the chain of events, hilarity holding undisputed sway except for a few brief moments. The crowning triumph of the production is found in the exquisite blending of colour in its scenic settings, and the beauty and harmony of movement which go to make some of the most gorgeous ensembles and stage pictures ever seen in Warrington. "Son Toy" offers a perfect vista of spectacular splendours. Life in the East is capable of the most picturesque trea'ment at the hands of the scenic artist and the " producer" of plays, and in this case both have risen to the height iltheir opportunity. The street in Pynka Pong and the Emperor's palace at Peking are elaborate settings, conceived in the bent of taste, reality and richness being given to the scenes by the gaily-dressed, animated throngs which people them. As for the costumes, the ladies would doubtless call them "a dream"; more beautiful creations of the kind it would be beyond the imagination of mere man, at any rate, to conceive. It was encouraging to find a much larger audience assembled on Monday than is usually the case on the opening night. The enthusiasm in all parts of the "house" was particularly marked, encores being accorded with great liberality. Apart from a little pardonable slowness in the earlier stages, the performance went with a steadim as which augured exceedingly well for a highly successful week. The members of the company had caught the spirit of the comedy, and had obviously spar ed no effort to ensa re that its interpretation shculd be fully in keeping witll that notable record on which the opera section Justly 1 prides itself. Musical comedy presents exceptional difficulties to the amateur. for so much depends upon freedom and naturalness of action and amusing " by-play " amongst the principals. The opera section's resources never stood it in better stead than On the present occasion. Thanks to the wealth of talent at its disposal, almost every part has been placed in a completely satiefactoty manner, and the performance as a whole is worthy to rook with the meet creditable achievements of the past. In the cast we find several of the names which are pleasantly remembered in connection with former operatic triumphs, but " San Toy " will ales be memorable for some striking debuts. The com- plete list of characters is appended ; Captain Bobbie Preston (Son of Sir Biogo Preston) Mr. J. Babbitt Yen How (a Mandarin) Mr. J. W. Bibby Sir Bingo Preston (British Consul at Pynka Pone Mr. F. Fieldhouse Sing Hi (President of the 'Lard of Ceremonies) Mr. J. Scotson Lieut. Harvey Tucker Mr. J. Canon Bardsley Fo Hop (a Cainere Student) Mr. J. H. Draper Hu Pi 1 M. . . r W Parker phi Ho ( Jewellers or rynza A. Mr. C. H. Hoult Messenger Mr. Joseph Hannan 5 Mr. Wallace Ray ILt Tarter i lii Guards t Mr. R. P. Anderton Old Mandarin (at Ccurt of Peking)Mr..l. F.. Hannan Emperor Mr. W. Moores Li Mr. G. 11. Smith Poppie (Daughter of Sir Bingo) MISS Sara Silcock Dudley (her Maid) Miss Nellie Mullins Ko Fan (Captain of the Emperor's Own) Miss Rhoda D. Bond Yu San (Officer of the Emperor's Own) Mita L. Duckworth Yang Ski ' (Misa Lily Boyle Me Kora Sion I Wives of Miss Eleanor H. Woods Shiley Pia Sing f Yen How Mire Alys Walsh LI Siang Mn Yu Rose Tucker (Trisie) Miss Marjorie Dodds Hon. Mrs. Hay Stackpoole hi ins Alice Taylor Lady Pickleton Miss B. Hutchinson San Toy Mrs. C. Garton Stone Mrs. C. Garton S:one, whose charming portrayal of Bomar in "Tom Jones" was one of the eigna, successes of last year, makes an ideal " San Toy.' Whether she is the more captivating in the enforced giinlehemr the mschame masquerade rase • into ' fartyroarswahecliwnty would be difficult to say. From the first moment of her appearance she Ltscivates the audience by the delightful ruzi rete and natural charm of her acting, and every incident in which •-he subsequently figures only serves to confirm one's original good impression Mrs. Stone's singing and dancing are alike admirable her opening song "It's nice to be a boy sometimes" being given in piquant and thoroughly pleas- ing style. Mr. J. Babbitt, always a favourite,</p>
        <p>, , .4,,... , .. „,:. qp. •• -• .-- . ~, , • i„) i ~ 4'i-, 14 * ' ,• f i ', .:;. ,--4 i ;., 1k .; .; 111 . •,- , ~ - . i ,i fi ' k' •-, '' • . :••- 4 : :., ':' , • ---...*•0, t, , -</p>
        <p>MR. WALTER MOORES as "The Emperor."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>XEAT SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>XEAT SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>adds to his laurels by a powerful and convincing study of the part of Captain Bebbit. Preston. He plays the lover with true ardour and devotion and always makes the most of hie songs, infusing such an intensi'y of feeling and soulfulness into his rendering of " Love has come fr, m Lotus- land "as to make it one of the most acceptable numbers of the evening. In the even more popular " Motherland "Le achieves another triumph, the stirring swing of the melody and the fervour of the interpretation calling forth rapturous applause. The " A.8.C." duet, with its novel and amusing action, is I cleverly given by Bobbie and San Toy, but over- playing I pay ng by the orchestra on Monday made the words indistinct to occupants of the circle. Another ear- haunting love duet—' A. little China maid "—is also beautifully sung. Ever since that fortunate day when Mr. G. H. Smith, as a newly- discovered " star, " first appeared in the local operatic firmament he has undoubtedly proved the life and soul of every production in which he has been cast for the leading ccm.c rola. And this is more than ever true of the preant work—without i the hart detracting friar the achievements of other performers. There seems to be no limit to Mr. Smith's possibilities as a Inunsrouo itoreraotritor. Bowever gi eat the contrast between the parts successively asr,igred to him, he accommodates himself to them as to the manner born—in fact one might be pardoned for concluding that Mr. Smith was never happier than when renresen.ing some comic per- sonality of light opera or musical comedy. Hie jokes appear to come spontaneously and his ingeniously- dev reed " gags " are always topical or to the point Like the heathen Chines of poetic notori.tv, the übiquitous Li has a smile which is child-like a d bland, but it is accompanied by a pronounced weakness for purloining unconsidered trifles and getting into</p>
        <p>awkward scrapes. He goes a little bit too far when , he appropriates a large bottle of eau-de-Cologne and , applies it internally, the immediate effect being an j ursteadiness of gait followed by a sig.% of " pink , elephants. " Mr. Smith is quite equal tithe peculiar demands of the part. Not only is he irresistibly j amusing when indulging in smart repartee in a sort , of Chinese-English, but his surprising eimblene-a and , agility enable him to execute many dances and curious manomvres with ease and finish. Be is equally happy in his songs "The Mandarin "and " Chinee soldier man," the latter, given as part of his unsoldier- like antics whilst in charge of the Ladies f the Guard never failing to score one of the "hits" of the evening One of the most conspicuous personal triumphs is achieved by a debutante —Mies Nellie Mullins—who has an attractive role as Dudley, the lady's mail, and realizes ins possibilities to the full. We could give her no higher praise thaa to say that she really makes the part live—there is neither self consciausiness n r exaggeration, but that vivacious and c y personality so essential to its successful portrayal. Her abilities as a vocalist are charmingly evidenced in "The Lady's Maid," a bright and metal song briskly rendered, the enunciation being especially commendable. It is however, in "Ithoia and her Pagoda" that Miss Mullins excels, the lilting melody and the bewitchingly beautiful dance combining to invest this item with the maximum of attractiveness. Miss Mullins shares the honours with Li in some of the outstandirg humorous episodes of the comedy. For instance, while singing "The Lady's Maid" she is busily engaged wita decking the Celestial's head and person in flowers and fembriee finery, to the great gratlification of that worthy's seman of vanity. The Chinese umbrella dance, in which the couple are associated, is undoubtedly one of the cleverest things in the entire performance and it is no perfecrly done as to merit the insistent demands for a repetition which inevit- ably follow. Equally popular is their novel dance in the guise of quaint Chinese figures. Mr. J. W. Bibby finds a congenial piece of characterisation in Yen How, the cunning iblauditrin, and his work in "San Toy" may be counted a worthy addition to his long list of operatic successes. His entry in • bath chair, attended by a fascinating bevy of wives, at once creates an atmosphere of gay good humour. The merriment is intensified at his repeated efforts to escape the somewhat embarrassing attentions of his retinue of fair charmers and, although comporting himself with a certain degree of austerity and , dignity—as all Mandarins 'tumid —he is alive It 3 the lighter side of the part, dry and sardonic jokes slipping out in apparently unpre- meditated fashion. No song in "San Toy" has more completely captured popular fancy than " Kovr-Tow, Kow-Tow to the great Yen How " and full justice is dose to this sparkling item, Mr. Bibby being well supported in the usual sprightly " business " by the irrepressible sines who, on receiving peremptory dismissal at his august decree take refuge behind a big Chinese umbrella, only to re-appear in a body at the right juncture. Mr. Bibby has another of the musical tit-bits of the comedy in " I mean to introduce it into China," the smart topical verses from the pen of Mr. Frank Berth who has not, of course, overlooked tins perennial theme of Warrington Bridge, imparting a welcome local flavour to a number which always goes with consider- able gusto. Congratulations are due to Miss Sara Siicsok upon a most auspicious and promising debut. The grace and refinement of her presentmeut of Poppy, the typical English maiden, create a marked impression and incline even the most critical to overlook a slight nervousness and st traces noticeable in the early part of the week. The high reputation which Miss Silcock's beautiful and unaffected singing has already gained in local musical circles is considerably enhanced by her artistic interpretation of" A posy from over the sea." She makes the best use of a sweet voice of excellent compass and flexibility, the pensive tenderness of the song being finely realized. Our only regret is that "San Toy" does not furnish greater opportunities for Miss Silesia to distinguish herself but, as it is, she receives one of the warmest escares of the evening Mr. F. Fieldhouse shows the confidence and self- possession of the experienced amateur as Sir Bingo Preston, the British consul, looking and acting the part to the life. He is neither too dignified nor too boisterous, but succeeds in striking a happy mean, hrs -perturbation at the disclosure of his eon's affection Lir San Toy, being admirably simu- lated. Mr. W. Moores is a typicil comic opera Emperor', always fussy and effusive but never very imposing, and contributes not a little to the all-pervading air of merriment. He sounds perhaps a little hit too much cf a bluff Englishman for a Chinese potentate, but he certainly makes the most of</p>
        <p>his opportunities and the completely harpy wal-in which he acquits himself amply Justifies his Fele on for this rather difficult role. Mr. J. Canon Ba y, who has considerably improved since last y as, is fairly natural as Lieutenant Harvey Tucker but night with advantage infuse more life and enthusiacm into his version of the young t fficer and lover. The same criticism applies to Mr. J. H Draper, !Jay' Fo Hop allows the student to dominate tini-brimen being to such AC &amp;lent as to rob the character of a great deal of its intevest and to lose sight of its dramatic possibilities. Some degree of animation is undoubtedly called for and the pleading of lois suit to San T, y should have a semblance of earnestness and reality. Mr. Draper is much more successful as a vozali t and the duet with San Toy, " When you are wed with me," furnishing, as it does, the one attempt at , leirnax in the work, arouses enthusiasm to a high ' pitch. Miss Rhoda D. Bond presents a highly effective study of Ku Fan, captain of the Emperor's Own, directii gibe movements of her unique corps in smart, imperious style and declaring the words of command with energy and distinctness. Nothing could be much funnier than the el isodes arising from her effrnie to instil some t,f the rudiments of military precision into that most awkward of recruits—the guileless Li. As sing Ili, President of the Board of Ceremonies, , Mr. J. Scotson makes the most of a small but interest- , ing part, and is very amusing when keeling the , amorous Empercr in check in the Palace scene. One disappointing feature of " San •Toy " is the dearth of concerted numbers of any intrinsic interest. At the Fame time, several entertaining items for the various contingents of wives and guards are given sPh rdreshing .mispness and verve, the delightful " r , ilk a Pang" cinareite, with its busy and effec'ive 1 action, calling kr ipecial conamend4l,n. The chorus as a body has few opportunities to shine, most of its work being purely stctionaLbut an inspiring enaemble lis crihcoming in the moon scene, when dreamy singing, enchanting scenery, lovely dresses, twinkling lanteina, and a gentle, subdued light, blend with harmonious si.:d picturesque effect. The finale of Act I. also has an impressivenesa foreign to the g *gamer-Ike texture of the greater I part of the coinedy, and it is most vividly</p>
        <p>MR. F. FIELDHOUSE as Sir Bingo Preston. Photos Parkinson.</p>
        <p>worked up. The voices are well balanced and peeectiv drilled, with the result that 'the few ambitious chord numbers in the work received adequaty treatment. Much of the popularity of "Sin Toy " is to be ascribed to its rapid succession of dances, a bewildering array of rhirming measures being executed with remarksble skill. The two groups of wives, in addition to the leading soloists, acquit them- selves uncommonly well in this respect. In Act II Miss Marjorie D dds dances a pan setti with a grace fulness and rhythmic beauty which finely exemplify "the poetty of motion "and earn for her not only loudly-expressed appreciation of the audience but a nightly encore. The orchestral score calls for tasteful] and delicate treatment and he society's instrumentalists have risen to the occasion in dealing with it, and have thereby added lustre to their fine record of services in that capacity. In some of the numbers, however, they do at t subordinate themselves sufficiently to the soloists—a little fault which might easily be remedied with great advantage. For the musical success of "San Toy " grateful acknowledgment must once more be paid to Mr. F. H. Cro:sley. the society's gifted conductor, who always throws himself heart and soul into the operatic eiderprises and inspires the confidence of all by his wise aLd tactful direction of the vocal and instnimental forces. But, as already iud:cated, " San Toy " is, to an even greater extent than the operas which have receded it, a triumph for the "producer's" art. Mr. Watford Lomax gained brilliant dis- tinction by his " c-aching " of our local amateurs in " Tom Jones," but thii year be surparwes that notable achievement. " San Toy "affords the fullest scope for the handling of" crowds," the realization of the light, joyous spirit in stage production, and thanks to Mr. Lomax's magnetic personality and wide experience, and to the responsiveness of the members of the company, wonders have been worked. The Opera Committee was indeed fortunate to enlist the services of such a power "behind the scenes" and to Mr. Lomax this week's performance of "San Toy " should be a source of supreme satisfaction. A. usual the preliminary arrangements have been carried out in the moat complete and eidvient manner by a small sub-committee of the Musical Society, with Mr 11. Bond as hon. secretary and treasurer. It would be impossibly to speak too highly of the co-operation of Mr. A. M. Loader, manager of the loyal Court Theatre, who willingly placed every facility, resource and suggestion at the disposal of the committee to ensure the smooth working of the enter- prise. The courteous help of Mr. H. Warburton, acting manager, must also be acknowledged. Special scenery has been secured by arrangement with Mr, John R. Eluddlestone,general maneger of the Blackpool Winter Gardens and Pavilions Co, Ltd. Messrs. B. J. Simmons and Co., London, are respon- sible for the suge properties and costumes, with the exception of those worn by Miss Sara Silcock, which are the work of Messrs. W. Hodgkinson, Ltd., War- rington. The wigs and make-up are by Messrs Birch. Brown and Son, Warrington, and Messrs. J. H. Fyan and Cu, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>4..110 - _ e _</p>
        <p>MISS MARJORIE DODDS (who dances a pas seal in Act II).</p>
