      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>CYCLIST'S STRANGE ADVENTURE.</head>
        <p>CYCLIST'S STRANGE ADVENTURE.</p>
        <p>Whilst passing through Appleton Dingle early yesterday morning, a workman named Joseph Mills noticed a bicycle and several articles of clothing lying in the road. He eearched the woods and found a man named Thomas Sumner, of 15, Robeon-street. Warrington, wandering about in a dazed condition clad only IEI a sbirt and a clot. Another man assisted Mills to drone Sumner and then Acting-Sergeant White of the Cheshire Constabulary took him in hand. After attending to a wound on the left side of the head and cuts on the face, the Sergeant conveyed the man to his home in a cab. It traaspiers that Sumner bad been in a visit to come ' friends at Whitley. There appears t 3 be no doubt that he took the wrong turning in the dark and dashing down a steep liii come in contact with a atone which threw him from his bicycle and rendered him practically noebnecious. He is recovering from the effects of his strange adventure.</p>
        <p>Warrington Billiard League. A Warrington Billiard League has been formed with Mr. H. Collia as president. The matches for next week are as follows: Junior Conservatives,. Catholic Y.M.S. ; Pariah Church Inst. v. Walkers ; Howley Tannery v. Our Lady's. Latchford ; Senior Conservatives v. Rylands; Liberal Clubv.Whitecr owl</p>
      </div>
