        <p>Warriogton. 13th October, 1911</p>
        <p>passing his engine he felt it lift slightly. He applied the brakes and turning round now that he had knocked somebody down. Upon going back be found a man lying in the 41t. way, whom he could see was badly hurt. Witness ran to the signalman at Walton Old Junction who lent for the ambulant*. The time of the accident was about G. 30 and it was quite a light morn- ing, witness being able to see the Gft. and 4ft. ways quite clearly. From the position in which the deceased wee found it appeared that he had tripped over the line before being run over. Frederick Ryder, foreman platelayer, 23, Wellfleid- street, said he was celled by the last witness to the , scene of the accident. He found the deceased lying ' upon his back in the 4ft. way with his head against the sal!. He was quits deed. P.C. Whalley spoke to removing the body to the Stag inn. The top of the heed RIM almost severed and the beck was badly injured. Two of the toes of the right foot weee cut oft near the first joint. The jury returned a verdict of " Accidentally killed while crossing the line."</p>
        <p>Sir Edward Rastrll hal telegraphed that the announcement of his retirement from the editorship of the " Liverpool Daily Post and Mercury "ii absolutely °otitis, sal without say foundation whatever.</p>
