      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>SHUNTER'S SAD END. Killed on the Railway Near Warrington.</head>
        <p>A SHUNTER'S SAD END. Killed on the Railway Near Warrington.</p>
        <p>An inquest was hsld at the Stag lon, Lower Walton on Thursday morning, by Mr. T. Ridgway, district coroner, ontthe body of William Jones, sged 59, of 11, Nicholson-street, who was employed by the Land N.W. Railway Company an a shunter and who was killed by an engine whilst crossing the line at Walton Old Junction on Wednesday. Richard Henry Jones, son of the deceasel, said that be last saw his father alive at 7 p.m. on Tuesday night. He was then in good health. Sydney Banner, 103. Foster-street, a locomotive driver said at 5.30 as. on Wednesday last deceased and he were ordered to take six waggons to Walton Old Junction. They left Punk Quay at 5.15 a.m and arrived at Walton at 25. Deceased went across to the signal box end that was the last witness saw of him. Frederick Price, 31, Walker-street, Warrington, an engine driver said that he was in charge of a shunting engine on Wednesday, returning from Acton Orange to Flank Quay. Ache was going down the bank a goods train passed and just as the rear of this train was</p>
      </div>
