      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Green—Phillips</p>
        <p>A pretty wedding took place at Holy Trinity Church. on Thursday, when Mr. Stanley Nield Green, loon of Mr. and Mrs. Sam N. Green, of Suez-street, was married to Miss Louie Phillips, daughter of Mrs. Phillips, of Mount-street. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton officiated. Mr. Bert Randall wee at the organ and played festal selections, including Mendelssolm'a Wedding March. The bride was given away by Mr. Whitfield, her uncle. She was charmingly attired in ivory satin, made plain, with long narrow tquare train, the bodice being of beauti- fully worked design in seed pearls. She wore a veil and wreath of orange blossom and carried a bouquet of lilies. The bridesmaid:was Miss Ada Whitfield who wore eamErroevere ddress of witii brig picture hat with white fringe and anchor plumes. Her bouquet was composed of pale pink roses. Mr Leonard Bowcock acted as beat man. The reception was held et the Winmarleigh Cafe, and amid the congratulations of marl friends, the happy couple departed for London, the bride travelling in a dark raspbeny costume with hat of black plumes. Many handsome presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-21_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Comparatively little interest is being taken in the forthcoming municipal elections, although Tuesday next is the last day for the receipt t nominations, In Whitecrom Ward, Councillor Henry Roberts, the retiring representative, and Mr. S. Gough, who haw come forward as a "Non-trader," are prosecuting their campaign, but up to the present their electioneer- ing has been confined to canvassing and the circula- tion of addresiss, no meetings having been held, In Orford and St. John's Wards, fights are promised by Labour and Socialist nominees, Councillor James Halliwell having as his opponent Mr. F. Robson, while Mr. James Ilampson ia trying conclusions with Ccuncillor Lashford.</p>
        <p>Up to last evening the following eanlidates had been nominated : _</p>
        <p>Tows HALL WARD : .°3lr. Thomas Birtles, Brent- wood, Fearn hes d, gentleman. WIIITECROSS WARD : *Mr. Hy. Roberts, Kingsley, Hill Cliffe, soap and chemical manufacturer. ST. JOHN'S WARD : Mr. James Hampson, 89, Old- ham-street, cement burner. 'Mr. Frederick 'rhomae Lasbford,s9,Chorley-street,. butcher. . _ .</p>
        <p>MALEY WARD : *Mr. Thomas Chantler. Mersey-street, beer-house keeper. LAT,TIFORD Wsmi : *Mr John Pembertcn, 14, Fairfield-road, Stockton Heath, builder. Sr. A1:910., WARD: •Dr. George William Joseph, 21, Bold-street, doctor of medicine. BED,ET WARD: *Mr. James Dickenson. 97 , Beßfley. road, furniture dealer. OR, as WARD: 'Mr. Janice Ihiliwell, Mardale, Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath, hoot factor.</p>
        <p>.—.• Mr. George Frederick Robson, 418, Krotsford-road,</p>
        <p>. , shop assistant. *lndicates present representative. Councillor Craik will .gan sten! for Fairfield Ward, and we understand that no opposition will be [ offered to hie return.</p>
        <p>QUESTIONS To CANDIDATES. At a meeting of the Tradesmen'. Association, held on Toeeday, it was resolved to submit the following questions to the candidates it the forthcoming municipal elections I. Will you support my motion for the reduction of the price of gee? 2. Are you in favour of. reduction of water charges made to occupiers of lock-op shop premises ? 3. Will you support any motlim with • view to the granting of return 'tramway tickets up to 8.30 a.m. to accommodate shop assistants ?</p>
      </div>
